{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_6d2bb3c4bcda462da8301d52","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 110 lines\nNote: showing lines 590-699. More of the file remains. Use offset=700 to continue.\n590:                              :path path})))]\n591:     (if (and (vector? v) (every? int? v))\n592:       v\n593:       (throw (ex-info \"Unsupported slot value in AST datoms\"\n594:                       {:rule :unsupported-value,\n595:                        :slot attr,\n596:                        :kind :refs,\n597:                        :value v,\n598:                        :entity eid,\n599:                        :path path})))))\n600: \n601: \n602: ;; =============================================================================\n603: ;; D1: the scope resolver (§3)\n604: ;; =============================================================================\n605: \n606: (defn resolve-name\n607:   \"§3 scope resolution of `name` against `stack`, the ordered frame\n608:    vector stack: frame depth 0 is the innermost frame, each frame is the\n609:    source-ordered parameter vector, position is the source parameter\n610:    index with the leftmost parameter at 0. Frames search inner-to-outer;\n611:    positions search right-to-left within a frame, so duplicate\n612:    parameters are rightmost-wins exactly as `yin.vm.engine/bind-params`\n613:    binds them — (fn [x x] x) resolves to the second x, [0 1]. Returns\n614:    {:bound [frame-depth position]} or {:free name} with the symbol\n615:    preserved exactly.\"\n616:   [stack name]\n617:   (let [position-in (fn [frame]\n618:                       (first (filter (fn [p] (= name (nth frame p)))\n619:                                      (range (dec (count frame)) -1 -1))))\n620:         binding (first (keep (fn [depth]\n621:                                (when-let [p (position-in (nth stack depth))]\n622:                                  [depth p]))\n623:                              (range (count stack))))]\n624:     (if binding\n625:       {:bound binding}\n626:       {:free name})))\n627: \n628: \n629: ;; =============================================================================\n630: ;; D1: the projection walk (§2 grammar, §3 scopes, no hashing)\n631: ;; =============================================================================\n632: \n633: (declare project-node mint-record)\n634: \n635: \n636: (defn- build-node\n637:   \"Build one walked node's resolved semantic node — the §4 projected\n638:    slots, children as resolved nodes — recursing over children in the\n639:    grammar's fixed order under the scope at each occurrence. Returns\n640:    [node child-hashes acc'], child-hashes being {slot h} with an ordered\n641:    vector of hashes under :yin.debruijn/operands; `active` is the set of\n642:    eids on the current path.\"\n643:   [tag index facts scalar eid stack acc active path]\n644:   (let [;; each step threads [node child-hashes acc]\n645:         child (fn [[node refs acc] slot attr stack]\n646:                 (let [[n h acc'] (project-node index\n647:                                                (child-ref facts attr eid path)\n648:                                                stack\n649:                                                acc\n650:                                                active\n651:                                                (conj path attr))]\n652:                   [(assoc node slot n) (assoc refs slot h) acc']))\n653:         children (fn [[node refs acc] slot attr]\n654:                    (let [[ns hs acc']\n655:                          (reduce (fn [[ns hs a] ref]\n656:                                    (let [[n h a'] (project-node index\n657:                                                                 ref\n658:                                                                 stack\n659:                                                                 a\n660:                                                                 active\n661:                                                                 (conj path attr))]\n662:                                      [(conj ns n) (conj hs h) a']))\n663:                                  [[] [] acc]\n664:                                  (child-refs facts attr eid path))]\n665:                      [(assoc node slot ns) (assoc refs slot hs) acc']))\n666:         leaf (fn [node] [node {} acc])]\n667:     (case tag\n668:       :literal (leaf {:yin.debruijn/type :literal,\n669:                       :yin.debruijn/value (get scalar :yin/value)})\n670:       :variable (let [name (get scalar :yin/name)\n671:                       resolution (resolve-name stack name)]\n672:                   (leaf (if-let [bound (:bound resolution)]\n673:                           {:yin.debruijn/type :variable,\n674:                            :yin.debruijn/bound bound}\n675:                           {:yin.debruijn/type :variable,\n676:                            :yin.debruijn/free (:free resolution)})))\n677:       :lambda (let [params (get scalar :yin/params)]\n678:                 (child (leaf (merge {:yin.debruijn/type :lambda,\n679:                                      :yin.debruijn/arity (count params)}\n680:                                     ;; truthy only: an explicit false and an\n681:                                     ;; absent :yin/macro? project identically\n682:                                     (when (get scalar :yin/macro?)\n683:                                       {:yin.debruijn/macro? (get scalar :yin/macro?)})))\n684:                        :yin.debruijn/body\n685:                        :yin/body\n686:                        (into [params] stack)))\n687:       :application (let [operator-eid (child-ref facts :yin/operator eid path)]\n688:                      (check-unexpanded index eid operator-eid path)\n689:                      (-> (leaf {:yin.debruijn/type :application})\n690:                          (child :yin.debruijn/operator :yin/operator stack)\n691:                          (children :yin.debruijn/operands :yin/operands)))\n692:       :dao.stream.apply/call (children (leaf {:yin.debruijn/type :dao.stream.apply/call,\n693:                                               :yin.debruijn/op (get scalar :yin/op)})\n694:                                        :yin.debruijn/operands\n695:                                        :yin/operands)\n696:       :if (-> (leaf {:yin.debruijn/type :if})\n697:               (child :yin.debruijn/test :yin/test stack)\n698:               (child :yin.debruijn/consequent :yin/consequent stack)\n699:               (child :yin.debruijn/alternate :yin/alternate stack))"}],"deferred":false}}
{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_5ad9fc758d5a460db8904b03","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 234 lines\n180:                                      (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %)\n181:                                            structural-attrs)))\n182:                               order)\n183:         batch {:attrs attrs,\n184:                :segments segments,\n185:                :seg (first segments),\n186:                :instructions instructions}]\n187:     (some #(% batch) rules)))\n188: \n189: \n190: ;; =============================================================================\n191: ;; The canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2, §7.5)\n192: ;; =============================================================================\n193: \n194: (def vector-operand-table\n195:   \"§2.4's operand table in UCF §7.3.2's positional form: mnemonic → ordered\n196:    `[attribute kind]` operands. Position `i` of a canonical tuple (from 1)\n197:    holds the operand entry `i-1` names; this one table drives the §7.5\n198:    validator's rules. Each kind names the\n199:    rule that judges it: `:data`, `:sym`, `:syms`, `:kw`, `:str`, `:bool`,\n200:    and `:buffer` belong to `:operand-kind` (a nil passes it and is\n201:    `:saturation`'s defect); `:pc` to `:target-bounds`; `:uint` to `:argc`.\"\n202:   {:const [[:yin.code/value :data]],\n203:    :var [[:yin.code/name :sym]],\n204:    :closure [[:yin.code/params :syms] [:yin.code/body :pc]],\n205:    :push [],\n206:    :call [[:yin.code/argc :uint] [:yin.code/tail? :bool]],\n207:    :return [],\n208:    :jump [[:yin.code/target :pc]],\n209:    :branch-false [[:yin.code/target :pc]],\n210:    :halt [],\n211:    :gensym [[:yin.code/prefix :str]],\n212:    :store-get [[:yin.code/key :data]],\n213:    :store-put [[:yin.code/key :data] [:yin.code/value :data]],\n214:    :stream-make [[:yin.code/buffer :buffer]],\n215:    :stream-put [],\n216:    :stream-cursor [],\n217:    :stream-next [],\n218:    :stream-close [],\n219:    :park [],\n220:    :resume [[:yin.code/parked-id :kw]],\n221:    :current-continuation [],\n222:    :ffi-call [[:yin.code/ffi-op :kw] [:yin.code/argc :uint]]})\n223: \n224: \n225: (def ^:private operand-kind-checks\n226:   \"§7.5 :operand-kind — kind → predicate. Absent kinds (`:pc`, `:uint`) are\n227:    not judged by the rule: `:target-bounds` and `:argc` own them. A nil in\n228:    a saturated slot passes here; `:saturation` owns it, the §7.4 `:slot-kind`\n229:    precedent.\"\n230:   {:data vm/plain-data?,\n231:    :sym symbol?,\n232:    :syms (fn [x] (and (vector? x) (every? symbol? x))),\n233:    :kw keyword?,\n234:    :str (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (string? x))),\n235:    :bool (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (boolean? x))),\n236:    :buffer (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (count? x)))})\n237: \n238: \n239: (def ^:private saturated-operands\n240:   \"§7.5 :saturation — the `[mnemonic index]` operands a canonical tuple\n241:    materializes (UCF §7.3.2); a nil in one of them is that rule's defect.\"\n242:   #{[:gensym 1] [:stream-make 1] [:call 2] [:ffi-call 2]})\n243: \n244: \n245: (defn- tuple-defect\n246:   [rule pc]\n247:   {:rule rule, :pc pc})\n248: \n249: \n250: (defn- mnemonic-of\n251:   \"The mnemonic of one tuple, or nil when the element has no first element\n252:    that could be one. A tuple is a vector — UCF §7.3.2's positional form —\n253:    so a list element has no mnemonic.\"\n254:   [t]\n255:   (when (and (vector? t) (seq t))\n256:     (nth t 0)))\n257: \n258: \n259: (defn- tuple-kinds\n260:   \"The operand kinds of one tuple's mnemonic, in tuple order.\"\n261:   [t]\n262:   (mapv second (get vector-operand-table (nth t 0))))\n263: \n264: \n265: ;; Each rule below takes the whole vector and returns a defect or nil. Rules\n266: ;; run in §7.5's order and the first defect wins, so a rule may assume every\n267: ;; earlier one held — exactly as `rules` above runs over a batch.\n268: \n269: (defn- nonempty\n270:   \"Rule 1. An empty vector names pc 0, where the first instruction belongs;\n271:    so does anything a pc cannot index: the canonical form is a vector\n272:    (UCF §7.3.2), so a list outer is not the form at any length.\"\n273:   [v]\n274:   (when-not (and (vector? v) (pos? (count v)))\n275:     (tuple-defect :nonempty 0)))\n276: \n277: \n278: (defn- mnemonic\n279:   \"Rule 2.\"\n280:   [v]\n281:   (some (fn [pc]\n282:           (when-not (contains? mnemonics (mnemonic-of (nth v pc)))\n283:             (tuple-defect :mnemonic pc)))\n284:         (range (count v))))\n285: \n286: \n287: (defn- arity\n288:   \"Rule 3. §2.4's operand table as saturated and made positional by UCF\n289:    §7.3.2: the mnemonic plus exactly its operands.\"\n290:   [v]\n291:   (some (fn [pc]\n292:           (let [t (nth v pc)]\n293:             (when-not (= (inc (count (get vector-operand-table (nth t 0))))\n294:                          (count t))\n295:               (tuple-defect :arity pc))))\n296:         (range (count v))))\n297: \n298: \n299: (defn- operand-kind\n300:   \"Rule 4. An operand is judged only by the kind §7.5 gives it.\"\n301:   [v]\n302:   (some (fn [pc]\n303:           (let [t (nth v pc)\n304:                 kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n305:             (some (fn [i]\n306:                     (let [check (get operand-kind-checks (nth kinds (dec i)))]\n307:                       (when (and check (not (check (nth t i))))\n308:                         (tuple-defect :operand-kind pc))))\n309:                   (range 1 (count t)))))\n310:         (range (count v))))\n311: \n312: \n313: (defn- saturation\n314:   \"Rule 5. A saturated operand is materialized in a canonical tuple; a nil\n315:    one names its pc.\"\n316:   [v]\n317:   (some (fn [pc]\n318:           (let [t (nth v pc)]\n319:             (when (some #(and (contains? saturated-operands [(nth t 0) %])\n320:                               (nil? (nth t %)))\n321:                         (range 1 (count t)))\n322:               (tuple-defect :saturation pc))))\n323:         (range (count v))))\n324: \n325: \n326: (defn- target-bounds\n327:   \"Rule 6. A resolved pc must be an integer inside the segment.\"\n328:   [v]\n329:   (let [length (count v)]\n330:     (some (fn [pc]\n331:             (let [t (nth v pc)\n332:                   kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n333:               (some (fn [i]\n334:                       (when (= :pc (nth kinds (dec i)))\n335:                         (let [x (nth t i)]\n336:                           (when-not (and (integer? x) (<= 0 x (dec length)))\n337:                             (tuple-defect :target-bounds pc)))))\n338:                     (range 1 (count t)))))\n339:           (range (count v)))))\n340: \n341: \n342: (defn- terminator\n343:   \"Rule 7. Nothing labelled follows pc length-1, so the one instruction\n344:    that can violate the rule is the last.\"\n345:   [v]\n346:   (let [pc (dec (count v))]\n347:     (when-not (contains? terminators (mnemonic-of (nth v pc)))\n348:       (tuple-defect :terminator pc))))\n349: \n350: \n351: (defn- argc\n352:   \"Rule 8. The :uint operands of `:call` and `:ffi-call`.\"\n353:   [v]\n354:   (some (fn [pc]\n355:           (let [t (nth v pc)\n356:                 kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n357:             (some (fn [i]\n358:                     (when (= :uint (nth kinds (dec i)))\n359:                       (when-not (count? (nth t i))\n360:                         (tuple-defect :argc pc))))\n361:                   (range 1 (count t)))))\n362:         (range (count v))))\n363: \n364: \n365: (def ^:private vector-rules\n366:   [nonempty mnemonic arity operand-kind saturation target-bounds terminator\n367:    argc])\n368: \n369: \n370: (defn well-formed-vector?\n371:   \"Check one canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2) against §7.5\n372:    (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`).\n373: \n374:    Returns nil when the vector is well formed, else the first defect as\n375:    `{:rule r :pc p}` — `:nonempty`, `:mnemonic`, `:arity`,\n376:    `:operand-kind`, `:saturation`, `:target-bounds`, `:terminator`, or\n377:    `:argc` — with p the offending pc (`:nonempty` names pc 0, where the\n378:    missing first instruction belongs). Rules run in §7.5's order, each\n379:    assuming the earlier ones held. §2.6 rules 1–4 are satisfied by the\n380:    vector form by construction (pc is the index); rules 5–6 and the UCF\n381:    §7.3.4 checks are the eight here. The address check — a vector hashing\n382:    to the address it claims — is not a structural rule and is not made\n383:    here: a correct hash is never structural validation.\"\n384:   [v]\n385:   (some #(% v) vector-rules))\n386: \n387: \n388: ;; =============================================================================\n389: ;; Segment rows (§6.2)\n390: ;; =============================================================================\n391: \n392: (defn project-segment\n393:   \"§6.2 (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`): the canonical instruction\n394:    vector with pc prepended — one `[pc tag & ops]` row per instruction, the\n395:    tag being the mnemonic. The relation is per segment: pcs collide across\n396:    segments, and source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a tuple\n397:    slot, so bare-pc rows from different segments are never unioned into one\n398:    relation. A query over several segments takes them as separate sources\n399:    or uses the segment-qualified form (`project-segment-qualified`).\"\n400:   [v]\n401:   (mapv (fn [pc t] (into [pc] t)) (range) v))\n402: \n403: \n404: (defn project-segment-qualified\n405:   \"§6.2's segment-qualified form `[segment-address pc tag & ops]`: one\n406:    address column prepended, so every tag's arity gains one uniformly and\n407:    per-tag arity stays fixed. The address is the vector's own\n408:    `(jing/segment-key v)` — the §7.1/UCF §7.3.2 rule the loaders already\n409:    follow — computed here rather than claimed by a caller, so a segment's\n410:    rows are named by the content they are projected from.\"\n411:   [v]\n412:   (let [addr (jing/segment-key v)]\n413:     (mapv (fn [r] (into [addr] r)) (project-segment v))))"}],"deferred":false}}
