{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"Key facts from linearize:\n\n1. Bodies are emitted out-of-line after the main sequence (loop after `:halt`). Each body is `mark! l` (label at body start), `lower-node body`, then `(emit! lambda :return)`. So each body ends with an explicit `:return`. Good — the first `:return` at-or-after body in `closure-body-ranges` is the body's own terminating `:return`, but only if there's no earlier `:return` within the body's pc range. Can `:return` appear inside a body before its end? `lower-node` never emits `:return` — only the body-emitting loop does. So within a body, the first `:return` at-or-after body is exactly its terminator… wait, bodies are emitted sequentially: body1 [.. :return] body2 [.. :return]. The first `:return` at-or-after body1's start is body1's own terminator. Because lower-node never emits :return. Correct.\n\nBut wait — what if one closure's body contains a nested lambda? The nested lambda's `:closure` instruction is emitted inline within the outer body; the nested body is appended later at the tail of the queue. So bodies never interleave: body i's range is [start_i, return_i], and body i+1 begins after return_i. Non-overlapping.\n\n2. Main sequence: root code + `:halt`. Bodies come after. So no body covers pc 0. Good.\n\n3. Now — `named-canonical-vector` filter: `instructions` excludes `seg`, includes entities that have one of #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op}. lower's output also includes the AST datoms themselves (input `:yin/*` datoms are part of the batch — wait, `lower` takes named-datoms and emits code datoms; the output is `(into [[seg ...] ...] (mapcat ...))` — does the output include the input AST datoms? Looking at `lower`: the return value is just segment entity + instruction entities. Doesn't re-emit input AST datoms. But B2's `adapt` calls `(linearize/lower named-datoms opts)` and passes only that output into `named-canonical-vector`. So batch = segment entity (attrs: :yin.code/type, :yin.code/length, :yin.code/derived-from) + instruction entities (:yin.code/segment, :yin.code/pc, :yin.code/op, operands, :yin.code/source).\n\nFilter: segment entity is excluded both by `(not= e seg)` and by not having structural attrs — wait, does it? Segment has :yin.code/type, :yin.code/length, :yin.code/derived-from. None in the structural set #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op}. So double-excluded. Instruction entities have all three structural attrs. OK.\n\nHmm — but what about AST entity references: `:yin.code/source` value is the AST entity id (from input datoms). That's an attribute of instruction entities, not its own entity in this batch. Fine — `:source` vector carries those ids.\n\nAlso `:yin.code/derived-from root-id` on segment. Fine.\n\nNow `index-batch`: datoms are `[e a v t m]` — 5 elements. Destructuring with `[e a v]` — takes first 3. Fine.\n\n`seg` detection: `(first (filter #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) order))`. OK.\n\n4. `named-canonical-vector` resolves `:pc`-kind operands through `pc-of` — for `:closure`, operands from vector-operand-table are [[:yin.code/params :syms] [:yin.code/body :pc]]. So tuple = [:closure params body-position]. body's label value in datoms is `(pc->eid (get labels v))` — an entity id; `pc-of` maps entity → position. Position equals original pc since ordered = by-pc over range n. Good.\n\n5. Now `adapt`: calls `code/well-formed-vector?` on named vector, then `rewrite`. rewrite checks `layout-conforms?`.\n\nChecking `layout-conforms?`:\n- ranges: for each :closure c in [c body r].\n- spans = distinct [b r].\n- every span has r non-nil and b > 0.\n- `(= (count spans) (count (distinct (map second spans))))` — r values are distinct. Hmm, this checks each body has a distinct end :return. Two closures sharing the same body would collapse in spans-distinct but then have equal r… if two :closure instructions point at the same body pc, spans distinct collapses to one, and r-distinct count is 1 = spans count 1. So shared bodies pass. Does lower ever emit two closures sharing a body? Each lambda gets a fresh label. But occurrence expansion — \"The image inherits occurrence expansion\" — each occurrence is expanded separately so no sharing. Fine either way.\n- Non-overlap: for every pair x<y (compare on [b r] vectors), `(< r1 b2)` or `(< r2 b1)`.\n\nWait — there's a subtle issue with non-overlap check when a body ends with r and the next body starts at b2 = r+1. Then (< r1 b2) is true. Fine.\n\nCan spans ever nest? Body A [b1 r1] contains a nested closure whose body B [b2 r2] satisfies b1 < b2 <= r2 <= r1? In lower's layout, nested bodies are appended after all outer bodies… actually the bodies queue processes FIFO: a body's nested lambdas get appended to the tail of the queue, so nested bodies come after the outer body's :return. So non-overlap holds.\n\nBut wait — could the outer body's [body, first-:return-at-or-after-body] range extend past the actual :return if there's no :return inside the body? No: each body ends with its own :return (emit! lambda :return). The first :return at-or-after body start is inside the body… unless the body itself contains an earlier :return — impossible since lower-node never emits :return. Correct.\n\nBut — what about `:halt`? Main sequence ends with :halt at some pc, then bodies begin. body-owner maps only body ranges. Closures in main sequence have no owner. Good.\n\n6. Now the important correctness question for `rewrite` on `:var`: `chain-of owner pc` — walks owner from the var's pc. owner maps each pc in [body, r] to the innermost closure c whose range covers it. But what about the `:return` instruction itself at pc r — it's included in range [body, (inc r)) — `(range body (inc r))`. Fine.\n\nBut subtlety: consider a `:closure` instruction located at pc c inside an outer body. owner(c) = outer closure. chain-of for a var at pc p inside an inner body: owner(p) = inner closure c_in; then owner(c_in) — c_in is the pc of the `:closure` instruction inside the outer body → outer closure c_out; then owner(c_out)… etc. Matches design: \"its parent is the body containing the `:closure`\".\n\nEdge case: closure instruction in main sequence: owner(c) absent → chain terminates. Good.\n\n7. `:var` at a pc not covered by any body (main sequence): stack = [] → resolve-name returns {:free name} → [:load-free name]. Correct — a var in main sequence can only be free (no enclosing binder). Matches named semantics (resolved against E).\n\n8. Now `image-scope-defect`:\n- Guard `(when (vector? v) ...)`.\n- For each pc, if tuple is :load-bound with count >= 3: depth/position check:\n  - count? both (integer, non-negative)\n  - chain = chain-of owner pc; if depth >= (count chain) → defect\n  - c = (nth chain depth); ct = (nth v c nil); if ct nil or count < 2 or arity (nth ct 1) not count? or position >= arity → defect.\n\nNote: for :load-bound tuples with count < 3, `image-scope-defect` silently skips (delegated to well-formed-image?'s arity rule). The docstring states \"assumes v is otherwise shape-valid\". OK.\n\nBut — chain-of depends on body-owner which depends on layout-conforms? being true. image-scope-defect doesn't check layout-conforms? first. If handed a non-conforming layout (overlapping bodies), body-owner is ill-defined (into {} keeps last). Docstring doesn't mention this. Hand-built test fixtures: `[[:closure 1 3] [:push] [:return] [:load-bound 5 0] [:return]]` — closure body at 3; ranges: [3, first :return at-or-after 3] = [3,4]. spans: b=3>0, r=4. Non-overlap trivial. Conforms. pc 3 body-owner → {3 c0, 4 c0}. chain-of(owner 3) = [0] (owner(3)=0; owner(0)? pc 0 is not in [3,4] → chain [0]). depth 5 >= 1 → defect {:rule :scope :pc 3}. Matches test expectation.\n\nSecond fixture: `[[:closure 1 2] [:return] [:load-bound 0 3] [:return]]` — closure body 2, range [2,3]. load-bound at pc 2: chain [0], depth 0 < 1 OK; ct = [:closure 1 2], arity 1; position 3 >= 1 → defect pc 2. Matches.\n\nHmm wait — in first fixture, `layout-conforms?`: body 3, r=4. Also pc 1 is `:push`, pc 2 is `:return`. Only closure at pc 0. spans [[3 4]]. pos? 3 OK. Fine.\n\nBut subtlety: the `:return` at pc 2 — irrelevant.\n\nNow — does `image-scope-defect` agree with B1's `scope-defect` on all inputs? Not required to be identical, but tests assert both reject hand-built fixtures with same {:rule :scope :pc p}. B1's scope-defect walks reachability. For fixture 1: B1: all-body-chains: walk from 0: pc0 :closure → chain '() owner{0 '()}, nested [[3 '(1)]]. Successors of pc0 = [1]. pc1 :push owner{1 '()}. pc2 :return terminator. Then queue [3 '(1)]: walk-body from 3: pc3 :load-bound owner{3 '(1)}, pc4 :return. Then scope-defect: :load-bound at pc3: chain '(1), depth 5 >= 1 → {:rule :scope :pc 3}. Matches.\n\nFixture 2: `[[:closure 1 2] [:return] [:load-bound 0 3] [:return]]`: B1 walk from 0: closure body 2 chain (1); pc1 :return terminator; body 2: pc2 :load-bound, pc3 :return. Load-bound at pc 2: depth 0 < 1, position 3 >= arity 1 → scope defect pc 2. Matches.\n\n9. Now deeper question about `image-scope-defect`: it uses chain-of/body-owner over the whole vector, but for a `:load-bound` in a body whose closure chain differs from the reachability-based chain (B1) — e.g., :jump across bodies. B1 handles conflicts; B2's is \"B2's own copy\" — the design says \"B2 validates non-negative depth and position against the frame arity vector\". Both validators are required to accept, so B2's simpler approach is acceptable. Note though: B2's image-scope-defect is NOT called from `adapt`! Look: adapt does well-formed-vector? check, then rewrite, then side-table merge. Never calls image-scope-defect on its own output, and never calls dc/image-defect either. Tests do: `every-corpus-program-adapts-to-a-valid-image` asserts both validators on the output. The design says \"scope validation is required at both entry points where an image can be accepted: B2 validates…\" and \"every image B2 emits must be accepted by B1's validator\". Should `adapt` itself run image-scope-defect? Hmm — rewrite uses resolve-name which constructs depth/position from the actual chain, so the output is scope-valid by construction. The validation requirement is about entry points where an image is accepted (i.e., where an external image is validated). B2 emitting is by construction. Design doc: \"B2 validates non-negative depth and position against the frame arity vector\" — this is the image-scope-defect function which B2 provides. Whether adapt calls it internally is arguable; the function exists and is tested. Acceptable, probably a P3 note.\n\nActually wait — does rewrite's output always satisfy scope-validity? `:load-bound`'s depth/position comes from resolve-name over the parameter vector chain. Position < (count params). But the de Bruijn image's `:closure` carries arity = (count params). chain-of at the var's pc gives the closure; its arity = count of its params. So position < arity and depth < chain length. Yes, valid by construction.\n\n10. `lift`:\n- `lift-once` for :load-bound: chain = chain-of owner pc; c = (nth chain depth). If depth is out of range (invalid image), nth throws IndexOutOfBounds — lift assumes valid image. Docstring doesn't state this. Minor.\n- `lift-params`: uses recorded params if given and count matches arity; else synthesizes. Synthesized names use `used` = free names ∪ all params assigned so far. `synthesize-name` loops until candidate not in used. Names `g__c_i`. Note: `(into free (mapcat identity) (vals acc))` — accumulates params assigned so far. reduce over closure-pcs (keep-indexed order = pc order), so deterministic. Good.\n\nBut subtle bug candidate: `lift-params` checks `(= (count given) arity)` but not that `given` is a vector of symbols. A foreign side table with right count but garbage names (e.g., numbers) would be used and then `lift` round-trip check catches if rewrite produces a different image — `resolve-name` compares `(= name (nth frame p))` — a numeric \"name\" would resolve identically (var refers to same position), so rewrite would produce same image and lift would return garbage \"names\" in named vector. But that's a foreign table supplying weird data; round-trip check is the design's own acceptance rule (\"the side table supplied is accepted only if its adapted lift returns the original image\"). By-design behavior. OK.\n\nAnother subtlety: round-trip check in `lift` uses `rewrite` which requires layout-conforms? and throws otherwise. If v's layout doesn't conform, lift throws a layout error rather than a lift error. Acceptable.\n\nNow exact-match law: `lift(adapt(lower x), side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`. lift-once reconstructs :var from params recorded in side-table. But wait — for rightmost-wins duplicate parameters: (fn [x x] x) resolves to [0 1]. lift-once reads (nth params 1) = 'x — original spelling. Exact match. Good.\n\nBut what about `:var` slots in the side table? rewrite records `{:kind :var}` for var slots (no name) — and adapt merges `:source`. lift doesn't need them. And named vector reconstruction: lift-once copies every other tuple unchanged. But named vector for :var is [:var name] — one operand. lift-once emits [:var name]. Exact-match law requires equality against named-canonical-vector's output where :var tuple is [:var name]. Yes.\n\nBut — structural detail: :var's side-table entry has :kind :var, and lift ignores. Fine.\n\n11. Now potential issue: `rewrite`'s output is built with `(conj debruijn ...)` starting from `[]` — conj on vector appends; final :debruijn is a vector. lift compares `(= v (:debruijn (rewrite attempt)))` — if v is a vector, vector equality with a vector is fine. adapt returns :image debruijn (vector). Tests use (= image (:debruijn (dl/rewrite lifted))). rewrite returns a vector; lifted is a vector from mapv. OK.\n\n12. `named-canonical-vector` — another check: `instructions` filter uses `(some #(contains? (get attrs e) %) structural)`. What about entities in the batch that have :yin.code/segment but aren't instructions? None. What about AST input entities? Not in lower's output. OK. But wait — in the test's `adapted` and `named-vector`, `dl/adapt (ast-datoms ast)` — ast-datoms = (second (vm/ast->datoms-with-root ast)). lower emits only code datoms. Good.\n\n13. Now `:closure` inside `named-canonical-vector`: vector-operand-table gives :closure [params :syms] [body :pc]. Datoms carry :yin.code/params (vector of syms) and :yin.code/body (resolved entity id → pc-of → position). Good.\n\nWait — potential defect in `tuple` fn: `(get code/vector-operand-table mnem)` — for mnemonic `:push` the entry is [] → tuple is [:push]. For `:resume` it's [[:yin.code/parked-id :kw]] → [:resume parked-id]. OK.\n\n14. `source` vector: `(mapv #(get-in attrs [% :yin.code/source]) ordered)`. adapt merges :source into each side-table entry — but only entries that exist (var and closure slots). `(reduce-kv ... side-table)` on existing entries. Design says side-table indexed by pc with closure entries carrying params and \"every entry carrying :source\". Yes — only :var/:closure entries exist. Matches B2 docstring (\"side table records binder names indexed by instruction slots… :var slots don't carry their own name\").\n\nHmm, design §2 says \"binder names and provenance are carried in a diagnostic side table indexed by instruction slots\". Fine.\n\n15. Now B2 test corpus: claims \"every named mnemonic yin.vm.code/mnemonics declares is reached at least once: :const :var :closure :push :call :return :jump :branch-false :halt :gensym :store-get :store-put :stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close :park :resume :current-continuation :ffi-call\". Let me verify coverage against corpus:\n- :const — literals ✓\n- :var — worked-example ✓\n- :closure ✓\n- :push — application ✓\n- :call ✓\n- :return — body ✓\n- :jump — if-in-tail ✓\n- :branch-false ✓\n- :halt ✓\n- :gensym — :default-gensym {:type :vm/gensym} ✓ and store-and-control with :prefix ✓\n- :store-get/:store-put — store-and-control ✓\n- :stream-make — :streams {:type :stream/make :buffer 4} and :default-buffer ✓\n- :stream-put — :streams ✓\n- :stream-cursor ✓, :stream-next ✓, :stream-close ✓ (nested in :streams)\n- :park — :park-resume ✓ ({:type :vm/park})\n- :resume ✓\n- :current-continuation — store-and-control ✓\n- :ffi-call — :ffi and :ffi-no-args ✓\n\nBut wait — is the test actually asserting mnemonic coverage? `every-corpus-program-adapts-to-a-valid-image` only asserts image-defect nil and side-table map. Nowhere does it assert that the corpus actually reaches every mnemonic! The docstring claims coverage but there's no test that computes the set of mnemonics reached across corpus and compares to code/mnemonics. Hmm — is that a defect? Design's B2 completion criterion: \"all nodes and opcodes\". A test that just claims coverage in docstring without asserting it is weaker — if a corpus program silently failed to emit a mnemonic (e.g., :park — does lowering of {:type :vm/park} require anything?), nothing catches it. This is a legitimate P2: mnemonic coverage is asserted only in prose, not in code. Actually wait — more importantly, would `adapt` succeed on all corpus programs? For instance, :streams AST `{:type :stream/put ...}` — vm/ast->datoms-with-root must support those node shapes. Test presumably passes (orchestrator has run it). Also `:park-resume`: if node with test being false literal, consequent park, alternate resume. OK.\n\nBut further, one actual check: does ast->datoms-with-root accept `{:type :stream/make}` without :buffer (default-buffer)? Presumably yes, since tests pass.\n\nHmm, but I can't run tests. My job: find defects by reading.\n\nCoverage-assertion gap: P3/P2.\n\n16. `corpus-images-load-and-run-on-b3` runs only 5 programs (:literal :duplicate-param :worked-example :zero-arity-call :if-in-tail). The rest can't run on B3 (stream/park/ffi/gensym/current-continuation are B4, so :not-yet-implemented). Reasonable. But `:store-and-control` includes :current-continuation — can't run. Fine.\n\nWait — :duplicate-param runs on B3: image = closure body with [:closure 2 b] ... [:load-bound 0 1] [:return]. Runs fine.\n\n17. Now the `:push` fix itself. Concerns:\n\na) No-op :push is correct for all current emit sites (verified: :push is always emitted immediately after a node lowering that leaves a value; in stack VM every producer conj'd).\n\nb) But: consider `:stream/put` lowering: lower target; :push; lower val-node; :stream-put. In named VM: target in val → pushed to St; val-node in val; :stream-put pops target from St and uses val. In stack VM (future B4): target conj'd; :push no-op; val-node conj'd; :stream-put must pop val-node then target. So B4 must implement :stream-put to consume two stack entries. The no-op :push comment says \"the value it would push is already there\". Fine.\n\nc) Any cases in named VM where a :push is emitted and the value in val was produced earlier (stale val)? All emit sites are immediately after lower-node. lower-node always ends with a producing instruction for all node types? Check each: :literal → :const ✓; :variable → :var ✓; :lambda → :closure ✓; :application → ends with :call (result to val) ✓; :if → ends with lower-node of alternative… wait, :if emits: test, branch-false else, consequent, jump end, mark else, alternate, mark end. Code path taken ends with consequent or alternate's producer ✓; :ffi-call ✓ (result to val presumably); :stream-make ✓; :stream-put — result to val? engine handles effect, run-effect returns val' ✓ presumably; :stream-cursor ✓; :stream-next ✓; :stream-close ✓; :gensym ✓; :store-get ✓; :store-put ✓ (val ← v); :park — emits :park but parks/halts — :push never after park (park only appears where a value is expected… :vm/park in linearize emits :park; if :vm/park is an operand of an application, lowering emits :push after it — named VM's :park halts the machine (writes parked continuation and control nil), so :push never executes. In stack VM B3, :park throws not-yet-implemented. B4 concern); :current-continuation ✓ val ← reified; :vm/resume — emits :resume after lower-node of val-node ✓.\n\nSo all :push sites follow a producer. No-op is sound.\n\nd) Masking risk: could a future caller emit :push with different intent? This fix makes :push silently a no-op — if B4 implements a producer that (by mistake) stores into some other register rather than conj'ing, :push would silently drop it. But stack VM has no other value registers; the comment documents the convention. Acceptable.\n\ne) Now — one important check: the stack VM's `:branch-false` pops the stack (stack' = pop stack). The named VM reads val and does not consume St. In lowered if: test is lowered (producer conj'd in stack VM; set val in named) then :branch-false. Stack VM pops test value — correct. But then :push after if as operand… consistent.\n\nBut here's a real divergence candidate: `:call` in named VM — f and args are on St; val is unchanged (whatever stale). After closure call, at callee entry val is still caller's stale val… and callee's :return returns val. In stack VM everything is through the stack. Since lowering always produces fresh values before use, no divergence.\n\nf) `:halt`: named halts with val. Stack halts; value = peek stack. In root lowering: root node lowered (ends with producer → top of stack), then :halt. But wait — what if root is `:application` with tail? true? The :call conj's result → peek OK. What if root is :vm/park? Named: parks (blocked state). Stack B3: throws not-yet-implemented. B4 concern.\n\nWhat if root is :if? Ends with arm producer ✓.\n\ng) Another: named `:return` with empty K: `put-registers vm nil nil val [] E nil` — halts with val, sets stack to []. value method returns val. Stack VM `:return` empty continuation: status halted, stack [val]. value = val ✓.\n\nh) Stack VM `:call` with `(fn? f)`: conj's result; named: val ← result, St truncated. Consistent.\n\ni) Now — `:call` with argc when stack is smaller than argc+1? f-pos negative → nth throws. Named: same (subvec would throw). Both error; parity of error messages is B5 concern. Fine.\n\nNow tail call in stack VM: `:call argc true` — continuation is not extended. But note: in named VM tail call, the frame record has :env E (caller env) — but tail call doesn't push the frame; apply-call with tail?=true keeps K. On :return from callee, frame popped restores env. In stack VM same structure. ✓.\n\nBut — subtle issue with stack VM tail calls and stack-base: callee's body executes; its :return pops continuation frame {return-pc, frames, stack-base}. Truncates stack to stack-base and conjs val. Since stack-base was recorded at original caller's call, tail call chain leaves exactly one value. ✓ matches named (truncates St to stack-base, val carries result).\n\nj) Now `frame-value`: frames is outermost-first; depth d reads idx = n-1-d. On call, body-frames = (conj (:frames f) locals) — closure captured frames at creation; extends with locals. ✓ design §4.\n\nk) `:closure` captures `frames` — current frame stack at closure creation. ✓.\n\nl) One thing to verify: `:load-bound` at main sequence (depth against empty frames): frame-value throws with depth out of range (idx = -1 negative). Valid images never do this (scope validation rejects). ✓.\n\nNow — RENAME verification. Files I read:\n\n- src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc: ns yin.vm.debruijn.stack ✓. Docstring line 10: \"B2 (the named-datom lowerer) does not yet exist, so this namespace never requires yin.vm.debruijn-code\" — stale docstring! B2 now exists. Line 18: \"are B4's phase\" fine. Also `eval` error message: \"B2 (the named-datom lowerer) does not exist yet, so there is no AST to convert here\" — stale. Docstring staleness: this file was merged before B2; the fix session added :push but didn't update the \"B2 does not exist yet\" claims. P3 (stale comment, not defect in code) — but the brief explicitly asks about \"stale docstring references\". These aren't references to old name but they're stale statements. Worth flagging as P3/P2.\n\n- stack.cljc references `yin.vm.debruijn.stack` nowhere as old name. Requires: yin.vm, yin.vm.engine ✓. No old `debruijn-vm` references ✓.\n\n- test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_test.cljc: ns yin.vm.debruijn.stack-test ✓; requires yin.vm.debruijn.stack ✓, yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test (intentionally non-renamed) ✓. Docstring: \"B2 (the named-datom lowerer) does not exist yet\" — also stale (lines 5-6). P3.\n\n- debruijn_linearize.cljc: requires yin.vm.code, yin.vm.debruijn, yin.vm.linearize ✓ — doesn't require stack VM. Line 147 docstring mentions \"yin.vm.debruijn.stack's :frames\" ✓ new name. No old name ✓.\n\n- debruijn_linearize_test.cljc: requires yin.vm.debruijn.stack ✓.\n\n- debruijn_code.cljc: docstring refers to \"docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md S2\" — but that file doesn't exist (doc wasn't renamed!). Same for stack.cljc, stack_test, linearize, linearize_test, debruijn_vm_contract_test (B0). All refer to docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md but actual file is docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md. This is the main rename inconsistency: design doc rename wasn't performed. The brief says it was. Finding: P1 or P2? Nothing breaks at runtime (only docstring path references), but the brief explicitly asks to verify rename consistency, and the doc itself (yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md line 282: \"The VM is a new yin.vm.debruijn-vm namespace\", line 389: \"New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\", line 326, line 390, lines 402-403, line 418) still uses old file/namespace names. So: doc rename missing + doc contents stale w.r.t. rename. P2 (must fix before commit? orchestrator claimed done — so P1 against brief's claim? I'll rate P2: it's documentation, no code breaks, but brief asserts a state that's false; commit would ship dangling doc references). Actually all source files' docstrings cite a nonexistent path — that's exactly the sort of stale doc reference the brief asks to catch. I'll rate it P1 against rename claim being false (must fix before commit: rename doc or fix references). Hmm, P1 is \"must fix before commit\". Committing with every file citing a nonexistent doc path is a real defect for this repo's standards (docs are heavily cross-referenced). Call it P1.\n\nWait — double-check: maybe the doc was renamed but glob missed it? Glob for docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn* found only -vm.md and -projection.md. Read of stack.md failed with \"File not found\". Confirmed missing.\n\nAlso verify: any other files still requiring old ns `yin.vm.debruijn-vm`? My grep output showed: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc ns yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test (intentional), references in collab prompts (fine), docs (stale), docs/orchestrator-log (historical, fine). No src requires of old ns. Let me re-verify nothing requires `yin.vm.debruijn-vm` as a namespace.\n\nAlso verify old file is gone: git status shows RM src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc -> src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc. But wait — `yin.vm.debruijn` (the dormant projection namespace) still exists at src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc, and now we also have directory src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/. Does having both debruijn.cljc file and debruijn/ directory cause any issue? For Clojure's require, no — yin.vm.debruijn resolves to debruijn.cljc; yin.vm.debruijn.stack resolves to debruijn/stack.cljc. Fine. In CLJS too, fine. In cljd? Should be fine. Not a defect, worth noting? Kondo is fine with it.\n\nLet me grep for any remaining requires of the old ns.\n\nNow — deep correctness concerns for B2 to scrutinize:\n\nA) `named-canonical-vector`'s `instructions` filter includes entities that have any of the structural attrs. All instruction entities have :yin.code/segment pointing to seg. Segment entity itself excluded. But `:yin.code/derived-from root-id` — root-id is an AST entity, not in the output batch. OK.\n\nBut: `index-batch` keys attrs by [e a]. Instruction entity's attrs include :yin.code/source — an AST entity id. `tuple` fn: `(get ia a)` for non-:pc kinds returns raw value. For :closure's params — :yin.code/params is a syms vector ✓.\n\nB) `by-pc` / `ordered`: `(mapv by-pc (range n))` — if pcs aren't exactly 0..n-1, by-pc returns nil → pc-of gets nil key, tuple fails weirdly. lower always produces contiguous pcs from 0. Fine, but no validation — `well-formed-vector?` is run later in adapt but after tuple construction; a nil in ordered would make `(get attrs nil)` → nil → tuple throws on nil ia with `(:yin.code/op ia)` returning nil → vector-operand-table lookup nil → get returns nil → map on nil throws NPE-ish. This is only for malformed input from lower, which can't happen. Acceptable.\n\nC) Now the important one: `rewrite` uses `(nth (nth v c) 1)` for closures in chain to get PARAMS — index 1 of named :closure tuple is params ✓ (named vector). In `image-scope-defect`, `(nth ct 1)` is ARITY of de Bruijn :closure ✓. Docstring notes both. ✓.\n\nD) `closure-body-ranges` used over named vectors in rewrite path, and over de Bruijn images in image-scope-defect and lift. Both have body at index 2 ✓.\n\nE) Exact-match lift law and `:source`: side table entries carry :source (AST entity id). lift ignores :source ✓.\n\nF) Round-trip check in `lift`: `(= v (:debruijn (rewrite attempt)))`. rewrite returns :debruijn as vector built by conj from []. attempt is mapv → vector. Equality between vectors ✓. But — v itself: if caller passes a list or vector-of-lists? Assume canonical vector. Fine.\n\nG) Synthesized names fresh against free names: `used = free ∪ assigned`. But subtle: free-names-of collects :load-free operands (symbols). Synthesized g__c_i symbols — can a free name literally be `g__3_0`? Then synthesizer skips (loop). ✓. Can synthesized names collide with MNEMONIC keywords? No, they're symbols.\n\nBut here's a subtler alpha-equivalence issue: lift-once for :load-free emits [:var name] — and rewrite resolves that :var against the param stack. If a free name happens to equal an enclosing synthesized parameter name, rewrite would resolve as :load-bound — changing the image! Does synthesis prevent this? `used` includes free-names-of v — so synthesized params never equal any free name in the image. ✓. And the given side-table path: original param might shadow… original named program: var resolved free because no enclosing binder with that name — resolve-name matched exactly the same stack during rewrite (adapt), so re-adapting lift of original table gives identical resolution. ✓ exact-match law holds.\n\nBut — round-trip check with foreign side table: if a foreign table's params cause a former :load-free's name to be captured by a synthesized/foreign param, rewrite's image differs → round-trip fails → fallback to synthesis. ✓ sound. If foreign table params resolve all vars identically but names differ (alpha rename) → round-trip passes → lift returns foreign spelling. That's by-design (\"accepted only if its adapted lift returns the original image\"). ✓.\n\nH) Determinism: adapt is deterministic — index-batch preserves first-appearance order; reduce deterministic; map over range. rewrite reduce over range n — side-table built with assoc — map. Determinism of image-hash tested. ✓. Note: side-table is a hash-map; tests only assert (map? side-table) and use get-in. Equality of maps is order-independent. ✓.\n\nI) Now — layout check placement: `adapt` calls `code/well-formed-vector?` before `rewrite` (which does layout-conforms?). Design: \"a `layout-conforms?` check failure is a validation defect\". ✓ throws.\n\nBut hmm — `well-formed-vector?` on named vector includes terminator rule: last instruction must be in code/terminators. lower emits root code then :halt then bodies… wait! Last instruction of the whole vector is the last body's :return, not :halt! Is :return a terminator? code/terminators — need to check. Probably #{:halt :return :jump}. Let me verify in code.cljc.\n\nAlso — main sequence ends with :halt mid-vector, then bodies follow. B1's target-bounds: closure body pc points into bodies region ✓.\n\nBut wait — deeper: `terminator-rule` only checks last pc. Last body's :return ✓ (if :return ∈ terminators).\n\nJ) B1's well-formed-image? on adapted image: image has :closure [arity body] :load-bound [d p] :load-free [name]. Opcode-table ✓. Saturation: [:call 2] — tail? must be non-nil; named vector's :call tuple from datoms: :yin.code/tail? (boolean ...) always materialized ✓.\n\nK) Another: `image-scalar-classes` / encode — corpus's :const values include keyword :done, string \"g\" prefix… :gensym's prefix operand is :str kind — in named datoms {:type :vm/gensym :prefix \"g\"} → :yin.code/prefix \"g\" ✓. For :default-gensym (no prefix) — does lower emit :gensym with :yin.code/prefix nil? emit! e :gensym :yin.code/prefix (get-attr e :yin/prefix) → nil value. Datom [e :yin.code/prefix nil] — hmm, does the datom layer drop nil values?? If nil attr values are dropped, tuple's `(get ia a)` → nil → [:gensym nil] — saturation rule requires non-nil at [:gensym 1]! Then well-formed-vector? would report :saturation defect and adapt would throw!\n\nWait — but tests presumably pass (orchestrator ran them). Let me think: does ast->datoms-with-root store :yin/prefix for gensym without prefix? Maybe the AST walker defaults prefix to nil and emits a datom with nil value… datoms with nil value are legal data in this codebase (plain attr-value). index-batch just assocs. `(get ia :yin.code/prefix)` → nil. Tuple [:gensym nil]. code/well-formed-vector? saturation: [:gensym 1] nil → defect! Then adapt throws \"Cannot adapt a malformed named vector: saturation\".\n\nHmm, but the semantic/load-image precedent — how does the named path handle this? In load-image's canonical step, same table read… and the named vector goes through load-vector which checks well-formed-vector?. So the named path should also fail on [:gensym nil]… unless lower/walker saturates defaults. lower-rows docstring says \"defaults saturated\" for row-lane. What about datom-lane? Let me check how vm/ast->datoms handles :vm/gensym — maybe defaults prefix to \"\" or emits a saturated value. Or maybe emit!'s operand pair [:yin.code/prefix nil] → datom with nil value → in index-batch, contains? true, value nil. Same result.\n\nWait, but actually — does the named path's own tests run {:type :vm/gensym} without prefix through lower + load-image? Named VM tests presumably cover gensym via eval. Let me check ast->datoms gensym handling. Actually — hold on. `well-formed-vector?`'s saturation rule says nil at saturated slot is defect. If named pipeline works for default gensym, then either walker emits a default, or… Let me look at vm/ast->datoms for :vm/gensym.\n\nActually the tests presumably pass — orchestrator ran them (\"orchestrator has already run them\"). If :default-gensym's adapt threw, tests would fail. So something must saturate. Let me verify anyway — grep ast->datoms for gensym.\n\nAlso check {:type :stream/make} without :buffer for `:default-buffer` — [:stream-make nil]? Saturation [:stream-make 1] → same question. In named VM, :stream-make uses `(or (nth inst 1) vm/default-stream-capacity)` — semantic.cljc line 369-370 — so named pipeline tolerates nil buffer?! But well-formed-vector? saturation rule would reject [:stream-make nil]… unless load path saturates nils before validation. Hmm, semantic.cljc:369 `(or (nth inst 1) vm/default-stream-capacity)` suggests nil buffer reaches VM — so vectors with nil buffer are considered well-formed somewhere? Contradicts saturation rule… unless ast->datoms saturates defaults (emits default-capacity). Need to check.\n\nLet me grep ast->datoms for gensym/stream-make defaults.\n\nThe lowering backs up the layout premise of B2 (the body is out-of-line, and each body is terminated by the body-emitting loop's own `:return`). One remaining question: the saturation rule — does `ast->datoms` saturate defaults for `:vm/gensym` without `:prefix` and `:stream/make` without `:buffer`? If it doesn't, then `adapt`'s `well-formed-vector?` check should throw an exception on those corpus programs.\n"}}
{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key facts from linearize:\n\n1. Bodies are emitted out-of-line after the main sequence (loop after `:halt`). Each body is `mark! l` (label at body start), `lower-node body`, then `(emit! lambda :return)`. So each body ends with an explicit `:return`. Good — the first `:return` at-or-after body in `closure-body-ranges` is the body's own terminating `:return`, but only if there's no earlier `:return` within the body's pc range. Can `:return` appear inside a body before its end? `lower-node` never emits `:return` — only the body-emitting loop does. So within a body, the first `:return` at-or-after body is exactly its terminator… wait, bodies are emitted sequentially: body1 [.. :return] body2 [.. :return]. The first `:return` at-or-after body1's start is body1's own terminator. Because lower-node never emits :return. Correct.\n\nBut wait — what if one closure's body contains a nested lambda? The nested lambda's `:closure` instruction is emitted inline within the outer body; the nested body is appended later at the tail of the queue. So bodies never interleave: body i's range is [start_i, return_i], and body i+1 begins after return_i. Non-overlapping.\n\n2. Main sequence: root code + `:halt`. Bodies come after. So no body covers pc 0. Good.\n\n3. Now — `named-canonical-vector` filter: `instructions` excludes `seg`, includes entities that have one of #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op}. lower's output also includes the AST datoms themselves (input `:yin/*` datoms are part of the batch — wait, `lower` takes named-datoms and emits code datoms; the output is `(into [[seg ...] ...] (mapcat ...))` — does the output include the input AST datoms? Looking at `lower`: the return value is just segment entity + instruction entities. Doesn't re-emit input AST datoms. But B2's `adapt` calls `(linearize/lower named-datoms opts)` and passes only that output into `named-canonical-vector`. So batch = segment entity (attrs: :yin.code/type, :yin.code/length, :yin.code/derived-from) + instruction entities (:yin.code/segment, :yin.code/pc, :yin.code/op, operands, :yin.code/source).\n\nFilter: segment entity is excluded both by `(not= e seg)` and by not having structural attrs — wait, does it? Segment has :yin.code/type, :yin.code/length, :yin.code/derived-from. None in the structural set #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op}. So double-excluded. Instruction entities have all three structural attrs. OK.\n\nHmm — but what about AST entity references: `:yin.code/source` value is the AST entity id (from input datoms). That's an attribute of instruction entities, not its own entity in this batch. Fine — `:source` vector carries those ids.\n\nAlso `:yin.code/derived-from root-id` on segment. Fine.\n\nNow `index-batch`: datoms are `[e a v t m]` — 5 elements. Destructuring with `[e a v]` — takes first 3. Fine.\n\n`seg` detection: `(first (filter #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) order))`. OK.\n\n4. `named-canonical-vector` resolves `:pc`-kind operands through `pc-of` — for `:closure`, operands from vector-operand-table are [[:yin.code/params :syms] [:yin.code/body :pc]]. So tuple = [:closure params body-position]. body's label value in datoms is `(pc->eid (get labels v))` — an entity id; `pc-of` maps entity → position. Position equals original pc since ordered = by-pc over range n. Good.\n\n5. Now `adapt`: calls `code/well-formed-vector?` on named vector, then `rewrite`. rewrite checks `layout-conforms?`.\n\nChecking `layout-conforms?`:\n- ranges: for each :closure c in [c body r].\n- spans = distinct [b r].\n- every span has r non-nil and b > 0.\n- `(= (count spans) (count (distinct (map second spans))))` — r values are distinct. Hmm, this checks each body has a distinct end :return. Two closures sharing the same body would collapse in spans-distinct but then have equal r… if two :closure instructions point at the same body pc, spans distinct collapses to one, and r-distinct count is 1 = spans count 1. So shared bodies pass. Does lower ever emit two closures sharing a body? Each lambda gets a fresh label. But occurrence expansion — \"The image inherits occurrence expansion\" — each occurrence is expanded separately so no sharing. Fine either way.\n- Non-overlap: for every pair x<y (compare on [b r] vectors), `(< r1 b2)` or `(< r2 b1)`.\n\nWait — there's a subtle issue with non-overlap check when a body ends with r and the next body starts at b2 = r+1. Then (< r1 b2) is true. Fine.\n\nCan spans ever nest? Body A [b1 r1] contains a nested closure whose body B [b2 r2] satisfies b1 < b2 <= r2 <= r1? In lower's layout, nested bodies are appended after all outer bodies… actually the bodies queue processes FIFO: a body's nested lambdas get appended to the tail of the queue, so nested bodies come after the outer body's :return. So non-overlap holds.\n\nBut wait — could the outer body's [body, first-:return-at-or-after-body] range extend past the actual :return if there's no :return inside the body? No: each body ends with its own :return (emit! lambda :return). The first :return at-or-after body start is inside the body… unless the body itself contains an earlier :return — impossible since lower-node never emits :return. Correct.\n\nBut — what about `:halt`? Main sequence ends with :halt at some pc, then bodies begin. body-owner maps only body ranges. Closures in main sequence have no owner. Good.\n\n6. Now the important correctness question for `rewrite` on `:var`: `chain-of owner pc` — walks owner from the var's pc. owner maps each pc in [body, r] to the innermost closure c whose range covers it. But what about the `:return` instruction itself at pc r — it's included in range [body, (inc r)) — `(range body (inc r))`. Fine.\n\nBut subtlety: consider a `:closure` instruction located at pc c inside an outer body. owner(c) = outer closure. chain-of for a var at pc p inside an inner body: owner(p) = inner closure c_in; then owner(c_in) — c_in is the pc of the `:closure` instruction inside the outer body → outer closure c_out; then owner(c_out)… etc. Matches design: \"its parent is the body containing the `:closure`\".\n\nEdge case: closure instruction in main sequence: owner(c) absent → chain terminates. Good.\n\n7. `:var` at a pc not covered by any body (main sequence): stack = [] → resolve-name returns {:free name} → [:load-free name]. Correct — a var in main sequence can only be free (no enclosing binder). Matches named semantics (resolved against E).\n\n8. Now `image-scope-defect`:\n- Guard `(when (vector? v) ...)`.\n- For each pc, if tuple is :load-bound with count >= 3: depth/position check:\n  - count? both (integer, non-negative)\n  - chain = chain-of owner pc; if depth >= (count chain) → defect\n  - c = (nth chain depth); ct = (nth v c nil); if ct nil or count < 2 or arity (nth ct 1) not count? or position >= arity → defect.\n\nNote: for :load-bound tuples with count < 3, `image-scope-defect` silently skips (delegated to well-formed-image?'s arity rule). The docstring states \"assumes v is otherwise shape-valid\". OK.\n\nBut — chain-of depends on body-owner which depends on layout-conforms? being true. image-scope-defect doesn't check layout-conforms? first. If handed a non-conforming layout (overlapping bodies), body-owner is ill-defined (into {} keeps last). Docstring doesn't mention this. Hand-built test fixtures: `[[:closure 1 3] [:push] [:return] [:load-bound 5 0] [:return]]` — closure body at 3; ranges: [3, first :return at-or-after 3] = [3,4]. spans: b=3>0, r=4. Non-overlap trivial. Conforms. pc 3 body-owner → {3 c0, 4 c0}. chain-of(owner 3) = [0] (owner(3)=0; owner(0)? pc 0 is not in [3,4] → chain [0]). depth 5 >= 1 → defect {:rule :scope :pc 3}. Matches test expectation.\n\nSecond fixture: `[[:closure 1 2] [:return] [:load-bound 0 3] [:return]]` — closure body 2, range [2,3]. load-bound at pc 2: chain [0], depth 0 < 1 OK; ct = [:closure 1 2], arity 1; position 3 >= 1 → defect pc 2. Matches.\n\nHmm wait — in first fixture, `layout-conforms?`: body 3, r=4. Also pc 1 is `:push`, pc 2 is `:return`. Only closure at pc 0. spans [[3 4]]. pos? 3 OK. Fine.\n\nBut subtlety: the `:return` at pc 2 — irrelevant.\n\nNow — does `image-scope-defect` agree with B1's `scope-defect` on all inputs? Not required to be identical, but tests assert both reject hand-built fixtures with same {:rule :scope :pc p}. B1's scope-defect walks reachability. For fixture 1: B1: all-body-chains: walk from 0: pc0 :closure → chain '() owner{0 '()}, nested [[3 '(1)]]. Successors of pc0 = [1]. pc1 :push owner{1 '()}. pc2 :return terminator. Then queue [3 '(1)]: walk-body from 3: pc3 :load-bound owner{3 '(1)}, pc4 :return. Then scope-defect: :load-bound at pc3: chain '(1), depth 5 >= 1 → {:rule :scope :pc 3}. Matches.\n\nFixture 2: `[[:closure 1 2] [:return] [:load-bound 0 3] [:return]]`: B1 walk from 0: closure body 2 chain (1); pc1 :return terminator; body 2: pc2 :load-bound, pc3 :return. Load-bound at pc 2: depth 0 < 1, position 3 >= arity 1 → scope defect pc 2. Matches.\n\n9. Now deeper question about `image-scope-defect`: it uses chain-of/body-owner over the whole vector, but for a `:load-bound` in a body whose closure chain differs from the reachability-based chain (B1) — e.g., :jump across bodies. B1 handles conflicts; B2's is \"B2's own copy\" — the design says \"B2 validates non-negative depth and position against the frame arity vector\". Both validators are required to accept, so B2's simpler approach is acceptable. Note though: B2's image-scope-defect is NOT called from `adapt`! Look: adapt does well-formed-vector? check, then rewrite, then side-table merge. Never calls image-scope-defect on its own output, and never calls dc/image-defect either. Tests do: `every-corpus-program-adapts-to-a-valid-image` asserts both validators on the output. The design says \"scope validation is required at both entry points where an image can be accepted: B2