age — `resolve-name` compares `(= name (nth frame p))` — a numeric \"name\" would resolve identically (var refers to same position), so rewrite would produce same image and lift would return garbage \"names\" in named vector. But that's a foreign table supplying weird data; round-trip check is the design's own acceptance rule (\"the side table supplied is accepted only if its adapted lift returns the original image\"). By-design behavior. OK.\n\nAnother subtlety: round-trip check in `lift` uses `rewrite` which requires layout-conforms? and throws otherwise. If v's layout doesn't conform, lift throws a layout error rather than a lift error. Acceptable.\n\nNow exact-match law: `lift(adapt(lower x), side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`. lift-once reconstructs :var from params recorded in side-table. But wait — for rightmost-wins duplicate parameters: (fn [x x] x) resolves to [0 1]. lift-once reads (nth params 1) = 'x — original spelling. Exact match. Good.\n\nBut what about `:var` slots in the side table? rewrite records `{:kind :var}` for var slots (no name) — and adapt merges `:source`. lift doesn't need them. And named vector reconstruction: lift-once copies every other tuple unchanged. But named vector for :var is [:var name] — one operand. lift-once emits [:var name]. Exact-match law requires equality against named-canonical-vector's output where :var tuple is [:var name]. Yes.\n\nBut — structural detail: :var's side-table entry has :kind :var, and lift ignores. Fine.\n\n11. Now potential issue: `rewrite`'s output is built with `(conj debruijn ...)` starting from `[]` — conj on vector appends; final :debruijn is a vector. lift compares `(= v (:debruijn (rewrite attempt)))` — if v is a vector, vector equality with a vector is fine. adapt returns :image debruijn (vector). Tests use (= image (:debruijn (dl/rewrite lifted))). rewrite returns a vector; lifted is a vector from mapv. OK.\n\n12. `named-canonical-vector` — another check: `instructions` filter uses `(some #(contains? (get attrs e) %) structural)`. What about entities in the batch that have :yin.code/segment but aren't instructions? None. What about AST input entities? Not in lower's output. OK. But wait — in the test's `adapted` and `named-vector`, `dl/adapt (ast-datoms ast)` — ast-datoms = (second (vm/ast->datoms-with-root ast)). lower emits only code datoms. Good.\n\n13. Now `:closure` inside `named-canonical-vector`: vector-operand-table gives :closure [params :syms] [body :pc]. Datoms carry :yin.code/params (vector of syms) and :yin.code/body (resolved entity id → pc-of → position). Good.\n\nWait — potential defect in `tuple` fn: `(get code/vector-operand-table mnem)` — for mnemonic `:push` the entry is [] → tuple is [:push]. For `:resume` it's [[:yin.code/parked-id :kw]] → [:resume parked-id]. OK.\n\n14. `source` vector: `(mapv #(get-in attrs [% :yin.code/source]) ordered)`. adapt merges :source into each side-table entry — but only entries that exist (var and closure slots). `(reduce-kv ... side-table)` on existing entries. Design says side-table indexed by pc with closure entries carrying params and \"every entry carrying :source\". Yes — only :var/:closure entries exist. Matches B2 docstring (\"side table records binder names indexed by instruction slots… :var slots don't carry their own name\").\n\nHmm, design §2 says \"binder names and provenance are carried in a diagnostic side table indexed by instruction slots\". Fine.\n\n15. Now B2 test corpus: claims \"every named mnemonic yin.vm.code/mnemonics declares is reached at least once: :const :var :closure :push :call :return :jump :branch-false :halt :gensym :store-get :store-put :stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close :park :resume :current-continuation :ffi-call\". Let me verify coverage against corpus:\n- :const — literals ✓\n- :var — worked-example ✓\n- :closure ✓\n- :push — application ✓\n- :call ✓\n- :return — body ✓\n- :jump — if-in-tail ✓\n- :branch-false ✓\n- :halt ✓\n- :gensym — :default-gensym {:type :vm/gensym} ✓ and store-and-control with :prefix ✓\n- :store-get/:store-put — store-and-control ✓\n- :stream-make — :streams {:type :stream/make :buffer 4} and :default-buffer ✓\n- :stream-put — :streams ✓\n- :stream-cursor ✓, :stream-next ✓, :stream-close ✓ (nested in :streams)\n- :park — :park-resume ✓ ({:type :vm/park})\n- :resume ✓\n- :current-continuation — store-and-control ✓\n- :ffi-call — :ffi and :ffi-no-args ✓\n\nBut wait — is the test actually asserting mnemonic coverage? `every-corpus-program-adapts-to-a-valid-image` only asserts image-defect nil and side-table map. Nowhere does it assert that the corpus actually reaches every mnemonic! The docstring claims coverage but there's no test that computes the set of mnemonics reached across corpus and compares to code/mnemonics. Hmm — is that a defect? Design's B2 completion criterion: \"all nodes and opcodes\". A test that just claims coverage in docstring without asserting it is weaker — if a corpus program silently failed to emit a mnemonic (e.g., :park — does lowering of {:type :vm/park} require anything?), nothing catches it. This is a legitimate P2: mnemonic coverage is asserted only in prose, not in code. Actually wait — more importantly, would `adapt` succeed on all corpus programs? For instance, :streams AST `{:type :stream/put ...}` — vm/ast->datoms-with-root must support those node shapes. Test presumably passes (orchestrator has run it). Also `:park-resume`: if node with test being false literal, consequent park, alternate resume. OK.\n\nBut further, one actual check: does ast->datoms-with-root accept `{:type :stream/make}` without :buffer (default-buffer)? Presumably yes, since tests pass.\n\nHmm, but I can't run tests. My job: find defects by reading.\n\nCoverage-assertion gap: P3/P2.\n\n16. `corpus-images-load-and-run-on-b3` runs only 5 programs (:literal :duplicate-param :worked-example :zero-arity-call :if-in-tail). The rest can't run on B3 (stream/park/ffi/gensym/current-continuation are B4, so :not-yet-implemented). Reasonable. But `:store-and-control` includes :current-continuation — can't run. Fine.\n\nWait — :duplicate-param runs on B3: image = closure body with [:closure 2 b] ... [:load-bound 0 1] [:return]. Runs fine.\n\n17. Now the `:push` fix itself. Concerns:\n\na) No-op :push is correct for all current emit sites (verified: :push is always emitted immediately after a node lowering that leaves a value; in stack VM every producer conj'd).\n\nb) But: consider `:stream/put` lowering: lower target; :push; lower val-node; :stream-put. In named VM: target in val → pushed to St; val-node in val; :stream-put pops target from St and uses val. In stack VM (future B4): target conj'd; :push no-op; val-node conj'd; :stream-put must pop val-node then target. So B4 must implement :stream-put to consume two stack entries. The no-op :push comment says \"the value it would push is already there\". Fine.\n\nc) Any cases in named VM where a :push is emitted and the value in val was produced earlier (stale val)? All emit sites are immediately after lower-node. lower-node always ends with a producing instruction for all node types? Check each: :literal → :const ✓; :variable → :var ✓; :lambda → :closure ✓; :application → ends with :call (result to val) ✓; :if → ends with lower-node of alternative… wait, :if emits: test, branch-false else, consequent, jump end, mark else, alternate, mark end. Code path taken ends with consequent or alternate's producer ✓; :ffi-call ✓ (result to val presumably); :stream-make ✓; :stream-put — result to val? engine handles effect, run-effect returns val' ✓ presumably; :stream-cursor ✓; :stream-next ✓; :stream-close ✓; :gensym ✓; :store-get ✓; :store-put ✓ (val ← v); :park — emits :park but parks/halts — :push never after park (park only appears where a value is expected… :vm/park in linearize emits :park; if :vm/park is an operand of an application, lowering emits :push after it — named VM's :park halts the machine (writes parked continuation and control nil), so :push never executes. In stack VM B3, :park throws not-yet-implemented. B4 concern); :current-continuation ✓ val ← reified; :vm/resume — emits :resume after lower-node of val-node ✓.\n\nSo all :push sites follow a producer. No-op is sound.\n\nd) Masking risk: could a future caller emit :push with different intent? This fix makes :push silently a no-op — if B4 implements a producer that (by mistake) stores into some other register rather than conj'ing, :push would silently drop it. But stack VM has no other value registers; the comment documents the convention. Acceptable.\n\ne) Now — one important check: the stack VM's `:branch-false` pops the stack (stack' = pop stack). The named VM reads val and does not consume St. In lowered if: test is lowered (producer conj'd in stack VM; set val in named) then :branch-false. Stack VM pops test value — correct. But then :push after if as operand… consistent.\n\nBut here's a real divergence candidate: `:call` in named VM — f and args are on St; val is unchanged (whatever stale). After closure call, at callee entry val is still caller's stale val… and callee's :return returns val. In stack VM everything is through the stack. Since lowering always produces fresh values before use, no divergence.\n\nf) `:halt`: named halts with val. Stack halts; value = peek stack. In root lowering: root node lowered (ends with producer → top of stack), then :halt. But wait — what if root is `:application` with tail? true? The :call conj's result → peek OK. What if root is :vm/park? Named: parks (blocked state). Stack B3: throws not-yet-implemented. B4 concern.\n\nWhat if root is :if? Ends with arm producer ✓.\n\ng) Another: named `:return` with empty K: `put-registers vm nil nil val [] E nil` — halts with val, sets stack to []. value method returns val. Stack VM `:return` empty continuation: status halted, stack [val]. value = val ✓.\n\nh) Stack VM `:call` with `(fn? f)`: conj's result;