ct in code) — but the brief explicitly asks about \"stale docstring references\". These aren't references to old name but they're stale statements. Worth flagging as P3/P2.\n\n- stack.cljc references `yin.vm.debruijn.stack` nowhere as old name. Requires: yin.vm, yin.vm.engine ✓. No old `debruijn-vm` references ✓.\n\n- test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_test.cljc: ns yin.vm.debruijn.stack-test ✓; requires yin.vm.debruijn.stack ✓, yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test (intentionally non-renamed) ✓. Docstring: \"B2 (the named-datom lowerer) does not exist yet\" — also stale (lines 5-6). P3.\n\n- debruijn_linearize.cljc: requires yin.vm.code, yin.vm.debruijn, yin.vm.linearize ✓ — doesn't require stack VM. Line 147 docstring mentions \"yin.vm.debruijn.stack's :frames\" ✓ new name. No old name ✓.\n\n- debruijn_linearize_test.cljc: requires yin.vm.debruijn.stack ✓.\n\n- debruijn_code.cljc: docstring refers to \"docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md S2\" — but that file doesn't exist (doc wasn't renamed!). Same for stack.cljc, stack_test, linearize, linearize_test, debruijn_vm_contract_test (B0). All refer to docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md but actual file is docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md. This is the main rename inconsistency: design doc rename wasn't performed. The brief says it was. Finding: P1 or P2? Nothing breaks at runtime (only docstring path references), but the brief explicitly asks to verify rename consistency, and the doc itself (yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md line 282: \"The VM is a new yin.vm.debruijn-vm namespace\", line 389: \"New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\", line 326, line 390, lines 402-403, line 418) still uses old file/namespace names. So: doc rename missing + doc contents stale w.r.t. rename. P2 (must fix before commit? orchestrator claimed done — so P1 against brief's claim? I'll rate P2: it's documentation, no code breaks, but brief asserts a state that's false; commit would ship dangling doc references). Actually all source files' docstrings cite a nonexistent path — that's exactly the sort of stale doc reference the brief asks to catch. I'll rate it P1 against rename claim being false (must fix before commit: rename doc or fix references). Hmm, P1 is \"must fix before commit\". Committing with every file citing a nonexistent doc path is a real defect for this repo's standards (docs are heavily cross-referenced). Call it P1.\n\nWait — double-check: maybe the doc was renamed but glob missed it? Glob for docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn* found only -vm.md and -projection.md. Read of stack.md failed with \"File not found\". Confirmed missing.\n\nAlso verify: any other files still requiring old ns `yin.vm.debruijn-vm`? My grep output showed: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc ns yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test (intentional), references in collab prompts (fine), docs (stale), docs/orchestrator-log (historical, fine). No src requires of old ns. Let me re-verify nothing requires `yin.vm.debruijn-vm` as a namespace.\n\nAlso verify old file is gone: git status shows RM src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc -> src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc. But wait — `yin.vm.debruijn` (the dormant projection namespace) still exists at src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc, and now we also have directory src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/. Does having both debruijn.cljc file and debruijn/ directory cause any issue? For Clojure's require, no — yin.vm.debruijn resolves to debruijn.cljc; yin.vm.debruijn.stack resolves to debruijn/stack.cljc. Fine. In CLJS too, fine. In cljd? Should be fine. Not a defect, worth noting? Kondo is fine with it.\n\nLet me grep for any remaining requires of the old ns.\n\nNow — deep correctness concerns for B2 to scrutinize:\n\nA) `named-canonical-vector`'s `instructions` filter includes entities that have any of the structural attrs. All instruction entities have :yin.code/segment pointing to seg. Segment entity itself excluded. But `:yin.code/derived-from root-id` — root-id is an AST entity, not in the output batch. OK.\n\nBut: `index-batch` keys attrs by [e a]. Instruction entity's attrs include :yin.code/source — an AST entity id. `tuple` fn: `(get ia a)` for non-:pc kinds returns raw value. For :closure's params — :yin.code/params is a syms vector ✓.\n\nB) `by-pc` / `ordered`: `(mapv by-pc (range n))` — if pcs aren't exactly 0..n-1, by-pc returns nil → pc-of gets nil key, tuple fails weirdly. lower always produces contiguous pcs from 0. Fine, but no validation — `well-formed-vector?` is run later in adapt but after tuple construction; a nil in ordered would make `(get attrs nil)` → nil → tuple throws on nil ia with `(:yin.code/op ia)` returning nil → vector-operand-table lookup nil → get returns nil → map on nil throws NPE-ish. This is only for malformed input from lower, which can't happen. Acceptable.\n\nC) Now the important one: `rewrite` uses `(nth (nth v c) 1)` for closures in chain to get PARAMS — index 1 of named :closure tuple is params ✓ (named vector). In `image-scope-defect`, `(nth ct 1)` is ARITY of de Bruijn :closure ✓. Docstring notes both. ✓.\n\nD) `closure-body-ranges` used over named vectors in rewrite path, and over de Bruijn images in image-scope-defect and lift. Both have body at index 2 ✓.\n\nE) Exact-match lift law and `:source`: side table entries carry :source (AST entity id). lift ignores :source ✓.\n\nF) Round-trip check in `lift`: `(= v (:debruijn (rewrite attempt)))`. rewrite returns :debruijn as vector built by conj from []. attempt is mapv → vector. Equality between vectors ✓. But — v itself: if caller passes a list or vector-of-lists? Assume canonical vector. Fine.\n\nG) Synthesized names fresh against free names: `used = free ∪ assigned`. But subtle: free-names-of collects :load-free operands (symbols). Synthesized g__c_i symbols — can a free name literally be `g__3_0`? Then synthesizer skips (loop). ✓. Can synthesized names collide with MNEMONIC keywords? No, they're symbols.\n\nBut here's a subtler alpha-equivalence issue: lift-once for :load-free emits [:var name] — and rewrite resolves that :var against the param stack. If a free name happens to equal an enclosing synthesized parameter name, rewrite would resolve as :load-bound — changing the image! Does synthesis prevent this? `used` includes free-names-of v — so synthesized params never equal any free name in the image. ✓. And the given side-table path: original param might shadow… original named program: var resolved free because no enclosing binder with that name — resolve-name matched exactly the same stack during rewrite (adapt), so re-adapting lift of original table gives identical resolution. ✓ exact-match law holds.\n\nBut — round-trip check with foreign side table: if a foreign table's params cause a former :load-free's name to be captured by a synthesized/foreign param, rewrite's image differs → round-trip fails → fallback to synthesis. ✓ sound. If foreign table params resolve all vars identically but names differ (alpha rename) → round-trip passes → lift returns foreign spelling. That's by-design (\"accepted only if its adapted lift returns the original image\"). ✓.\n\nH) Determinism: adapt is deterministic — index-batch preserves first-appearance order; reduce deterministic; map over range. rewrite reduce over range n — side-table built with assoc — map. Determinism of image-hash tested. ✓. Note: side-table is a hash-map; tests only assert (map? side-table) and use get-in. Equality of maps is order-independent. ✓.\n\nI) Now — layout check placement: `adapt` calls `code/well-formed-vector?` before `rewrite` (which does layout-conforms?). Design: \"a `layout-conforms?` check failure is a validation defect\". ✓ throws.\n\nBut hmm — `well-formed-vector?` on named vector includes terminator rule: last instruction must be in code/terminators. lower emits root code then :halt then bodies… wait! Last instruction of the whole vector is the last body's :return, not :halt! Is :return a terminator? code/terminators — need to check. Probably #{:halt :return :jump}. Let me verify in code.cljc.\n\nAlso — main sequence ends with :halt mid-vector, then bodies follow. B1's target-bounds: closure body pc points into bodies region ✓.\n\nBut wait — deeper: `terminator-rule` only checks last pc. Last body's :return ✓ (if :return ∈ terminators).\n\nJ) B1's well-formed-image? on adapted image: image has :closure [arity body] :load-bound [d p] :load-free [name]. Opcode-table ✓. Saturation: [:call 2] — tail? must be non-nil; named vector's :call tuple from datoms: :yin.code/tail? (boolean ...) always materialized ✓.\n\nK) Another: `image-scalar-classes` / encode — corpus's :const values include keyword :done, string \"g\" prefix… :gensym's prefix operand is :str kind — in named datoms {:type :vm/gensym :prefix \"g\"} → :yin.code/prefix \"g\" ✓. For :default-gensym (no prefix) — does lower emit :gensym with :yin.code/prefix nil? emit! e :gensym :yin.code/prefix (get-attr e :yin/prefix) → nil value. Datom [e :yin.code/prefix nil] — hmm, does the datom layer drop nil values?? If nil attr values are dropped, tuple's `(get ia a)` → nil → [:gensym nil] — saturation rule requires non-nil at [:gensym 1]! Then well-formed-vector? would report :saturation defect and adapt would throw!\n\nWait — but tests presumably pass (orchestrator ran them). Let me think: does ast->datoms-with-root store :yin/prefix for gensym without prefix? Maybe the AST walker defaults prefix to nil and emits a datom with nil value… datoms with nil value are legal data in this codebase (plain attr-value). index-batch just assocs. `(get ia :yin.code/prefix)` → nil. Tuple [:gensym nil]. code/well-formed-vector? saturation: [:gensym 1] nil → defect! Then adapt throws \"Cannot adapt a malformed named vector: saturation\".\n\nHmm, but the semantic/load-image precedent — how does the named path handle this? In load-image's canonical step, same table read… and the named vector goes through load-vector which checks well-formed-vector?. So the named path should also fail on [:gensym nil]… unless lower/walker saturates defaults. lower-rows docstring says \"defaults saturated\" for row-lane. What about datom-lane? Let me check how vm/ast->datoms handles :vm/gensym — maybe defaults prefix to \"\" or emits a saturated value. Or maybe emit!'s operand pair [:yin.code/prefix nil] → datom with nil value → in index-batch, contains? true, value nil. Same result.\n\nWait, but actually — does the named path's own tests run {:type :vm/gensym} without prefix through lower + load-image? Named VM tests presumably cover gensym via eval. Let me check ast->datoms gensym handling. Actually — hold on. `well-formed-vector?`'s saturation rule says nil at saturated slot is defect. If named pipeline works for default gensym, then either walker emits a default, or… Let me look at vm/ast->datoms for :vm/gensym.\n\nActually the tests presumably pass — orchestrator ran them (\"orchestrator has already run them\"). If :default-gensym's adapt threw, tests would fail. So something must saturate. Let me verify anyway — grep ast->datoms for gensym.\n\nAlso check {:type :stream/make} without :buffer for `:default-buffer` — [:stream-make nil]? Saturation [:stream-make 1] → same question. In named VM, :stream-make uses `(or (nth inst 1) vm/default-stream-capacity)` — semantic.cljc line 369-370 — so named pipeline tolerates nil buffer?! But well-formed-vector? saturation rule would reject [:stream-make nil]… unless load path saturates nils before validation. Hmm, semantic.cljc:369 `(or (nth inst 1) vm/default-stream-capacity)` suggests nil buffer reaches VM — so vectors with nil buffer are considered well-formed somewhere? Contradicts saturation rule… unless ast->datoms saturates defaults (emits default-capacity). Need to check.\n\nLet me grep ast->datoms for gensym/stream-make defaults.\n\nThe lowering backs up the layout premise of B2 (the body is out-of-line, and each body is terminated by the body-emitting loop's own `:return`). One remaining question: the saturation rule — does `ast->datoms` saturate defaults for `:vm/gensym` without `:prefix` and `:stream/make` without `:buffer`? If it doesn't, then `adapt`'s `well-formed-vector?` check should throw an exception on those corpus programs.\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_6cc7f073da374abebaa34a06","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"gensym|stream/make|:yin/buffer|:yin/prefix|default-stream-capacity","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"-C":4,"head_limit":100}}]}}
