it as a scope defect. B1's scope-defect walks reachability, and if the chain supports it, it would accept the load-bound. So for malformed-layout images, B2 and B1 could disagree — but such an image would first fail B2's rewrite layout check at ADAPT time. `image-scope-defect` is only run on adapt output (which conforms) and on hand-built fixtures in tests. The docstring says \"independent of scope-defect — both are required to admit the image.\" If both are required to admit, then disagreement means rejection — conservative, no issue. Not a defect.\n\nOne more: `chain-of` inside `lift-once` for `:load-bound` — `(nth chain depth)` throws on out-of-range depth (IndexOutOfBounds), but for a malformed image without a side-table, lift… with side-table nil: attempt = lift-once v nil — could throw a raw IndexOutOfBounds rather than ex-info. lift's contract doesn't promise to validate. P3.\n\nWait, actually `lift-once` is called first with the side-table inside `lift` — `(lift-once v side-table)` — before the round-trip verification. For a FOREIGN image with out-of-range :load-bound, lift throws a raw index error. Acceptable? The design says B1's validator is the sole admitter, and lift on an invalid image is outside the contract. P3 note.\n\nOK, summary of findings:\n\n**P1:**\n1. The design-doc rename has not been carried out: `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` does not exist; the doc is still `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md`. The docstrings of all files under review (stack.cljc line 2, stack_test.cljc line 2, debruijn_linearize.cljc line 2, debruijn_linearize_test.cljc line 2, plus the modified debruijn_code.cljc line 2, debruijn_code_test.cljc line 2, debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc line 2) cite the nonexistent path, and the doc's own contents still use the old names (line 282 \"yin.vm.debruijn-vm namespace\", B3/B4 box lines 389-390, 402-403, B5 box line 418 cite debruijn_vm file paths). The brief asserts the rename was done; in this worktree, it isn't. Required