orked example) would genuinely fail round-trip and reach the fallback.\n\n5. `lift`/`lift-once` throws raw IndexOutOfBounds (via `nth chain depth`) on scope-invalid images without a trusted side-table; the docstring doesn't spell out the precondition (that B1 has validated the image). Consider documenting or guarding.\n\n6. B4 forward constraint created by the no-op `:push`: any future value-producing instructions (:stream-*, :ffi-call, :gensym, :current-continuation, :resume) must conj their result directly onto `:stack`; otherwise the no-op `:push` silently discards the value (there is no `val` register to fall back on). The comment above `:push` establishes the convention but doesn't call out the B4 obligation. Also `:resume` semantics differ: named `:push` after `:resume`... actually :resume is never followed immediately by :push inside lower (vm/resume node lowering: lower val-node, :resume — :resume's result is the resumption of the parked program; a :push after that belongs to the parked program's own continuation). Fine — just note the convention.\n\nWait, let me actually recheck the :resume/:push interaction: `:vm/resume` as an operand of an application: lower-node(resume) → lower val-node, :resume; then emit! :push. In the named VM: :resume restores the parked configuration — control transfers into the parked program; the :push after :resume never executes in the resumer's flow… instead the parked program continues, eventually :return-ing to… Hmm, this is B4 territory; parked-continuation machinery. Out of scope.\n\n7. Verdict on the `:push` fix: CORRECT. The comment's reasoning has been verified against semantic.cljc:275-277 (val/St split, opcode 22 commits val) and all five `:push` emission sites in linearize (lines 93, 96, 113, 120 datom lane; 270, 273, 288, 293 row lane — each immediately after a producer node's `lower-node`, and every producer in B3's implemented set conj's onto :stack). No other value register exists in DebruijnVM, and unimplemented producers throw `:not-yet-implemented`, so the no-op cannot mask a lost value today. Also verified that none of the merged stack_test fixtures contain :push, so no behavioral change to signed-off tests.\n\nFurther verification: the named VM's `:branch-false` reads `val` and doesn't touch St (semantic.cljc:282), while the stack VM pops — consistent because lower never emits `:push` after the test node. Good, worth a one-liner mention.\n\nAlso verify rename consistency in requires: no file requires yin.vm.debruijn-vm; stack_test requires yin.vm.debruijn.stack ✓ and yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test (intentionally retained name, ns exists in test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc) ✓.\n\nAny other P1-level issues in B2 itself? Let me think harder about potential defects:\n\n- `named-canonical-vector`'s `:source` vector: `(mapv #(get-in attrs [% :yin.code/source]) ordered)` — every instruction entity has :yin.code/source ✓ (lower emits it). adapt merges into side-table entries ✓.\n\n- `adapt`'s side-table merge: `(reduce-kv (fn [st pc entry] (assoc st pc (assoc entry :source (nth source pc)))) {} side-table)` — ✓.\n\n- Determinism: image is a vector built by conj — deterministic ✓. side-table is a hash-map — its contents are deterministic; hash-map equality is order-independent; image-hash is only over the image ✓.\n\n- `rewrite` on vectors with :var operands… `(nth t 1)` — named :var tuple is always 2 elements from canonical construction ✓.\n\n- Edge case: a program with a lambda in TAIL position of the main sequence, e.g., root = (lit 42)? main = [:const 42] [:halt], no body ✓. Root = lambda: main = [:closure params body] [:halt], body after halt. closure-body-ranges: body b=2, r=3 ✓ pos? ✓. chain-of for load-bound in body: owner(p)=0 → owner(0)? pc 0 is not in [2,3] → chain [0] ✓.\n\n- Edge case: two sibling lambdas: bodies emitted in queue order, contiguous ✓ disjoint.\n\n- Nested-closure FIFO subtlety: outer lambda A (body B_A) contains inner lambda C (body B_C). Queue: [A]. Emit A's body: mark s_A; lower body (emits :closure for C inline, appends C to queue); emit :return at r_A. Then C: mark s_C = r_A + 1; emit; :return r_C. Ranges [s_A r_A], [s_C r_C] disjoint ✓. Owner of C's :closure instruction pc (inside [s_A r_A]) = A ✓.\n\nBut wait — one more: what if the main sequence contains lambda C1 and lambda C2, and C1's body contains C3? Queue order: [C1, C2] during main lowering. Then loop: C1's body (appends C3 → queue [C1,C2,C3]), C2's body, C3's body. Ranges still contiguous and disjoint ✓.\n\n- Deeper subtlety about `:if` inside a body and `first :return at or after body`: :if emits :jump over the else arm; no :return inside ✓.\n\n- `layout-conforms?` requires `(pos? b)` — no body covers pc 0. What if the root node itself is inside… body always begins after main's :halt ≥ pc 1. What about a 1-instruction main? Minimum: root literal → [:const v] [:halt], body at pc 2 ✓.\n\n- `well-formed-vector?` on the named vector from lower: the last instruction is the last body's :return ∈ terminators ✓; but what if the program has no lambda? Last = :halt ✓.\n\n- Hmm, main sequence's :halt is mid-vector, followed by bodies. B1's `terminator-rule` only checks the last pc ✓. B1's `target-bounds`: closure body pc ≤ last ✓. B1's `scope-defect` walk: successors of :halt = [] (terminator) — so bodies are only reached via :closure declarations ✓, which walk-body schedules ✓.\n\n- One potential mismatch between B1 and B2: B1's scope check uses reachability-based chains with conflict detection; B2's image-scope-defect uses layout-based ranges. For adapt output both agree. The test fixtures confirm agreement on the two rejection cases. Fine.\n\n- Test `hand-built-out-of-range-images...` asserts `(nil? (dc/well-formed-image? image))` on fixture 1: [[:closure 1 3] [:push] [:return] [:load-bound 5 0] [:return]] — last :return ✓ terminator; body ref 3 ≤ 4 ✓; arity: :closure has 2 operands ✓ (table: arity+body); :load-bound 2 operands ✓. Shape-valid ✓.\n\n- Fixture 2: [[:closure 1 2] [:return] [:load-bound 0 3] [:return]] — shape ✓.\n\nNow, is there a case where `rewrite` produces `:load-free` for a name that is a parameter of a closure whose body doesn't cover that var's pc? That's precisely correct (free).\n\nWhat about `:var` referring to a parameter of a closure whose :closure instruction is at a pc covered by another body, but the var is in the main sequence? Vars in the main sequence: chain [] → free ✓ (can't be bound — no enclosing lambda).\n\nI'm fairly confident B2 is sound. One more — `resolve-name`'s rightmost-wins: position-in scans `(range (dec (count frame)) -1 -1)` → rightmost first ✓. And `bind-positional` binds duplicates: `(vec (take arity (concat args ...)))` — a positional frame retains both x slots; the rightmost position (index 1) gets the argument. Named bind-params: {'x second-arg}? Named's bind-params zips params and args into a MAP — (fn [x x] 1) called with (5): map {x 5} — single value. de Bruijn frame [5 nil]: position 1 → nil!! Wait. resolve-name resolves (fn [x x] x) to [0 1] (rightmost). bind-positional with args [5]: (take 2 (concat [5] (repeat nil))) = [5 nil]. load-bound 0 1 → nil!!! But named: {x 5} → 5. Divergence!?\n\nHold on — let me recheck engine/bind-params. The comment in stack.cljc says bind-params \"zips parameters and args, nil-filling.\" For duplicate parameters [x x] and args [5]: zip → {x 5, x 5}? With into/merge, last wins: {x 5}. So named (fn [x x] x) called with one arg returns 5.\n\nDe Bruijn: frame [5 nil], var x resolves to position 1 (rightmost wins) → nil. Divergence!\n\nWait, wait. Let me recheck resolve-name's docstring: \"position is the source parameter index, leftmost param is 0. Frames search inside-out; position search within a frame is right-to-left, so duplicate parameters are rightmost-wins exactly as yin.vm.engine/bind-params binds them — (fn [x x] x) resolves to the second x, [0 1].\"\n\nSo resolve-name says [0 1], claiming that matches bind-params's binding. But bind-positional gives [5 nil] for args [5] — position 1 = nil, whereas named gives 5. Is that right? Named bind-params: params [x x], args [5]. Zip: (zipmap [x x] [5])? zipmap stops at the shorter — {x 5}. So x → 5. De Bruijn's frame should be… for rightmost-wins to give 5 at position 1, the frame should be [nil 5]? bind-positional (take 2 (concat [5] (repeat nil))) = [5 nil] — position 1 = nil. Divergence in under-arity calls with duplicate params!\n\nFull arity: args [5 6]: named zipmap {x 5, x 6} → last wins → x=6. de Bruijn [5 6], position 1 → 6 ✓ match.\n\nUnder-arity args [5]: named {x 5} → 5. de Bruijn [5 nil] → position 1 → nil ✗ diverge.\n\nUnder-arity args []: named {} → x nil. de Bruijn [nil nil] → nil ✓.\n\nOver-arity args [5 6 7]: named {x 5 x 6} → 6 (zip stops at params length 2). de Bruijn (take 2 [5 6 7]) = [5 6] → position 1 → 6 ✓.\n\nSo the divergence is only for under-arity calls to closures with duplicate parameters where the var resolves to a rightmost position beyond the supplied args. Example: ((fn [x x] x) 5) → named: 5; de Bruijn: nil.\n\nHmm wait — but is that reachable via B2 + B3? Program: (app (lam '[x x] (tail (v 'x))) (lit 5)). B2 rewrite: var x → [0 1]. B3 call: argc 1, arity 2, locals = [5 nil]. load-bound 0 1 → nil. Named VM: bind-params {x 5} → val 5. Parity violation!\n\nBut wait — I should check what engine/bind-params actually does. Let me read it. bind-params might be nil-filling differently, e.g., (zipmap params (concat args (repeat nil)))? zipmap [x x] [5 nil ...] → {x 5} then {x nil}? zipmap with duplicate keys: later pair wins → {x nil}?? zipmap implementation: (reduce (fn [m [k v]] (assoc m k v)) {} (map vector keys vals)) — vals = [5 nil nil...]? No — zipmap stops at the shorter of the two seqs. (zipmap [x x] [5]) → pairs ([x 5]) — stops at length 1 → {x 5}. Hmm, zipmap takes min length. So {x 5} → 5.\n\nBut if bind-params is implemented as (into {} (map vector params (concat args (repeat nil)))) — takes params length: [x x] with [5 nil] → {x 5} then {x nil} — last wins → nil! Then de Bruijn matches.\n\nNeed to read engine/bind-params. Let me grep.\n\nAlso, the stack_test's under-arity test uses distinct params [a b] — doesn't cover duplicate-param under-arity. And B2's runnable corpus includes :duplicate-param, but the root is a bare lambda (never called!) — so the divergence isn't exercised. This might be a real P1/P2 parity defect — depending on bind-params's semantics. Let me read.\n\nThe trace of B2 is mostly fine, but I've discovered a potential parity discrepancy: in the case of an insufficient-arity call against duplicate parameters — `((fn [x x] x) 5)`. `resolve-name` resolves `x` to position 1 (rightmost takes precedence), and `bind-positional` returns the frame `[5 nil]` → so it becomes `nil`. Whether the named VM returns `5` or `nil` depends on the exact implementation of `engine/bind-params`. I'm reading it now.\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_bc3012c52f90453ea09a4ef9","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"defn bind-params","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"-A":25}}]}}
