B3: de Bruijn VM kernel'): the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:282:The VM is a new `yin.vm.debruijn-vm` namespace. It may implement existing VM\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:326:    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:389:    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:390:    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_test.cljc\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:402:    Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:403:    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_effects_test.cljc\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:418:    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_parity_test.cljc"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"e2c5bc96-f056-4656-80e2-149dc8c0b05c"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Important finding: the design document has NOT been renamed. `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` doesn't exist, and it's still `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md`. The docstrings inside the source/tests reference `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` — this is a dangling reference. The brief claims the rename has been performed, but it actually hasn't (at least as far as the docs are concerned). Wait — is it possible the doc file is gitignored, or that there are two files? The Glob only found yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md and yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md. So the doc rename is missing. This is P1 or P2: docstring references to a nonexistent path. Furthermore, the docs themselves still list old filenames (like src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc, etc.).\n\nShell commands were denied — can't run git. That's fine; it's read-only anyway.\n\nNext, read the design docs (sections 1–3 + the B2 box), the helper namespaces: yin.vm.debruijn (resolve-name), yin.vm.code (vector-operand-table, well-formed-vector?), yin.vm.debruijn-code (well-formed-image?, image-defect, image-hash), yin.vm.linearize (lower — where :push is emitted), and check the :push context in semantic.cljc more thoroughly. Also check references to yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The design doc rename appears NOT to have happened — the doc still lives at `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md`. Noted as a finding. Now reading the doc and supporting namespaces.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_1816a621900e4112a8d5b3e6","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790067269480,"messageId":"0633ba99-ed8b-4f5e-bbd0-8aa5915acc01"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_1816a621900e4112a8d5b3e6","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 667 lines\n1: # yin.vm de Bruijn VM\n2: \n3: Status: design; not implemented\n4: \n5: This document specifies a second executable path for `yin.vm`. The existing\n6: path lowers named Universal AST datoms to `:yin.code/*` and executes that\n7: image. This design defines a sibling VM for a derived linear image. No second\n8: lossless source view is built. The merged projection is retained as a dormant,\n9: non-load-bearing artifact and is not consumed by this VM or its linker.\n10: \n11: ## 1. Architecture and invariants\n12: \n13: Design invariant I: a yin.vm can share executable code over a `dao.stream`\n14: linker. The linker may be local or remote, but its boundary is always the\n15: stream.\n16: \n17: Three artifacts are associated with one named AST. The named datoms are the\n18: bijective source representation. The executable de Bruijn image is the\n19: execution artifact and the sole sharing identity, H. The merged projection is\n20: an alpha-canonical, lossy historical artifact. It is dormant, has no consumer\n21: in this design, and is not itself executable.\n22: \n23: Architecture A means lowering from projected records. Architecture B, the\n24: recommended architecture, lowers from named datoms and is not the same as A.\n25: Lowering projected records cannot promise named execution semantics because\n26: projection deliberately canonicalizes scalar spelling, preserves free names\n27: only in canonical form, removes binder names and provenance, and drops\n28: front-end `:yin/tail?`. Restricting the input to already-canonical programs,\n29: weakening continuation guarantees, and treating the records as a cache key\n30: would make that path unsound; making them non-identities would make it a poor\n31: executable artifact.\n32: \n33: Named datoms remain the lossless truth. Binder names and provenance are kept\n34: in the diagnostic side table used by the executable image, while lexical\n35: positions are derived by `resolve-name`; no second lossless fact structure is\n36: persisted. This follows the datom.world rule \"derive, don't persist\".\n37: \n38: The executable VM promises equivalence with the named semantic VM only after\n39: normalization. Results, effects, streams, cursors, stores, terminal and\n40: blocked outcomes, and errors must agree. Error comparison uses the message;\n41: ex-data is compared after recursively applying the value normalizer to every\n42: ex-data value. Closures compare as `{:type :closure :arity n}` only;\n43: continuation and parked values compare by type only; stream and cursor\n44: references compare by identity; host handles and telemetry are excluded.\n45: Behavioural application tests cover closure contents. This normalizer is\n46: defined and tested in B0, rather than assumed to be an existing repository\n47: utility.\n48: \n49: The free environment is the VM's initial environment value, fixed for one VM\n50: instance. It is not the mutable callee `:env` register left by an earlier\n51: named `run`. The named VM's environment leak is a separate defect and is not\n52: silently included in this contract. B5 uses fresh VM instances for ordinary\n53: parity. Park/resume fixtures that cross program boundaries are restricted to\n54: free names supplied by the initial environment or store until the named-VM\n55: leak is fixed; otherwise B5 records a named-path refusal.\n56: \n57: Until B7 dependency closure exists, invariant I safely shares an image only\n58: when it is closed for the receiving environment, or when that environment\n59: binds every free name identically. Free names are `:load-free` operands, not\n60: bindings. Closed means that the receiver-side closure check passes: every\n61: free name resolves through primitives or modules, and none is shadowed by the\n62: receiver's free environment or store. An image with no `:load-free` operands\n63: passes trivially. Otherwise B6 refuses it with `:unresolved-free` or\n64: `:shadowed-free`, rather than executing it.\n65: \n66: H is alpha-invariant for binder renames because binder names are outside the\n67: hash. Equal H therefore implies agreement on exact scalar spelling and free\n68: name operands as well as the same executable image. Alpha-equivalent source\n69: programs share H only when their front-end tail flags agree; this is sound but\n70: intentionally incomplete.\n71: \n72: The following are not promised to be identical: binder spelling in the\n73: dormant projection, instruction program counters, temporary ids, allocation\n74: identity, stack-trace text, source-map formatting, or interchangeability of\n75: continuations between the two VMs. Named datoms remain available for complete\n76: decoding and debugging; the executable image is not an inverse encoding.\n77: \n78: The VM obeys the datom.world invariants: state is explicit data, execution is\n79: an interpreter above streams, callbacks are never retained, effects are\n80: stream-visible, and the existing AST, merged dormant projection, lease, waitset,\n81: and named VM\n82: contracts are not changed.\n83: \n84: The following table records compliance with design invariant I and the\n85: datom.world principles.\n86: \n87:     +--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+\n88:     | Requirement              | Mechanism                                  |\n89:     +--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+\n90:     | I: share executable code | B1-B2 define H and the lift; B6 fetches    |\n91:     | over dao.stream          | and verifies images over streams without   |\n92:     |                          | requiring B3 or B4 (§2, §6, §7.2).         |\n93:     | Everything is a stream   | Effects, transfer, linking and failure     |\n94:     |                          | are stream outcomes (§4, §7.2).            |\n95:     | Everything is a          | Parked machine state is explicit data and  |\n96:     | continuation             | resumes through stream-carried events      |\n97:     |                          | within a VM; cross-host UCF transport is   |\n98:     |                          | deferred (§4, §7.2).                       |\n99:     | No hidden global state   | VM state, free-env and registries are      |\n100:     |                          | explicit values (§4).                      |\n101:     | No implicit control flow | Steps classify explicit data; missing      |\n102:     |                          | code parks and emits a request (§7.2).     |\n103:     | No callbacks             | No loader or callback is invoked; events   |\n104:     |                          | cross stream boundaries (§7.2).            |\n105:     | No shared mutable state  | Frames, stores and continuations are       |\n106:     |                          | persistent transition data (§4).           |\n107:     | No layer collapse        | Named source, lowering, VM and linker      |\n108:     |                          | remain separate interpreters (§1, §7.2).   |\n109:     | No assumed graphs        | Image refs and body ranges are validated   |\n110:     |                          | before execution (§3).                     |\n111:     | Derive, do not persist   | Named datoms stay authoritative; lexical   |\n112:     |                          | positions are derived (§1).                |\n113:     | Interpretation semantics | Named and de Bruijn VMs are compared by    |\n114:     |                          | the explicit normalizer (§1, B0).          |\n115:     | Host boundaries          | Cross-host common domain is tested;        |\n116:     |                          | unsupported classes refuse (§2, B1).       |\n117:     | Strained: frame env      | Frames require a lift for completion and   |\n118:     |                          | exported continuations (§4, B4).           |\n119:     | Strained: image state    | Loaded images are explicit values; the     |\n120:     |                          | linker must supply missing-code events.    |\n121:     | Strained: diagnostics    | Binder names and provenance are side data  |\n122:     |                          | and never executable identity (§2, §3).    |\n123:     +--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+\n124: \n125: ### Benefit and exit criterion\n126: \n127: The primary benefit is network-shareable, alpha-invariant, content-addressed\n128: executable code over `dao.stream`. Existing named code is already content\n129: addressed by `yin.vm.content`; this adds binder-alpha-invariant image identity\n130: and portable bytes for the common scalar domain. Sharing does not require the\n131: frame VM: its additional benefit is positional lookup performance, which is\n132: measured but not assumed. Free names still require B7 dependency closure.\n133: Physical Jing addresses remain print-based until the DaoJing CBOR work lands;\n134: H uses its own canonical encoding and is independent of that address.\n135: \n136: After B3, the semantic VM section 8 harness reports throughput, allocation,\n137: image size, and load time on identical pure-program corpora. The report is\n138: informational, not an acceptance condition. Retiring the semantic VM would\n139: require its own design because existing consumers depend on it; the owner may\n140: instead make the de Bruijn VM the default while retaining the semantic VM.\n141: \n142: ## 2. Instruction dimension and code identity\n143: \n144: The executable image uses a new `:yin.debruijn.code/*` dimension. The\n145: implementation defines and exports its descriptor; persistent publication and\n146: discovery are outside B0 through B7. Existing `:yin.code/*` semantics are\n147: unchanged. The new descriptor derives its opcode table as data from the\n148: existing `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table`; only lexical addressing differs:\n149: `:var` becomes `:load-bound` or `:load-free`, and `:closure` carries\n150: arity and body references rather than parameter symbols. The stack operations,\n151: `:const`, `:push`, `:halt`, `:branch-false`, stream operations, store\n152: operations, park, resume, gensym, and FFI retain their existing opcode shapes.\n153: Its descriptor declares a lowering-contract version, arity, ordered slots,\n154: canonical encoding, and one lift\n155: morphism from `:yin.debruijn.code/*` to `:yin.code/*`; the lift uses diagnostic\n156: binder names or synthesized names. Exact-spelling slots are typed raw `Bytes`,\n157: so no-NFC behavior is a slot rule, not an exception to the dimension contract.\n158: \n159: The new code namespace copies its small framing and length-prefix helpers and\n160: defines its own scalar tag table; it does not reach private vars in the merged\n161: dormant projection namespace.\n162: It uses `jing/sha256` and a separate executable scalar encoding: distinct tags\n163: represent nil, booleans, long, double, ratio, bigint, char, string, keyword,\n164: symbol, vectors, lists, maps, sets, and other explicitly supported values.\n165: Map and set entries are ordered by encoded bytes; vectors and lists are\n166: distinct classes. Numeric bits and string/name UTF-8 bytes are used exactly as\n167: supplied; no NFC or integral-double folding occurs in hashed bytes.\n168: Unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are refused with `:unsupported-value`, as they are\n169: by the projection's UTF-16 guard. Values outside the declared executable\n170: domain are refused with a qualified `unsupported-value` diagnostic, not\n171: silently canonicalized. This is deliberately distinct\n172: from `yin.vm.debruijn/encode-value`.\n173: \n174: Binder names and provenance are carried in a diagnostic side table indexed by\n175: instruction slot and are outside the image hash. Exact scalar bytes are in\n176: the `:const` operands and are included in the image hash; free names remain\n177: hashed `:load-free` operands.\n178: \n179: Host classification is explicit. On CLJS, a JavaScript number is encoded as a\n180: long when it is a safe integer and as a double otherwise. Integral-valued\n181: doubles are outside the CLJS common scalar domain, because JavaScript cannot\n182: distinguish them from longs; a CLJS receiver refuses such an image with\n183: `unsupported-value`. CLJS cannot distinguish source spellings that already\n184: read as the same number. Characters are a distinct class only where the\n185: host has one and distinguishes it from a one-codepoint string: the JVM.\n186: ClojureDart's `char?` does not reliably tell a genuine char from a\n187: one-codepoint string (verified during B1), so this dimension treats\n188: ClojureDart the same as CLJS here: no distinct :char class, chars encode\n189: as strings. Ratios and host bigints are JVM classes; neither ClojureDart's\n190: core nor this dimension defines a project-local equivalent, so B1 refuses\n191: them with `:unsupported-value` on CLJS and ClojureDart alike (the ratio\n192: fixture's designed either/or, resolved: encoded on the JVM, refused\n193: elsewhere). Cross-host byte identity is required only for values present\n194: on all three hosts. B1 tests for `1` versus `1.0` are JVM/Dart tests;\n195: ratio and char tests are JVM-only; shared cross-host fixtures cover the\n196: common scalar domain.\n197: \n198: The scalar classes an image uses are derived from tags in its hashed `:const`\n199: operands. A producer can scan those tags to identify common-domain-safe images.\n200: A receiving host that lacks a class refuses the image with a qualified\n201: `unsupported-value` outcome before execution; this is a host-boundary outcome,\n202: not a stream gap.\n203: \n204: The code-image identity H is computed by one B1 function, `image-hash`, over\n205: the descriptor hash, including its lowering-contract version, and the\n206: canonical positional instruction vector: pc-indexed tuples, refs resolved to\n207: pcs, no header, and exact scalar bytes. Provenance is\n208: a diagnostic side table indexed by pc and is outside the hash. H is the only\n209: executable and sharing identity. No projection fingerprint or second identity\n210: is attached to the image, request, response, verification, or cache. Any later\n211: cache is keyed by H.\n212: No cache is specified by B0 through B5; any later cache is an explicit value\n213: keyed by H. A receiver hashes the received canonical wire bytes before\n214: decoding; those bytes are H's preimage. `jing/segment-key` is only the storage\n215: address for the same bytes and is not H; `yin.vm.content` stores and fetches\n216: bytes, while verification recomputes `image-hash`.\n217: \n218: ## 3. Lowering and scope\n219: \n220: The lowerer receives complete `:yin/*` named datoms. It reuses only the public\n221: `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` helper. That helper compares names exactly,\n222: including rightmost-wins duplicate parameters, and does not canonicalize\n223: values. Projection-only helpers such as `index-frame`, `build-node`, and\n224: `project-node` are not reused. Unexpanded-macro validation remains the named\n225: front end's responsibility.\n226: The projection is therefore kept, not removed: if it is ever retired, this\n227: public helper must first move into this design's namespace.\n228: \n229: The walk is deterministic and left to right. Lambda bodies are out of line,\n230: applications evaluate operator then operands, and `if` evaluates one arm.\n231: The lowerer copies the front end's `:yin/tail?` exactly as the named linearizer\n232: does. It does not infer tail position from syntax. Only `:application` has a\n233: tail operand in the existing instruction table; `:dao.stream.apply/call`\n234: lowers to `:ffi-call` and has no tail operand.\n235: \n236: The image inherits occurrence expansion, fresh lambda labels, and body layout\n237: from `yin.vm.linearize/lower`; B2 does not repeat that traversal. Any adapter\n238: memo is keyed by the emitted body and pc context. If a future source-level\n239: memo is introduced, its key must include node identity, the full vector of\n240: parameter-name vectors, and tail context. Frame arities remain separate scope\n241: validation data and cannot determine name resolution.\n242: \n243: The executable image may carry a debug node hash and named source reference.\n244: Those fields are always diagnostic metadata outside code identity. They replace\n245: no named source map.\n246: \n247: Scope validation is mandatory in both places where an image can enter:\n248: \n249: 1. B2 validates nonnegative depth and position against the frame-arity vector.\n250: 2. B1's image validator repeats the check by walking each body's declared\n251:    arity and enclosing-body chain. A shape-valid hand-built image with\n252:    `[:load-bound [5 0]]` is rejected before execution.\n253: \n254: No projected reader participates in this path; only the B1 image validator\n255: admits executable images.\n256: \n257: ## 4. VM state and execution\n258: \n259: The sibling VM uses explicit state:\n260: \n261:     {:segment code-image\n262:      :pc pc\n263:      :frames [frame ...]\n264:      :free-env initial-name-map\n265:      :stack operand-vector\n266:      :continuation continuation-data\n267:      :store store\n268:      :status status}\n269: \n270: Frames are positional vectors ordered outermost to innermost; frame zero for a\n271: bound reference is the innermost frame, read from the end of the vector. A\n272: closure captures the persistent frame stack and its body reference. Calls use\n273: the positional equivalent of `bind-params`: `(vec (take arity (concat args\n274: (repeat nil))))`. Missing arguments are nil-filled and extras are dropped.\n275: The existing named `bind-params` remains name-keyed and unchanged.\n276: \n277: `:load-free` uses the existing `resolve-var` order with `free-env`, store,\n278: primitives, and module registry. Positional locals never fall through to a\n279: store key. `:macro?` is retained only in named datoms; runtime closure\n280: application ignores it exactly as the named runtime does after macro expansion.\n281: \n282: The VM is a new `yin.vm.debruijn-vm` namespace. It may implement existing VM\n283: protocols without adding methods, and it may reuse data-only engine helpers\n284: for name resolution, primitive descriptions, stream descriptors, store\n285: operations, gensym, and continuation records. It reimplements any helper\n286: that serializes the named register layout, including private response wait\n287: entries and named telemetry snapshots. No existing protocol or VM semantics\n288: change.\n289: \n290: It does not implement `vm/IVMState/environment` until a frame-to-named lift is\n291: defined. If that method is later implemented, it returns the lifted named\n292: environment, not the positional frame vector.\n293: \n294: Park and resume carry the frame stack, free environment, operand stack, code\n295: identity, continuation chain, store view, parked records, gensym counter,\n296: primitives, modules, code aliases, and the semantic VM's other non-environment\n297: registers as explicit data. B4 supplies a frame-aware completion adapter or a\n298: lift through the descriptor morphism before exporting a continuation. UCF\n299: remains proposed and deferred, so heterogeneous continuation transport is not\n300: yet promised.\n301: \n302: ## 5. Effects and equivalence boundaries\n303: \n304: The executable path covers the existing literal, variable, lambda,\n305: application, `if`, stream, store, gensym, continuation, park, resume, and FFI\n306: operations. Macro expansion remains upstream; unexpanded macro diagnostics\n307: are not runtime execution.\n308: \n309: Equivalence excludes the named VM's stale-register leak. A named VM instance\n310: used for B5 must begin with a fixed initial environment, or the test records a\n311: named-path defect and does not claim parity. It also excludes representation\n312: details that the normalizer intentionally erases. It does not exclude exact\n313: literal values, spelling-sensitive store keys, front-end tail choices, stream\n314: outcomes, or error classifications.\n315: \n316: ## 6. Implementation phases\n317: \n318: Each phase B0 through B7 has a file box, a must-not-change list, completion\n319: criteria, and JVM, Node/CLJS, ClojureDart, kondo, and cljstyle verification.\n320: The shortest sharing path is B0, B1, B2, B6, then B3 through B5, then B7.\n321: B6 depends on B1, B2, and the lift, not on B3 or B4; it may execute a fetched\n322: image through the existing semantic VM after lifting with synthesized names.\n323: \n324: ### B0: contract and normalizer\n325: \n326:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc\n327:     Existing edits: none\n328:     Must not change: AST, emitter, merged projection namespace, named VM,\n329:     code dimension\n330: \n331: Freeze the result/error normalizer and actual parity corpus:\n332: `parity-test` plus the every-tag corpora in `content_test` and\n333: `completion_test`. Completion requires named-VM self-parity and idempotence of\n334: the normalizer, plus normalized closure, continuation, error, stream, cursor,\n335: and store comparisons, duplicate-parameter, and all-node fixtures.\n336: \n337: ### B1: executable dimension and validator\n338: \n339:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc\n340:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc\n341:     Existing edits: none\n342:     Must not change: yin.vm.code, :yin.code/*, merged projection namespace\n343: \n344: Define and export the descriptor, derived opcode table, exact executable\n345: scalar encoder, code-image hash, and validator. Completion includes malformed\n346: rows,\n347: out-of-range bound operands, exact spelling preservation, distinct hashes\n348: for composed and decomposed e-acute, a ratio fixture that is either\n349: encoded or refused with `:unsupported-value` (JVM encodes, CLJS and\n350: ClojureDart both refuse), distinct hashes for `1`/`1.0` on JVM and Dart,\n351: distinct hashes for ratios and chars on the JVM, and identical bytes\n352: across hosts for the common scalar domain. Golden image bytes and H\n353: values for a frozen corpus are checked on all three host lanes. A\n354: lowering layout change must fail those fixtures rather than silently\n355: forking identity.\n356: \n357: ### B2: named-datom lowerer adapter\n358: \n359:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc\n360:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize_test.cljc\n361:     Existing edits: none\n362:     Must not change: yin.vm.linearize and named lowering semantics\n363: \n364: Run the existing `yin.vm.linearize/lower` on the named datoms, then rewrite\n365: each `:var` operand and replace each `:closure` parameter vector with its\n366: arity. Scope reconstruction is a pure function of the image, never of\n367: `:yin.code/source`: each body range is `[entry, first :return]`, its parent is\n368: the body containing the `:closure`, and the name stack for `resolve-name` is\n369: built innermost-first. This is opposite to the runtime frame vector's\n370: outermost-to-innermost order and the conversion is explicit. `segment-scope`\n371: is the public precedent; the new namespace reproduces the rules of private\n372: `closure-ranges` and `layout-conforms?` rather than changing their visibility.\n373: A failed\n374: `layout-conforms?` check is a validation defect. Completion requires\n375: deterministic output, every node and opcode, lexical validation, a structural\n376: opcode-by-opcode comparison with `lower` differing only at variable and\n377: closure operands, image encode/validate/load round trips, and\n378: `lift(adapt(lower x), side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`. The lift is a\n379: function of the image plus its diagnostic side table. With the original binder\n380: names in the side table, equality holds; with synthesized names, the lifted\n381: result is alpha-equivalent to `lower x`, including `(fn [x x] x)`. Synthesized\n382: names must be fresh against the image's free-name set. A supplied side table is\n383: accepted only when adapting its lift returns the original image.\n384: Every image B2 emits must be accepted by B1's validator, and every hand-built\n385: out-of-range image must be rejected by both validators.\n386: \n387: ### B3: de Bruijn VM kernel\n388: \n389:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\n390:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_test.cljc\n391:     Existing edits: none\n392:     Must not change: semantic VM, engine, IVM protocols, named environment,\n393:     merged projection namespace\n394: \n395: Implement frames, closures, loads, calls, returns, branches, literals, and\n396: store operations. Completion requires pure-program parity using B0's\n397: normalizer and fresh initial environments, plus the informational section 1\n398: benchmark report, not an acceptance condition.\n399: \n400: ### B4: effects and continuations\n401: \n402:     Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\n403:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_effects_test.cljc\n404:     Existing edits: none\n405:     Must not change: dao.stream protocols, lease, waitset, named effect rules,\n406:     merged projection namespace\n407: \n408: Implement stream operations, primitives, FFI, gensym, current-continuation,\n409: park, and resume. Completion requires parity for values, errors, effects,\n410: stores, stream outcomes, and blocked states. Cross-program park/resume tests\n411: must use only initial-environment or store-resolved free names until the\n412: named-VM environment leak is fixed. Before a continuation is exported, B4\n413: must either lift frames through the descriptor morphism or run the frame-aware\n414: completion adapter so `:yin.k/requires` is not under-approximated.\n415: \n416: ### B5: differential integration\n417: \n418:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_parity_test.cljc\n419:     Existing edits: none\n420:     Must not change: named storage and the existing linearizer pipeline\n421: \n422: Compare `ast->datoms` -> B2 -> de Bruijn VM against\n423: `ast->datoms` -> `linearize/lower` -> semantic VM. Use the actual parity and\n424: content/completion corpora, not helper-only tests as execution fixtures.\n425: Completion requires programs differing only in binder names to produce the\n426: same H on every host lane for the common scalar domain. Programs differing in\n427: exact scalar spelling, front-end tail flags, or free names must produce\n428: different H values. An image sent over a `dao.stream` from one host lane must\n429: load, validate, and execute on another with the same normalized result as\n430: local execution. B5 uses committed golden bytes for cross-runtime identity;\n431: the real cross-process stream transfer is a B6 acceptance test. All three host\n432: lanes must agree under the normalizer.\n433: \n434: ### B6: committed closed-image stream linker\n435: \n436:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n437:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_responder.cljc\n438:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n439:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_responder_test.cljc\n440:     Existing edits: none\n441:     Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, dao.stream,\n442:     named VM semantics\n443: \n444: Implement fetch-by-H for closed images. B6 tests use an explicit fetch; the\n445: `:call-hash` instruction is emitted only by the later dependency linker. A\n446: host emits a REQUEST value carrying H, and a responder process holds an\n447: explicit value mapping H to image bytes or queries an H-to-address datom chosen\n448: by its composition. The response carries canonical wire bytes or a qualified\n449: absence/unsupported outcome. The receiver hashes those bytes before decoding,\n450: checks H and the descriptor, runs the closure check, validates, and loads only\n451: verified bytes. Every free name must resolve through primitives or modules and\n452: must not be shadowed by free-env or store; failures are `:unresolved-free` or\n453: `:shadowed-free`. Completion requires JVM to Dart stream transfer, where the\n454: receiver initially knows only H, mismatch and unsupported refusal, absence\n455: events, and equal normalized results using the existing semantic VM via lift.\n456: \n457: ### B7: dependency closure linker\n458: \n459:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n460:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_dependency_test.cljc\n461:     Existing edits: none\n462:     Must not change: merged projection namespace or B6 closed-image semantics\n463: \n464: Add value/stream name environments, hash-of-unit dependency closure, and\n465: strongly connected component manifests. Completion requires cycle-safe\n466: ordering, explicit authority and provenance, and stream-defined retry,\n467: timeout, and permanent-absence outcomes.\n468: \n469: ## 7. Test matrix and non-goals\n470: \n471: The matrix includes exact scalar spelling, names, tail flags,\n472: provenance, scope, duplicate parameters, nil fill, extras dropped, control\n473: flow, streams, effects, continuations, macros, malformed images, and all\n474: existing parity/content/completion execution fixtures. It also includes\n475: alpha-equivalent binder renames with equal image hashes, exact-spelling and\n476: tail-flag differences with distinct hashes, free-name changes, stream transfer,\n477: and cross-host code-image bytes.\n478: \n479: Non-goals are equality saturation, a new JIT, global distribution beyond B6\n480: and B7, and any change to the merged dormant projection or its fingerprint.\n481: \n482: ### 7.1 Prior art: Unison's runtime\n483: \n484: Unison's published runtime documentation describes let-rec minimization,\n485: lambda lifting, ANF, an IR with De Bruijn indices and stack positions, and\n486: decompilation. The supplied source listing is reported to contain runtime\n487: components including ANF, MCode, Machine, Serialize, Decompile, and\n488: Canonicalizer. These facts support architecture B as an analogy:\n489: the executable form is derived from a full-information term and uses explicit\n490: stack positions, rather than being lowered from a lossy identity projection.\n491: They do not establish that Unison executes its exact stored identity form.\n492: \n493: The named linearizer already supplies a deterministic stack-oriented image.\n494: Lambda lifting, ANF, and let-rec minimization are therefore not adopted by\n495: this design: lambda lifting would change closure capture into extra ordinary\n496: parameters, and the resulting calling convention is a separate optimization\n497: contract. They remain possible upstream AST-to-AST stream stages as described\n498: below. Unison's decompilation\n499: to displayable terms is not assumed here to preserve exact source datoms, tail\n500: flags, tempids, or provenance. This design infers no stronger decompilation\n501: property from those sources.\n502: \n503: In this project, lambda lifting or ANF may be separate AST-to-AST stream\n504: stages upstream of both lowerers. Such a stage reads and writes named\n505: `:yin/*` datoms, is optional, and changes arity. Synthesized parameters need\n506: side-table entries. It must write `:yin/tail?` on every node it creates,\n507: because both lowerers copy that flag and infer nothing. Lifted and unlifted\n508: programs must be compared under an explicit arity caveat, and the B0\n509: normalizer applies to whichever named AST the lowerer receives. This VM\n510: neither depends on nor forbids those stages.\n511: \n512: The verified identity documentation is:\n513: \n514:     https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/the-big-idea/\n515:     https://unison-lang.org/whats-new/writeup-of-our-first-unison-meetup\n516: \n517: The verified runtime documentation and source directory are:\n518: \n519:     https://raw.githubusercontent.com/unisonweb/unison/trunk/\n520:       unison-runtime/src/Unison/Runtime/docs.markdown\n521:     https://github.com/unisonweb/unison/tree/trunk/\n522:       unison-runtime/src/Unison/Runtime\n523: \n524: UNVERIFIED here: evaluation as a documented pipeline stage, the detailed\n525: component listing, whether the runtime input is the stored identity artifact,\n526: reference resolution, MCode and Machine semantics, and whether\n527: `docs.markdown` is current.\n528: \n529: ### 7.2 Committed B6 and later B7 linker topology\n530: \n531: Reference-by-hash is a stream process, not hidden VM machinery. B6 is the\n532: committed closed-image fetch path. It consumes executable images and a\n533: value-held name environment, uses only H as identity and request key, emits\n534: verified image requests, consumes image bytes, and emits a linked image or an\n535: explicit refusal. This follows the axiom that\n536: all IO and data flow through append-only streams. No global mutable registry\n537: is introduced, and no direct function-to-function call crosses the boundary.\n538: The boundary is `dao.stream`; local and remote resolution are the same\n539: transport-neutral mechanism, and their physical placement is not a linker\n540: decision.\n541: \n542: `yin.vm.content` stores and fetches bytes. B hashes received canonical wire\n543: bytes before decoding, then verifies H with `image-hash`, not by treating\n544: `jing/segment-key` as H. `load-vector`\n545: and `:code-aliases` remain named-VM machinery. B6 does not require persistent\n546: publication or discovery: an observer or peer wired by the composition supplies\n547: the response stream.\n548: \n549: A linked dimension may contain `:call-hash H` once B7 emits it. If H is\n550: unavailable, the linked interpreter parks its explicit continuation and emits a\n551: REQUEST value carrying H. A response stream carries canonical image bytes or\n552: a qualified absence/unsupported outcome. B hashes the wire bytes before\n553: decoding, rejects mismatches, runs the receiver closure check, loads only\n554: verified bytes, and resumes. The request token, parked continuation, and\n555: response are data; no callback is retained. Gaps, timeouts, and permanent\n556: absence remain explicit stream events.\n557: \n558: B7 supplies dependency closure for free names. Mutually recursive definitions\n559: form one strongly connected component, whose members are ordered by canonical\n560: image hash before the component hash is computed. A name environment is a\n561: value or stream with a declared ledger, trust, and provenance. Streams provide\n562: failure events but do not choose retry, timeout, or permanent-absence policy.\n563: \n564: This does not require a semantic change to B0 through B5. B3 already makes\n565: loading and continuation state explicit, and B4 carries park and resume as\n566: data transitions. The VM accepts loaded images only as values in its state and\n567: never invokes a loader; absence is a park plus a request emission.\n568: \n569: Completion for B6 requires closed-image fetch by H, verified cross-runtime\n570: execution, malformed or missing-address diagnostics, and explicit absence and\n571: unsupported outcomes. B7 additionally requires cycle-safe manifests and\n572: dependency closure. Neither modifies the merged dormant projection or\n573: establishes any property of the Unison runtime, which is UNVERIFIED here.\n574: \n575: ### 7.3 Lossless source view non-goal\n576: \n577: The VM does not build a second lossless record DAG. Named datoms remain the\n578: lossless source, and lexical positions, binder diagnostics, and provenance are\n579: derived into the executable image as needed. A separate inverse view would\n580: require its own design and is outside B0 through B7.\n581: \n582: If a future coarser semantic-deduplication identity is wanted, it must be\n583: derived from the executable image by hashing a normalized view with canonical\n584: scalars and tail flags dropped. It must not reuse or equal the dormant pinned\n585: projection fingerprint; it would be a new derived value and a separate\n586: decision.\n587: \n588: ## 8. Risks and owner decisions\n589: \n590: Risks include divergence from front-end tail flags, exact scalar spelling,\n591: named-VM environment leakage, malformed bound operands, source-map loss, and\n592: UCF's deferred transport contract.\n593: \n594: DECIDED:\n595: \n596: 1. D1 normalizer: adopt recursive ex-data normalization, closure type and\n597:    arity comparison, continuation and parked type comparison, stream/cursor\n598:    ids, host-handle-free stores, and telemetry exclusion. This serves\n599:    interpretation parity without inventing hidden state.\n600: 2. D2 lossless DAG: drop it from B0 through B7 and make it a non-goal. Named\n601:    datoms remain authoritative; lexical positions and diagnostics are derived.\n602:    This serves \"derive, don't persist\" and removes redundant authority.\n603: 3. D3 descriptor: adopt the arity, slots, raw-Bytes encoding, scalar domain,\n604:    and lift to `:yin.code/*`. Unsupported host classes refuse before running.\n605: 4. D4 environment leak: `yin.vm.semantic` writes the callee's merged env into\n606:    `:env` on return and park (`semantic.cljc:285-293, 317`); `vm-load-program`\n607:    does not reset it (`semantic.cljc:679-690`), while `vm-eval` restores the\n608:    initial env (`semantic.cljc:779`). Treat this as a separate named-VM defect\n609:    outside these phases; retain the fixture restriction until fixed. The fix\n610:    requires its own design.\n611: 5. D5 hash identity: `:call-hash H` names the canonical executable image hash\n612:    of a definition unit; a recursive component is one hashed unit. H is not a\n613:    projection identity.\n614: 6. D6 linker principles: the name environment is a value or stream, trust and\n615:    provenance are composition policy, SCCs hash as units with canonical member\n616:    ordering, and retry, timeout, and absence are stream events. The boundary\n617:    is always `dao.stream`, whether local or remote.\n618: 7. D7 end state: both VMs coexist. B3 reports benchmark numbers only; the\n619:    semantic VM is never retired without its own design.\n620: 8. D8 committed B6: closed images are fetched by H over `dao.stream`, verified\n621:    with `image-hash`, and loaded only after verification. This directly serves\n622:    invariant I.\n623: 9. D9 H function: B1's single `image-hash` function hashes the descriptor\n624:    hash, including the lowering-contract version, and the canonical positional\n625:    vector. Jing addresses are storage locations, not H.\n626: 10. D10 scalar classes: classes are derived from hashed constant tags; a host\n627:     lacking one refuses before execution. No redundant class manifest exists.\n628: 11. D11 free names: closed means the receiver closure check passes against\n629:     its primitive/module tables with no free-env or store shadowing. B6 refuses\n630:     `:unresolved-free` and `:shadowed-free`; an image with no free operands\n631:     passes trivially.\n632: 12. D12 sharing identity: H is the only identity on the sharing path. The\n633:     merged projection remains dormant, untouched, and has no new dependents.\n634:     This serves invariant I and \"derive, do not persist\" because H is already\n635:     alpha-invariant for binder names.\n636: 13. D13 dependency order: B6 depends on B1, B2, and the lift, not B3 or B4;\n637:     the existing semantic VM can execute a fetched image after lifting. This\n638:     serves invariant I by making sharing available before the new VM kernel.\n639: 14. D14 wire verification: receivers hash canonical received bytes before\n640:     decoding, and the descriptor hash includes the lowering-contract version.\n641:     This serves content integrity and host-boundary safety.\n642: 15. D15 receiver closure: free names are derived by scanning operands and are\n643:     accepted only when primitive/module resolution is unshadowed. This serves\n644:     explicit state and prevents same-H semantic drift.\n645: 16. D16 responder ownership: B6 responders hold an explicit H-to-bytes value\n646:     or query an H-to-address datom selected by composition. This serves the\n647:     no-global-state and no-callback invariants.\n648: \n649: DEFERRED:\n650: \n651: - B6 request/response schema and contract stamps.\n652: - B7 dependency-closure and SCC manifest mechanics.\n653: - The exact retry, timeout, and permanent-absence event vocabulary.\n654: - A named-VM environment-leak fix design, which owns D4's release condition.\n655: - If the merged projection is ever retired, move the public `resolve-name`\n656:   helper into this design's namespace before removal. Dormant means kept, not\n657:   removed.\n658: \n659: No owner decision blocks B0. B0 starts with D1 and the frozen parity corpus.\n660: \n661: ## 9. What this design must not change\n662: \n663: The AST schema, AST walker, named emitter, named linearizer, semantic VM,\n664: existing `:yin.code/*` dimension, `dao.stream` protocols, lease layer, waitset,\n665: named storage, and the merged projection namespace and pinned fingerprint\n666: remain unchanged. The projection is dormant and has no new dependents. The\n667: executable encoding and VM are additional artifacts."}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"2d19525d-589c-44f8-9cf1-ce6aa26f009a"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Finished reading the design docs. Main B2 requirements (lines 357–385):\n\n- Run `linearize/lower` on named datoms and rewrite the `:var` operands. Replace the `:closure` parameter vector with arity.\n- Scope reconstruction is a pure function of the image only. Body range is `[entry, first :return]`. Parent = the body containing the `:closure`. The name stack for resolve-name is built innermost-first. Explicit conversion against the runtime frame vector.\n- `segment-scope` is a public precedent. Reproduce the private `closure-ranges` and `layout-conforms?` rules.\n- If `layout-conforms?` fails, it's a validation defect.\n- Completion conditions: deterministic output; all nodes and opcodes; lexical validation; opcode-level structural comparison against lower (differences only in var/closure operands); image encode/validate/load roundtrip; `lift(adapt(lower x), side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`; the lift using synthesized names is alpha-equivalent including `(fn [x x] x)`; synthesized names must be fresh with respect to the free-name set; the side table is accepted only if, after adaptation, its lift returns the original image; every image B2 emits is accepted by B1's validator; hand-crafted out-of-range images are rejected by both validators.\n\nAlso §3: \"Scope validation is required at both points: B2 validates that depth/position are non-negative against the frame arity vector, and B1's validator repeats this.\"\n\nPoints to note: \"image encode/validate/load roundtrip\" — B2's tests have `corpus-images-load-and-run-on-b3` (load into B3) and dc/well-formed-image?/image-defect, but does encode/decode roundtrip exist? The tests have image-hash determinism and create-vm load, but no B1 encode/decode roundtrip tests (e.g., something like dc/encode-image / decode). Need to check the debruijn-code API. If there are encode-vector/decode functions, B2 tests may be missing the encode roundtrip. Read debruijn_code.cljc.\n\nNext, read the auxiliary namespaces: yin.vm.debruijn (resolve-name), yin.vm.code (vector-operand-table, well-formed-vector?), yin.vm.debruijn-code, yin.vm.linearize (where :push is emitted).\n\nFinished reading the design document. Next up are the supporting namespaces — `debruijn_code.cljc`, `code.cljc` (operand tables / well-formedness), `debruijn.cljc` (resolve-name), and `linearize.cljc` (where `:push` is emitted).","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_6b52163ae94948babbcaa2cb","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_8ff3a84822444cbf8f6704eb","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"resolve-name|vector-operand-table|well-formed-vector\\?","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"head_limit":60}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790067293070,"messageId":"f30ef2d3-7ee9-4362-a101-9ec4d2d25c3d"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_6b52163ae94948babbcaa2cb","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 852 lines\n1: (ns yin.vm.debruijn-code\n2:   \"B1 (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md S2): the executable de Bruijn\n3:    image dimension (`:yin.debruijn.code/*`) and its validator. Standalone:\n4:    no lowerer (B2), no VM (B3), no linker (B6) -- only the dimension's\n5:    descriptor, its derived opcode table, its own exact scalar encoder, the\n6:    one function that computes H (`image-hash`), and the validator that is\n7:    the sole admitter of an executable image on this path (S3).\n8: \n9:    The opcode table is derived AS DATA from `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-\n10:    table` (S2): every mnemonic's operand shape is read straight off that\n11:    table and renamespaced into `:yin.debruijn.code/*`, except `:var`,\n12:    which splits into `:load-bound` (depth + position) and `:load-free`\n13:    (name), and `:closure`, which trades its parameter-symbol vector for an\n14:    arity and a body pc. `yin.vm.code`, `:yin.code/*`, and the merged\n15:    dormant projection namespace (`yin.vm.debruijn`, `yin.vm.pipeline`) are\n16:    never edited and never reached through a private var; the small\n17:    framing and length-prefix helpers below (`to-hex`, `framed`,\n18:    `int64-le-hex`, `double-le-hex`, `utf8-byte-length`, `code-unit-at`)\n19:    are copied from `yin.vm.debruijn`, which documents them as meant to be\n20:    copied, not imported. The UTF-16 surrogate-pairing guard is\n21:    independently reimplemented to the same rule, not copied, per this\n22:    phase's own instruction.\n23: \n24:    This dimension's own executable scalar encoder is deliberately\n25:    distinct from `yin.vm.debruijn/encode-value`: it never applies NFC to\n26:    strings or identifier parts (exact bytes as supplied), it never folds\n27:    an integral double into a long (1 and 1.0 hash distinctly), and it\n28:    supports :ratio and :bigint as their own classes rather than excluding\n29:    them -- the merged projection's canonical-value-table declares both\n30:    out of its own domain; this dimension does not inherit that\n31:    exclusion. Host support for a class is explicit per host (see\n32:    `host-supported-scalar-classes`).\n33: \n34:    B1 does not build a decode/receive path: `image-hash`, `encode-image`,\n35:    and `encode-scalar` all go from an already-constructed Clojure value\n36:    (a hand-built canonical instruction vector, or a scalar inside one) to\n37:    its canonical bytes and hash. The 'CLJS receiver refuses a foreign\n38:    image whose wire bytes claim :double for an integral value' rule (S2)\n39:    describes a later receive/decode path this phase does not implement;\n40:    see the final report for why that is out of this file box.\"\n41:   (:require [clojure.set :as set]\n42:             [dao.jing :as jing]\n43:             [yin.vm :as vm]\n44:             [yin.vm.code :as code])\n45:   #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" ByteData])))\n46: \n47: \n48: ;; =============================================================================\n49: ;; The opcode table, derived as data from yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table\n50: ;; =============================================================================\n51: \n52: (defn- renamespace-slots\n53:   \"One mnemonic's operand slots, carried over unchanged in shape: only the\n54:    attribute is moved from `:yin.code/*` into this dimension's own\n55:    `:yin.debruijn.code/*` namespace; the kind and order are read straight\n56:    off `code/vector-operand-table`, never hand-copied.\"\n57:   [slots]\n58:   (mapv (fn [[attr kind]] [(keyword \"yin.debruijn.code\" (name attr)) kind]) slots))\n59: \n60: \n61: (def ^:private carried-mnemonics\n62:   \"Every mnemonic whose operand shape is unchanged from the named table\n63:    (S2): all of them except `:var` and `:closure`, the two lexical-\n64:    addressing mnemonics this dimension redefines below.\"\n65:   (disj code/mnemonics :var :closure))\n66: \n67: \n68: (def opcode-table\n69:   \"S2's operand table for `:yin.debruijn.code/*`, in `yin.vm.code/vector-\n70:    operand-table`'s own positional form: mnemonic -> ordered `[attribute\n71:    kind]` operands. `:var` becomes `:load-bound` (a depth and a position,\n72:    both `:uint`) or `:load-free` (a name, `:sym`); `:closure` carries an\n73:    arity (`:uint`) and a body reference (`:pc`) instead of a parameter\n74:    vector. Every other mnemonic is `renamespace-slots` applied to\n75:    `code/vector-operand-table`'s own entry, so a change to that table's\n76:    shape for a carried mnemonic is inherited here automatically rather\n77:    than silently drifting out of sync.\"\n78:   (into (into {}\n79:               (map (fn [mnemonic]\n80:                      [mnemonic (renamespace-slots (get code/vector-operand-table mnemonic))]))\n81:               carried-mnemonics)\n82:         {:load-bound [[:yin.debruijn.code/depth :uint] [:yin.debruijn.code/position :uint]],\n83:          :load-free [[:yin.debruijn.code/name :sym]],\n84:          :closure [[:yin.debruijn.code/arity :uint] [:yin.debruijn.code/body :pc]]}))\n85: \n86: \n87: (def mnemonics\n88:   (set (keys opcode-table)))\n89: \n90: \n91: ;; =============================================================================\n92: ;; The descriptor\n93: ;; =============================================================================\n94: \n95: (def scalar-classes\n96:   \"This dimension's own executable scalar domain (S2), in the fixed order\n97:    that assigns each class its tag byte below. Deliberately distinct from\n98:    `yin.vm.debruijn/canonical-value-table`: :ratio and :bigint are their\n99:    own classes here, not excluded, and :int64 is named :long (S2's own\n100:    wording) with no NFC or integral-double folding anywhere in this\n101:    table's encoding.\"\n102:   [:nil :bool :long :double :ratio :bigint :char :string :keyword :symbol\n103:    :vector :list :map :set])\n104: \n105: \n106: (def lowering-contract-version\n107:   \"Bumped whenever this dimension's opcode table, scalar table, or byte\n108:    rules change shape -- the version the descriptor hash folds in, so an\n109:    incompatible lowering never collides with an old image's H.\"\n110:   1)\n111: \n112: \n113: (def descriptor\n114:   \"The `:yin.debruijn.code/*` dimension declared as data (S2): a lowering-\n115:    contract version, arity (the opcode table's own mnemonic count), the\n116:    ordered opcode table, the canonical encoding rule, and one lift\n117:    morphism from `:yin.debruijn.code/*` to `:yin.code/*` -- declared and\n118:    exported here as a documented contract point only. The morphism's\n119:    exact shape and implementation are B2's file box; this phase names\n120:    only its target (`:yin.code/*`) here as data; its intended rule\n121:    (diagnostic binder names or synthesized names for bound positions) is\n122:    prose, kept in this docstring rather than hashed, per P3-4 of the\n123:    debruijn-b1-fix review: this whole vector feeds `descriptor-hash` (via\n124:    `encode-scalar`), so an editorial change to a hashed sentence would\n125:    fork every image's H even though `lowering-contract-version` already\n126:    guards every real shape change. `image-hash`'s own docstring carries\n127:    the H formula in prose for the same reason -- descriptor stays pure\n128:    data: keywords, version numbers, arity, slots.\"\n129:   [[:yin.debruijn.code/dimension :dim/lowering-contract-version lowering-contract-version]\n130:    [:yin.debruijn.code/dimension :dim/arity (count opcode-table)]\n131:    [:yin.debruijn.code/dimension :dim/slots opcode-table]\n132:    [:yin.debruijn.code/dimension\n133:     :dim/encoding\n134:     {:hash :sha256,\n135:      :scalar-classes scalar-classes,\n136:      :framing :tagged-and-length-delimited,\n137:      :fixed-width-kinds [:uint :pc],\n138:      :nfc false,\n139:      :integral-double-folding false,\n140:      :map-set-order :canonical-encoded-bytes}]\n141:    [:yin.debruijn.code/dimension :dim/lift-to [:yin.code/*]]])\n142: \n143: \n144: ;; =============================================================================\n145: ;; Small framing and length-prefix helpers\n146: ;; =============================================================================\n147: ;; Copied from `yin.vm.debruijn`, which documents them as meant to be\n148: ;; copied rather than imported: the width-padded hex renderer, the\n149: ;; tagged-and-length-delimited framing shape, the little-endian int64 and\n150: ;; IEEE-754 double byte rules, the UTF-8 byte-length counter, and the\n151: ;; per-host UTF-16 code-unit reader those two need. Not one private var of\n152: ;; that namespace is referenced.\n153: \n154: (def ^:private hex-digit-chars \"0123456789abcdef\")\n155: \n156: \n157: (defn- to-hex\n158:   \"Width-padded lowercase hex of the non-negative integer n.\"\n159:   [n width]\n160:   (let [base (if (zero? n)\n161:                \"0\"\n162:                (loop [n n, acc \"\"]\n163:                  (if (zero? n)\n164:                    acc\n165:                    (recur (unsigned-bit-shift-right n 4)\n166:                           (str (nth hex-digit-chars (bit-and n 0xf)) acc)))))\n167:         padded (str (apply str (repeat width \"0\")) base)]\n168:     (subs padded (- (count padded) width))))\n169: \n170: \n171: (defn- code-unit-at\n172:   [s i]\n173:   #?(:clj (int (.charAt ^String s i))\n174:      :cljs (.charCodeAt s i)\n175:      :cljd (.codeUnitAt s i)))\n176: \n177: \n178: (defn- utf8-byte-length\n179:   \"The byte length of `s` under UTF-8, counted from its code units so\n180:    every host agrees without encoding anything: a surrogate pair is one\n181:    4-byte sequence, never two 3-byte ones.\"\n182:   [s]\n183:   (let [unit-length (fn [c]\n184:                       (cond (< c 0x80) 1\n185:                             (< c 0x800) 2\n186:                             :else 3))\n187:         n (count s)]\n188:     (loop [i 0, total 0]\n189:       (if (>= i n)\n190:         total\n191:         (let [c (code-unit-at s i)]\n192:           (if (and (<= 0xD800 c 0xDBFF)\n193:                    (< (inc i) n)\n194:                    (<= 0xDC00 (code-unit-at s (inc i)) 0xDFFF))\n195:             (recur (+ i 2) (+ total 4))\n196:             (recur (inc i) (+ total (unit-length c)))))))))\n197: \n198: \n199: (def ^:private class-tag\n200:   (into {} (map-indexed (fn [i c] [c (to-hex i 2)])) scalar-classes))\n201: \n202: \n203: (defn- framed\n204:   \"One self-delimiting encoded part under this dimension's own class-tag\n205:    table: the class's tag byte, the 8-hex UTF-8 byte length of `content`,\n206:    and `content` itself.\"\n207:   [class content]\n208:   (str (get class-tag class) (to-hex (utf8-byte-length content) 8) content))\n209: \n210: \n211: (defn- int64-le-hex\n212:   \"8 bytes, two's complement, little-endian. A :cljs number -- which this\n213:    dimension only lets reach here as a safe integer, per the CLJS\n214:    classification rule below -- decomposes through floor and mod by\n215:    2^32, whose low bytes survive the host's 32-bit bit ops.\"\n216:   [v]\n217:   #?(:cljs (let [lo (mod v 4294967296)\n218:                  hi (js/Math.floor (/ v 4294967296))\n219:                  byte-at (fn [x i]\n220:                            (to-hex (bit-and (unsigned-bit-shift-right x (* i 8))\n221:                                             0xff)\n222:                                    2))]\n223:              (apply str (map #(byte-at (if (< % 4) lo hi) (mod % 4)) (range 8))))\n224:      :default (let [n (long v)]\n225:                 (apply str (map #(to-hex (bit-and (unsigned-bit-shift-right n (* % 8)) 0xff) 2)\n226:                                 (range 8))))))\n227: \n228: \n229: (defn- double-le-hex\n230:   \"IEEE-754 bits, little-endian, one quiet NaN encoding regardless of a\n231:    host's NaN payload. :clj reads the bits through doubleToLongBits,\n232:    :cljs through a DataView with littleEndian explicitly true, :cljd\n233:    through a big-endian ByteData store read in reverse.\"\n234:   [v]\n235:   (if (not= v v)\n236:     \"000000000000f87f\"\n237:     #?(:clj (int64-le-hex (Double/doubleToLongBits (double v)))\n238:        :cljs (let [view (js/DataView. (js/ArrayBuffer. 8))]\n239:                (.setFloat64 view 0 v true)\n240:                (apply str (map #(to-hex (.getUint8 view %) 2) (range 8))))\n241:        :cljd (let [bd (ByteData. 8)]\n242:                (.setFloat64 bd 0 v)\n243:                (apply str (map #(to-hex (.getUint8 bd %) 2) (range 7 -1 -1)))))))\n244: \n245: \n246: ;; =============================================================================\n247: ;; Host scalar classification (S2 item 4)\n248: ;; =============================================================================\n249: \n250: (defn- well-formed-utf16?\n251:   \"True when every surrogate in `s` is paired: the precondition every\n252:    host's UTF-8 encoder needs to agree. Matches the rule `yin.vm.debruijn/\n253:    well-formed-utf16?` enforces, independently written rather than\n254:    reused, per this phase's own instruction.\"\n255:   [s]\n256:   (let [n (count s)]\n257:     (loop [i 0]\n258:       (if (>= i n)\n259:         true\n260:         (let [c (code-unit-at s i)]\n261:           (cond\n262:             (<= 0xDC00 c 0xDFFF) false\n263:             (<= 0xD800 c 0xDBFF) (if (and (< (inc i) n)\n264:                                           (<= 0xDC00 (code-unit-at s (inc i)) 0xDFFF))\n265:                                    (recur (+ i 2))\n266:                                    false)\n267:             :else (recur (inc i))))))))\n268: \n269: \n270: (defn- ident-parts-ok?\n271:   [x]\n272:   (and (or (nil? (namespace x)) (well-formed-utf16? (namespace x)))\n273:        (well-formed-utf16? (name x))))\n274: \n275: \n276: (defn- host-char?\n277:   \"True only where the host has a character type distinct from a\n278:    one-codepoint string (S2 item 4): the JVM only. On CLJS this always\n279:    returns false -- JS has no character type at all. On ClojureDart,\n280:    `char?` does NOT reliably distinguish a genuine char from an ordinary\n281:    one-codepoint string (verified: `(dc/scalar-class \\\"a\\\")` returned\n282:    :char on Dart before this fix), so this dimension never classifies a\n283:    CLJD value as :char either, rather than trust an ambiguous host\n284:    predicate.\"\n285:   #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n286:   [v]\n287:   #?(:cljd false :cljs false :clj (char? v)))\n288: \n289: \n290: (defn- host-ratio?\n291:   \"Ratio is supported as its own class on the JVM, where `clojure.lang.\n292:    Ratio` and `ratio?` are real, unambiguous types; refused on CLJS (S2\n293:    item 4: 'JS has neither natively') and on ClojureDart, whose cljd.core\n294:    defines no `ratio?` and no ratio type at all -- unlike `yin.vm.debruijn`\n295:    and `dao.jing.cbor`, this dimension defines no project-local Rational\n296:    carrier of its own to fill that gap, so Dart falls in with CLJS here.\"\n297:   #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n298:   [v]\n299:   #?(:cljd false :cljs false :clj (ratio? v)))\n300: \n301: \n302: (defn- host-bigint?\n303:   \"Bigint is supported only where this repo already has a host-verified,\n304:    unambiguous predicate for it: `clojure.lang.BigInt` / `BigInteger` on\n305:    the JVM, distinct from `int?` (which is also true for both on the\n306:    JVM, so it cannot be used to tell them apart). `yin.vm.debruijn/\n307:    numeric-class` -- the existing repo pattern for cross-host numeric\n308:    classification -- does not attempt bigint detection on :cljd either\n309:    (its :cljd branch falls straight to double-class for anything\n310:    `int?` does not claim); this dimension matches that absence rather\n311:    than guess an unverified ClojureDart class name that could either\n312:    silently misclassify or fail to compile. A Dart or JS bigint value is\n313:    refused with :unsupported-value rather than encoded.\"\n314:   #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n315:   [v]\n316:   #?(:cljd false\n317:      :cljs false\n318:      :clj (or (instance? clojure.lang.BigInt v) (instance? java.math.BigInteger v))))\n319: \n320: \n321: (defn- host-long?\n322:   \"A JVM boxed integral type; a CLJS safe integer (S2 item 4's own\n323:    wording); a Dart native int (`int?`), the same test `yin.vm.debruijn/\n324:    numeric-class` already uses for this host.\"\n325:   [v]\n326:   #?(:clj (or (instance? Long v) (instance? Integer v) (instance? Short v) (instance? Byte v))\n327:      :cljs (and (number? v) (js/Number.isSafeInteger v))\n328:      :cljd (int? v)))\n329: \n330: \n331: (defn- host-double?\n332:   \"On the JVM, exactly `java.lang.Double` -- not 'every remaining number':\n333:    a JVM BigDecimal or Float is a distinct type this dimension's 14-class\n334:    domain does not declare, and folding either into :double would hash\n335:    its value at whatever precision `(double v)` happens to produce\n336:    instead of refusing it, exactly the silent canonicalization S2\n337:    forbids. On CLJS and CLJD, every remaining number: not a ratio, not a\n338:    bigint, not a long by this host's own rule -- CLJS has no BigDecimal/\n339:    Float distinction from a plain number to lose, so the complement rule\n340:    stays correct there.\"\n341:   [v]\n342:   #?(:clj (instance? Double v)\n343:      :default (and (number? v) (not (host-long? v)) (not (host-ratio? v)) (not (host-bigint? v)))))\n344: \n345: \n346: (defn scalar-class\n347:   \"Classify `v` under this dimension's own S2 scalar domain: the class\n348:    keyword when `v` is representable, nil when it is not. Recursive over\n349:    vectors, lists, maps, and sets -- a collection classifies only when\n350:    every element does. No normalization and no folding of any kind\n351:    happen here; classification is the identity question, not the byte\n352:    rule (`encode-scalar` owns the bytes).\"\n353:   [v]\n354:   (cond\n355:     (nil? v) :nil\n356:     (boolean? v) :bool\n357:     (host-char? v) :char\n358:     (host-ratio? v) :ratio\n359:     (host-bigint? v) :bigint\n360:     (host-long? v) :long\n361:     (host-double? v) :double\n362:     (string? v) (when (well-formed-utf16? v) :string)\n363:     (keyword? v) (when (ident-parts-ok? v) :keyword)\n364:     (symbol? v) (when (ident-parts-ok? v) :symbol)\n365:     (vector? v) (when (every? #(some? (scalar-class %)) v) :vector)\n366:     (map? v) (when (and (not (record? v))\n367:                         (every? (fn [[k x]]\n368:                                   (and (some? (scalar-class k))\n369:                                        (some? (scalar-class x))))\n370:                                 v))\n371:                :map)\n372:     (set? v) (when (every? #(some? (scalar-class %)) v) :set)\n373:     (sequential? v) (when (every? #(some? (scalar-class %)) v) :list)\n374:     :else nil))\n375: \n376: \n377: (defn scalar-value?\n378:   [v]\n379:   (some? (scalar-class v)))\n380: \n381: \n382: (def host-supported-scalar-classes\n383:   \"The scalar classes this host's encoder can represent (S2 item 4/5):\n384:    every declared class except the ones this host's own predicates above\n385:    never return. CLJS and CLJD both exclude :char, :ratio, and :bigint\n386:    (see `host-char?`, `host-ratio?`, `host-bigint?`); the JVM excludes\n387:    none.\"\n388:   (into #{}\n389:         (remove #?(:cljs #{:char :ratio :bigint} :cljd #{:char :ratio :bigint} :clj #{}))\n390:         scalar-classes))\n391: \n392: \n393: ;; =============================================================================\n394: ;; The exact executable scalar encoder (S2 item 3)\n395: ;; =============================================================================\n396: \n397: (defn- ident-content\n398:   \"Namespace and name framed separately, exactly as supplied: no NFC.\n399:    An absent namespace frames as :nil rather than an empty string, so\n400:    `sym` and `ns/sym` never collide.\"\n401:   [x]\n402:   (str (if-let [ns (namespace x)] (framed :string ns) (framed :nil \"\"))\n403:        (framed :string (name x))))\n404: \n405: \n406: (defn encode-scalar\n407:   \"This dimension's own scalar encoder (S2 item 3), deliberately distinct\n408:    from `yin.vm.debruijn/encode-value`: exact numeric bits and exact\n409:    UTF-8 bytes, no NFC, no integral-double-to-long folding, :ratio and\n410:    :bigint as their own classes, map and set entries ordered by their own\n411:    encoded bytes. A value outside `scalar-class`'s domain -- including an\n412:    unpaired UTF-16 surrogate, which `well-formed-utf16?` already refuses\n413:    at classification -- throws a qualified :unsupported-value diagnostic\n414:    naming the value; nothing is silently canonicalized.\"\n415:   [v]\n416:   (case (scalar-class v)\n417:     :nil (framed :nil \"\")\n418:     :bool (framed :bool (if v \"01\" \"00\"))\n419:     :long (framed :long (int64-le-hex v))\n420:     :double (framed :double (double-le-hex v))\n421:     ;; :ratio never arises on :cljs or :cljd (`host-ratio?` is always\n422:     ;; false on both), but `case` still compiles every branch, and neither\n423:     ;; host resolves a bare `numerator`/`denominator` var (ClojureDart has\n424:     ;; no ratio type at all; dao.jing.cbor's own Rational carrier needs\n425:     ;; its own host-specific accessor wrapper for exactly this reason) --\n426:     ;; guarded out here rather than left as an unresolved-symbol error on\n427:     ;; a branch that can't run.\n428:     :ratio #?(:cljs (framed :ratio \"\")\n429:               :cljd (framed :ratio \"\")\n430:               ;; JVM Ratio numerator/denominator are BigIntegers and can\n431:               ;; exceed int64 range (e.g. (/ 10000000000000000000N 3)); a\n432:               ;; component outside that range is refused with the same\n433:               ;; qualified diagnostic every other out-of-domain value\n434:               ;; gets, rather than an unqualified IllegalArgumentException\n435:               ;; from int64-le-hex's own (long v) cast.\n436:               :default (let [num (numerator v), den (denominator v)]\n437:                          (when-not (and (<= Long/MIN_VALUE num Long/MAX_VALUE)\n438:                                         (<= Long/MIN_VALUE den Long/MAX_VALUE))\n439:                            (throw (ex-info \"Ratio component outside int64 range in executable scalar encoding\"\n440:                                            {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v})))\n441:                          (framed :ratio (str (framed :long (int64-le-hex num))\n442:                                              (framed :long (int64-le-hex den))))))\n443:     :bigint (framed :bigint (str v))\n444:     :char (framed :char (str v))\n445:     :string (framed :string v)\n446:     :keyword (framed :keyword (ident-content v))\n447:     :symbol (framed :symbol (ident-content v))\n448:     :vector (framed :vector (apply str (map encode-scalar v)))\n449:     :list (framed :list (apply str (map encode-scalar v)))\n450:     :map (let [entries (sort (map (fn [[k x]] [(encode-scalar k) (encode-scalar x)]) v))]\n451:            (when (some (fn [[a b]] (= (first a) (first b))) (partition 2 1 entries))\n452:              (throw (ex-info \"Map keys collide in canonical executable encoding\"\n453:                              {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v})))\n454:            (framed :map (apply str (mapcat identity entries))))\n455:     :set (let [elements (sort (map encode-scalar v))]\n456:            (when (some (fn [[a b]] (= a b)) (partition 2 1 elements))\n457:              (throw (ex-info \"Set elements collide in canonical executable encoding\"\n458:                              {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v})))\n459:            (framed :set (apply str elements)))\n460:     (throw (ex-info \"Unsupported value in executable scalar encoding\"\n461:                     {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v}))))\n462: \n463: \n464: ;; =============================================================================\n465: ;; The canonical positional instruction vector's bytes, and image-hash\n466: ;; =============================================================================\n467: \n468: (def ^:private canonical-mnemonic-order\n469:   \"A fixed order for the mnemonic tag table, independent of hash-map\n470:    iteration order on any host: the mnemonic keywords sorted.\"\n471:   (vec (sort mnemonics)))\n472: \n473: \n474: (def ^:private mnemonic-tag\n475:   (into {} (map-indexed (fn [i m] [m (to-hex i 2)])) canonical-mnemonic-order))\n476: \n477: \n478: (defn- mnemonic-of\n479:   [t]\n480:   (when (and (vector? t) (seq t)) (nth t 0)))\n481: \n482: \n483: (defn- tuple-kinds\n484:   [t]\n485:   (mapv second (get opcode-table (mnemonic-of t))))\n486: \n487: \n488: (defn- encode-operand\n489:   \"One operand's bytes under its declared kind (S2 item 6): a :data/:sym/\n490:    :kw operand is a scalar under `encode-scalar` (a bare symbol or\n491:    keyword is already inside that domain); :str and :bool are framed\n492:    directly; :uint, :pc, and :buffer are fixed-width 8-byte little-endian\n493:    integers with no tag or length prefix at all -- self-delimiting by\n494:    construction, since every tuple's arity is fixed by its mnemonic, so a\n495:    framing tag would only be redundant weight.\"\n496:   [kind x]\n497:   (case kind\n498:     (:data :sym :kw) (encode-scalar x)\n499:     ;; a non-nil :str operand is a string, i.e. exactly `encode-scalar`'s\n500:     ;; :string class -- routed through it rather than framed directly, so\n501:     ;; it gets the same well-formed-utf16? guard a :const string value\n502:     ;; already gets, instead of hashing an unpaired surrogate unrefused\n503:     :str (if (nil? x) (framed :string \"\") (encode-scalar x))\n504:     :bool (framed :bool (if x \"01\" \"00\"))\n505:     (:uint :pc :buffer) (int64-le-hex (or x 0))))\n506: \n507: \n508: (defn- encode-tuple\n509:   [t]\n510:   (let [kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n511:     (apply str (get mnemonic-tag (mnemonic-of t))\n512:            (map-indexed (fn [i kind] (encode-operand kind (nth t (inc i)))) kinds))))\n513: \n514: \n515: (defn encode-image\n516:   \"The canonical positional instruction vector's own bytes (S2 item 6):\n517:    every pc-indexed tuple's bytes, in pc order, concatenated with no\n518:    outer header -- pc order and each tuple's fixed arity are the only\n519:    framing the whole vector needs.\"\n520:   [v]\n521:   (apply str (map encode-tuple v)))\n522: \n523: \n524: (def descriptor-hash\n525:   \"The descriptor's own content hash, under this dimension's own\n526:    encoder -- the same 'hash the descriptor through its own canonical\n527:    encoder' shape `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash` uses for its\n528:    descriptor, computed here with this dimension's distinct encoder\n529:    rather than that one.\"\n530:   (jing/sha256 (encode-scalar descriptor)))\n531: \n532: \n533: (defn image-hash\n534:   \"The ONE function that computes H (S2 item 6): `jing/sha256` over the\n535:    descriptor hash (which already folds in the lowering-contract\n536:    version) concatenated with the canonical instruction vector's own\n537:    bytes -- pc-indexed tuples, refs already resolved to pcs, no header,\n538:    exact scalar bytes. `jing/sha256`, not `jing/sha256-bytes`: this\n539:    dimension's canonical encoding is hex text (`encode-image` returns a\n540:    string, via `framed`/`to-hex`), the same shape `jing/sha256` hashes\n541:    for `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash`, so hashing the string directly\n542:    is the fit; `sha256-bytes` would apply to a raw byte buffer this\n543:    encoder never produces. H is the ONLY executable and sharing identity\n544:    this file computes: no projection fingerprint, no second identity.\"\n545:   [v]\n546:   (jing/sha256 (str descriptor-hash (encode-image v))))\n547: \n548: \n549: ;; =============================================================================\n550: ;; Host-boundary scalar-class support (S2 item 5)\n551: ;; =============================================================================\n552: \n553: (defn image-scalar-classes\n554:   \"Every scalar class this image uses, scanned from every :data-kind\n555:    operand across every mnemonic -- not only :const's value, but also\n556:    :store-get's and :store-put's :data operands, since those are hashed\n557:    bytes too and a scalar class hiding only there would let S2's host-\n558:    support scan (below) miss it. S2's own wording names :const alone;\n559:    scanning every :data operand is strictly more conservative than that\n560:    floor, never less, so it does not weaken the design's own rule.\"\n561:   [v]\n562:   (into #{}\n563:         (mapcat (fn [t]\n564:                   (keep-indexed (fn [i kind]\n565:                                   (when (= :data kind) (scalar-class (nth t (inc i)))))\n566:                                 (tuple-kinds t))))\n567:         v))\n568: \n569: \n570: (defn host-unsupported-classes\n571:   [v]\n572:   (set/difference (image-scalar-classes v) host-supported-scalar-classes))\n573: \n574: \n575: (defn validate-host-support\n576:   \"S2 item 5's host-boundary refusal: nil when every scalar class `v`\n577:    uses is representable on this host, else a qualified :unsupported-\n578:    value diagnostic naming the missing classes. A host-boundary outcome,\n579:    worded distinctly from a :dao.stream/gap, per the design.\"\n580:   [v]\n581:   (let [missing (host-unsupported-classes v)]\n582:     (when (seq missing)\n583:       {:rule :unsupported-value, :boundary :host, :missing-classes missing})))\n584: \n585: \n586: ;; =============================================================================\n587: ;; The validator (S2 item 7, S3)\n588: ;; =============================================================================\n589: ;; Two passes: generic structural well-formedness over this dimension's\n590: ;; own opcode table (mirroring `yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?`'s rules,\n591: ;; not its code -- that private rule vector operates over the named\n592: ;; table and is not reused), then the scope rule S3 adds: a :load-bound\n593: ;; operand must address a real enclosing frame and a real parameter\n594: ;; inside it, not merely carry two nonnegative integers.\n595: \n596: (defn- tuple-defect\n597:   [rule pc]\n598:   {:rule rule, :pc pc})\n599: \n600: \n601: (defn- nonempty\n602:   [v]\n603:   (when-not (and (vector? v) (pos? (count v))) (tuple-defect :nonempty 0)))\n604: \n605: \n606: (defn- mnemonic-rule\n607:   [v]\n608:   (some (fn [pc]\n609:           (when-not (contains? mnemonics (mnemonic-of (nth v pc)))\n610:             (tuple-defect :mnemonic pc)))\n611:         (range (count v))))\n612: \n613: \n614: (defn- arity-rule\n615:   [v]\n616:   (some (fn [pc]\n617:           (let [t (nth v pc)]\n618:             (when-not (= (inc (count (get opcode-table (mnemonic-of t)))) (count t))\n619:               (tuple-defect :arity pc))))\n620:         (range (count v))))\n621: \n622: \n623: (defn- nonneg-int?\n624:   \"A nonnegative integer, bounded to the safe-integer range on CLJS: JVM\n625:    `integer?` already excludes an unsafe-magnitude double, but CLJS's\n626:    `integer?` is true for any integral-valued JS number, including one\n627:    past 2^53 that is not exactly representable. Left unbounded there, an\n628:    image like `[:call 1e30 true]` would pass this validator on CLJS while\n629:    the same image is correctly rejected on JVM (`integer?` false for a\n630:    Double), and `int64-le-hex`'s own floor/mod decomposition would then\n631:    produce silently inexact bytes for it -- one image, valid on one host\n632:    and not another, is exactly what the sole-admitter validator exists to\n633:    prevent.\"\n634:   [x]\n635:   (and (integer? x) (not (neg? x))\n636:        #?(:cljs (js/Number.isSafeInteger x) :default true)))\n637: \n638: \n639: (def ^:private operand-kind-checks\n640:   \"Every operand kind this dimension's table uses except :pc, which\n641:    `target-bounds-rule` owns because judging it needs the vector's own\n642:    length.\"\n643:   {:data vm/plain-data?,\n644:    :sym symbol?,\n645:    :kw keyword?,\n646:    ;; a non-nil :str operand must also be well-formed UTF-16 (the same\n647:    ;; guard `encode-scalar` applies to a :string value), so a malformed\n648:    ;; :str operand is rejected here, before hashing -- not only inside\n649:    ;; `encode-operand`\n650:    :str (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (and (string? x) (well-formed-utf16? x)))),\n651:    :bool (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (boolean? x))),\n652:    :buffer (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (nonneg-int? x))),\n653:    :uint nonneg-int?})\n654: \n655: \n656: (defn- operand-kind-rule\n657:   [v]\n658:   (some (fn [pc]\n659:           (let [t (nth v pc), kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n660:             (some (fn [i]\n661:                     (let [check (get operand-kind-checks (nth kinds (dec i)))]\n662:                       (when (and check (not (check (nth t i))))\n663:                         (tuple-defect :operand-kind pc))))\n664:                   (range 1 (count t)))))\n665:         (range (count v))))\n666: \n667: \n668: (def ^:private saturated-operands\n669:   \"The `[mnemonic index]` operands a canonical tuple must materialize\n670:    even though their :operand-kind check alone would tolerate nil -- the\n671:    same set `yin.vm.code`'s own :saturation rule judges\n672:    (`code/vector-operand-table`'s saturated pairs), over this\n673:    dimension's carried :gensym, :stream-make, :call, and :ffi-call.\n674:    :ffi-call's argc (index 2) is also already rejected as nil by the\n675:    stricter `:uint nonneg-int?` kind check above, so this entry is\n676:    redundant today; it is kept anyway so this set matches the source\n677:    table's own saturated pairs exactly, rather than silently drifting if\n678:    the :uint kind check is ever relaxed.\"\n679:   #{[:gensym 1] [:stream-make 1] [:call 2] [:ffi-call 2]})\n680: \n681: \n682: (defn- saturation-rule\n683:   [v]\n684:   (some (fn [pc]\n685:           (let [t (nth v pc)]\n686:             (when (some #(and (contains? saturated-operands [(mnemonic-of t) %])\n687:                               (nil? (nth t %)))\n688:                         (range 1 (count t)))\n689:               (tuple-defect :saturation pc))))\n690:         (range (count v))))\n691: \n692: \n693: (defn- target-bounds-rule\n694:   [v]\n695:   (let [length (count v)]\n696:     (some (fn [pc]\n697:             (let [t (nth v pc), kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n698:               (some (fn [i]\n699:                       (when (= :pc (nth kinds (dec i)))\n700:                         (let [x (nth t i)]\n701:                           (when-not (and (nonneg-int? x) (<= x (dec length)))\n702:                             (tuple-defect :target-bounds pc)))))\n703:                     (range 1 (count t)))))\n704:           (range (count v)))))\n705: \n706: \n707: (defn- terminator-rule\n708:   [v]\n709:   (let [pc (dec (count v))]\n710:     (when-not (contains? code/terminators (mnemonic-of (nth v pc)))\n711:       (tuple-defect :terminator pc))))\n712: \n713: \n714: (def ^:private structural-rules\n715:   [nonempty mnemonic-rule arity-rule operand-kind-rule saturation-rule\n716:    target-bounds-rule terminator-rule])\n717: \n718: \n719: (defn well-formed-image?\n720:   \"Generic structural well-formedness of one canonical instruction vector\n721:    `v` against this dimension's own `opcode-table`: nil when `v` is well\n722:    formed, else the first defect as `{:rule r :pc p}` -- `:nonempty`,\n723:    `:mnemonic`, `:arity`, `:operand-kind`, `:saturation`, `:target-\n724:    bounds`, or `:terminator`. Malformed rows and out-of-range :pc\n725:    operands (jump/branch-false targets, closure bodies) are rejected\n726:    here; :load-bound's own scope rule is `scope-defect`, below.\"\n727:   [v]\n728:   (some #(% v) structural-rules))\n729: \n730: \n731: (defn- successors\n732:   [v pc]\n733:   (let [t (nth v pc), op (mnemonic-of t)]\n734:     (cond\n735:       (= :jump op) [(nth t 1)]\n736:       (= :branch-false op) [(nth t 1) (inc pc)]\n737:       (contains? code/terminators op) []\n738:       :else [(inc pc)])))\n739: \n740: \n741: (defn- walk-body\n742:   \"Breadth-first from `entry` over `successors`, recording every visited\n743:    pc's enclosing arity chain (innermost first) into `owner0` and\n744:    collecting the `[body-pc chain]` of every nested :closure this walk\n745:    passes through -- a :closure instruction's own body is a different\n746:    body, scheduled by the caller, never merged into this one; the walk\n747:    continues past the :closure instruction itself into the rest of this\n748:    body.\n749: \n750:    A pc this walk reaches that `owner0` already claims under a DIFFERENT\n751:    chain -- reachable through a cross-body :jump, not only through a\n752:    second :closure declaration -- stops the walk and reports that pc as\n753:    `:conflict` rather than silently keeping whichever chain got there\n754:    first: two different declared scopes for one pc is a malformed image,\n755:    not a race to resolve.\"\n756:   [v entry chain owner0]\n757:   (loop [frontier [entry], owner owner0, nested []]\n758:     (if-let [pc (first frontier)]\n759:       (cond\n760:         (or (neg? pc) (>= pc (count v)))\n761:         (recur (rest frontier) owner nested)\n762: \n763:         (contains? owner pc)\n764:         (if (= (get owner pc) chain)\n765:           (recur (rest frontier) owner nested)\n766:           {:owner owner, :nested nested, :conflict pc})\n767: \n768:         :else\n769:         (let [t (nth v pc), op (mnemonic-of t)\n770:               nested' (if (= :closure op)\n771:                         (conj nested [(nth t 2) (cons (nth t 1) chain)])\n772:                         nested)]\n773:           (recur (into (vec (rest frontier)) (successors v pc))\n774:                  (assoc owner pc chain)\n775:                  nested')))\n776:       {:owner owner, :nested nested, :conflict nil})))\n777: \n778: \n779: (defn- all-body-chains\n780:   \"pc -> enclosing arity chain (innermost first), for every pc reachable\n781:    from the image's own root body (entry 0, empty chain) or from any\n782:    :closure's declared body -- the S3 scope resolver's own data, built\n783:    once per validation rather than per :load-bound operand. A pc this\n784:    walk never reaches (dead code in a hand-built fixture) is simply\n785:    absent from the result; `scope-defect` treats that as a defect too,\n786:    since a :load-bound at an unreached pc cannot be validated at all.\n787: \n788:    Two :closure instructions -- anywhere in the image, in either order,\n789:    walked first or second -- declaring different arities for the SAME\n790:    body pc is a conflict, not a choice of which one wins: returns\n791:    `{:conflict pc}` the moment that pc's second, disagreeing chain is\n792:    reached, instead of `{:owner ...}`.\"\n793:   [v]\n794:   (loop [queue [[0 '()]], owner {}]\n795:     (if-let [[entry chain] (first queue)]\n796:       (cond\n797:         (and (contains? owner entry) (not= (get owner entry) chain))\n798:         {:conflict entry}\n799: \n800:         (contains? owner entry)\n801:         (recur (rest queue) owner)\n802: \n803:         :else\n804:         (let [{:keys [owner nested conflict]} (walk-body v entry chain owner)]\n805:           (if conflict\n806:             {:conflict conflict}\n807:             (recur (into (vec (rest queue)) nested) owner))))\n808:       {:owner owner})))\n809: \n810: \n811: (defn scope-defect\n812:   \"S3's second scope check, over and above `well-formed-image?`'s\n813:    nonnegative-shape check on every :uint operand: every :load-bound\n814:    [depth position] must address a real enclosing frame and a real\n815:    parameter inside it, walked against each body's own declared arity\n816:    and its enclosing-body chain. A shape-valid `[:load-bound [5 0]]`\n817:    pointing past a body's declared arity or enclosing chain is a defect\n818:    here even though `well-formed-image?` alone would accept it.\n819: \n820:    Two :closure instructions declaring different arities for the same\n821:    body pc -- in either declaration order, and whether reached through\n822:    ordinary body scheduling or a cross-body :jump -- are `all-body-\n823:    chains`'s own `:scope-conflict` defect, checked first: which chain\n824:    would otherwise 'win' depends on walk order, so this is rejected\n825:    before any :load-bound is even examined.\n826: \n827:    Returns the first defect as `{:rule :scope :pc p}` or\n828:    `{:rule :scope-conflict :pc p}`, or nil.\"\n829:   [v]\n830:   (let [{:keys [owner conflict]} (all-body-chains v)]\n831:     (if conflict\n832:       (tuple-defect :scope-conflict conflict)\n833:       (some (fn [pc]\n834:               (let [t (nth v pc)]\n835:                 (when (= :load-bound (mnemonic-of t))\n836:                   (let [depth (nth t 1), position (nth t 2), chain (get owner pc)]\n837:                     (when (or (nil? chain)\n838:                               (not (nonneg-int? depth))\n839:                               (not (nonneg-int? position))\n840:                               (>= depth (count chain))\n841:                               (>= position (nth chain depth)))\n842:                       (tuple-defect :scope pc))))))\n843:             (range (count v))))))\n844: \n845: \n846: (defn image-defect\n847:   \"The B1 image validator (S2, S3): the first defect in `v`, checking\n848:    generic shape first, then the scope rule S3 adds. nil when `v` is a\n849:    valid executable image on this path -- the only admitter of one; no\n850:    projected reader participates.\"\n851:   [v]\n852:   (or (well-formed-image? v) (scope-defect v)))"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_8ff3a84822444cbf8f6704eb","content":[{"type":"text","text":"src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:194:(def vector-operand-table\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:262:  (mapv second (get vector-operand-table (nth t 0))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:293:            (when-not (= (inc (count (get vector-operand-table (nth t 0))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:370:(defn well-formed-vector?\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/content.cljc:16:   `code/well-formed-vector?` (§7.5) respectively — and both verify every\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/content.cljc:120:  (when-let [defect (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/content.cljc:140:    (when-let [{:keys [rule pc]} (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:610:                                   (get code/vector-operand-table mnem)))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:727:   (when-let [defect (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:193:         (code/well-formed-vector? vector)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:211:(defn resolve-name\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:222:  ([source sym] (resolve-name source sym nil))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:8:   `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table` and section 2 of the design doc.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:19:   `:closure`'s body pc at tuple index 2. `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name`\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:41:;; read each entity's operands off `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table` with\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:46:;; already derives from `vector-operand-table`.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:87:                             (get code/vector-operand-table mnem)))))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:163:   enclosing parameter vectors (`yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name`, the one\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:191:                  resolution (debruijn/resolve-name stack name)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:222:   well formed (`yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?`) or fails the layout\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:229:         defect (code/well-formed-vector? vector)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:331:   name came from, per `resolve-name`'s own contract, so an occurrence\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:367:   from the exact parameter array `resolve-name` matched it against. When\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc:371:   image and fresh against every free name, so `resolve-name`'s\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:49:;; The opcode table, derived as data from yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:56:   off `code/vector-operand-table`, never hand-copied.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:75:   `code/vector-operand-table`'s own entry, so a change to that table's\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:80:                     [mnemonic (renamespace-slots (get code/vector-operand-table mnemonic))]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:590:;; own opcode table (mirroring `yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?`'s rules,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:672:   (`code/vector-operand-table`'s saturated pairs), over this\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:606:(defn resolve-name\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:671:                      resolution (resolve-name stack name)]"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"9ae8d782-0600-4e6c-83e8-2d5dc269d55c"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, I'll read `resolve-name` in debruijn.cljc (around lines 606–680), `vector-operand-table` in code.cljc (around lines 194–260), and `well-formed-vector?` (around lines 370–400), and check where `:push` is emitted in linearize.cljc. Also, the usage sites of `:push` in semantic.cljc — where in the named VM does it become necessary (i.e., which producers leave their value in `val` rather than pushing it?). Let me take a look at linearize.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_6d2bb3c4bcda462da8301d52","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc","offset":590,"limit":110}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_5ad9fc758d5a460db8904b03","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc","offset":180,"limit":240}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790067305698,"messageId":"f9b550c3-a318-4442-af59-a9ab80892ae8"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_6d2bb3c4bcda462da8301d52","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 110 lines\nNote: showing lines 590-699. More of the file remains. Use offset=700 to continue.\n590:                              :path path})))]\n591:     (if (and (vector? v) (every? int? v))\n592:       v\n593:       (throw (ex-info \"Unsupported slot value in AST datoms\"\n594:                       {:rule :unsupported-value,\n595:                        :slot attr,\n596:                        :kind :refs,\n597:                        :value v,\n598:                        :entity eid,\n599:                        :path path})))))\n600: \n601: \n602: ;; =============================================================================\n603: ;; D1: the scope resolver (§3)\n604: ;; =============================================================================\n605: \n606: (defn resolve-name\n607:   \"§3 scope resolution of `name` against `stack`, the ordered frame\n608:    vector stack: frame depth 0 is the innermost frame, each frame is the\n609:    source-ordered parameter vector, position is the source parameter\n610:    index with the leftmost parameter at 0. Frames search inner-to-outer;\n611:    positions search right-to-left within a frame, so duplicate\n612:    parameters are rightmost-wins exactly as `yin.vm.engine/bind-params`\n613:    binds them — (fn [x x] x) resolves to the second x, [0 1]. Returns\n614:    {:bound [frame-depth position]} or {:free name} with the symbol\n615:    preserved exactly.\"\n616:   [stack name]\n617:   (let [position-in (fn [frame]\n618:                       (first (filter (fn [p] (= name (nth frame p)))\n619:                                      (range (dec (count frame)) -1 -1))))\n620:         binding (first (keep (fn [depth]\n621:                                (when-let [p (position-in (nth stack depth))]\n622:                                  [depth p]))\n623:                              (range (count stack))))]\n624:     (if binding\n625:       {:bound binding}\n626:       {:free name})))\n627: \n628: \n629: ;; =============================================================================\n630: ;; D1: the projection walk (§2 grammar, §3 scopes, no hashing)\n631: ;; =============================================================================\n632: \n633: (declare project-node mint-record)\n634: \n635: \n636: (defn- build-node\n637:   \"Build one walked node's resolved semantic node — the §4 projected\n638:    slots, children as resolved nodes — recursing over children in the\n639:    grammar's fixed order under the scope at each occurrence. Returns\n640:    [node child-hashes acc'], child-hashes being {slot h} with an ordered\n641:    vector of hashes under :yin.debruijn/operands; `active` is the set of\n642:    eids on the current path.\"\n643:   [tag index facts scalar eid stack acc active path]\n644:   (let [;; each step threads [node child-hashes acc]\n645:         child (fn [[node refs acc] slot attr stack]\n646:                 (let [[n h acc'] (project-node index\n647:                                                (child-ref facts attr eid path)\n648:                                                stack\n649:                                                acc\n650:                                                active\n651:                                                (conj path attr))]\n652:                   [(assoc node slot n) (assoc refs slot h) acc']))\n653:         children (fn [[node refs acc] slot attr]\n654:                    (let [[ns hs acc']\n655:                          (reduce (fn [[ns hs a] ref]\n656:                                    (let [[n h a'] (project-node index\n657:                                                                 ref\n658:                                                                 stack\n659:                                                                 a\n660:                                                                 active\n661:                                                                 (conj path attr))]\n662:                                      [(conj ns n) (conj hs h) a']))\n663:                                  [[] [] acc]\n664:                                  (child-refs facts attr eid path))]\n665:                      [(assoc node slot ns) (assoc refs slot hs) acc']))\n666:         leaf (fn [node] [node {} acc])]\n667:     (case tag\n668:       :literal (leaf {:yin.debruijn/type :literal,\n669:                       :yin.debruijn/value (get scalar :yin/value)})\n670:       :variable (let [name (get scalar :yin/name)\n671:                       resolution (resolve-name stack name)]\n672:                   (leaf (if-let [bound (:bound resolution)]\n673:                           {:yin.debruijn/type :variable,\n674:                            :yin.debruijn/bound bound}\n675:                           {:yin.debruijn/type :variable,\n676:                            :yin.debruijn/free (:free resolution)})))\n677:       :lambda (let [params (get scalar :yin/params)]\n678:                 (child (leaf (merge {:yin.debruijn/type :lambda,\n679:                                      :yin.debruijn/arity (count params)}\n680:                                     ;; truthy only: an explicit false and an\n681:                                     ;; absent :yin/macro? project identically\n682:                                     (when (get scalar :yin/macro?)\n683:                                       {:yin.debruijn/macro? (get scalar :yin/macro?)})))\n684:                        :yin.debruijn/body\n685:                        :yin/body\n686:                        (into [params] stack)))\n687:       :application (let [operator-eid (child-ref facts :yin/operator eid path)]\n688:                      (check-unexpanded index eid operator-eid path)\n689:                      (-> (leaf {:yin.debruijn/type :application})\n690:                          (child :yin.debruijn/operator :yin/operator stack)\n691:                          (children :yin.debruijn/operands :yin/operands)))\n692:       :dao.stream.apply/call (children (leaf {:yin.debruijn/type :dao.stream.apply/call,\n693:                                               :yin.debruijn/op (get scalar :yin/op)})\n694:                                        :yin.debruijn/operands\n695:                                        :yin/operands)\n696:       :if (-> (leaf {:yin.debruijn/type :if})\n697:               (child :yin.debruijn/test :yin/test stack)\n698:               (child :yin.debruijn/consequent :yin/consequent stack)\n699:               (child :yin.debruijn/alternate :yin/alternate stack))"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_5ad9fc758d5a460db8904b03","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 234 lines\n180:                                      (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %)\n181:                                            structural-attrs)))\n182:                               order)\n183:         batch {:attrs attrs,\n184:                :segments segments,\n185:                :seg (first segments),\n186:                :instructions instructions}]\n187:     (some #(% batch) rules)))\n188: \n189: \n190: ;; =============================================================================\n191: ;; The canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2, §7.5)\n192: ;; =============================================================================\n193: \n194: (def vector-operand-table\n195:   \"§2.4's operand table in UCF §7.3.2's positional form: mnemonic → ordered\n196:    `[attribute kind]` operands. Position `i` of a canonical tuple (from 1)\n197:    holds the operand entry `i-1` names; this one table drives the §7.5\n198:    validator's rules. Each kind names the\n199:    rule that judges it: `:data`, `:sym`, `:syms`, `:kw`, `:str`, `:bool`,\n200:    and `:buffer` belong to `:operand-kind` (a nil passes it and is\n201:    `:saturation`'s defect); `:pc` to `:target-bounds`; `:uint` to `:argc`.\"\n202:   {:const [[:yin.code/value :data]],\n203:    :var [[:yin.code/name :sym]],\n204:    :closure [[:yin.code/params :syms] [:yin.code/body :pc]],\n205:    :push [],\n206:    :call [[:yin.code/argc :uint] [:yin.code/tail? :bool]],\n207:    :return [],\n208:    :jump [[:yin.code/target :pc]],\n209:    :branch-false [[:yin.code/target :pc]],\n210:    :halt [],\n211:    :gensym [[:yin.code/prefix :str]],\n212:    :store-get [[:yin.code/key :data]],\n213:    :store-put [[:yin.code/key :data] [:yin.code/value :data]],\n214:    :stream-make [[:yin.code/buffer :buffer]],\n215:    :stream-put [],\n216:    :stream-cursor [],\n217:    :stream-next [],\n218:    :stream-close [],\n219:    :park [],\n220:    :resume [[:yin.code/parked-id :kw]],\n221:    :current-continuation [],\n222:    :ffi-call [[:yin.code/ffi-op :kw] [:yin.code/argc :uint]]})\n223: \n224: \n225: (def ^:private operand-kind-checks\n226:   \"§7.5 :operand-kind — kind → predicate. Absent kinds (`:pc`, `:uint`) are\n227:    not judged by the rule: `:target-bounds` and `:argc` own them. A nil in\n228:    a saturated slot passes here; `:saturation` owns it, the §7.4 `:slot-kind`\n229:    precedent.\"\n230:   {:data vm/plain-data?,\n231:    :sym symbol?,\n232:    :syms (fn [x] (and (vector? x) (every? symbol? x))),\n233:    :kw keyword?,\n234:    :str (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (string? x))),\n235:    :bool (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (boolean? x))),\n236:    :buffer (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (count? x)))})\n237: \n238: \n239: (def ^:private saturated-operands\n240:   \"§7.5 :saturation — the `[mnemonic index]` operands a canonical tuple\n241:    materializes (UCF §7.3.2); a nil in one of them is that rule's defect.\"\n242:   #{[:gensym 1] [:stream-make 1] [:call 2] [:ffi-call 2]})\n243: \n244: \n245: (defn- tuple-defect\n246:   [rule pc]\n247:   {:rule rule, :pc pc})\n248: \n249: \n250: (defn- mnemonic-of\n251:   \"The mnemonic of one tuple, or nil when the element has no first element\n252:    that could be one. A tuple is a vector — UCF §7.3.2's positional form —\n253:    so a list element has no mnemonic.\"\n254:   [t]\n255:   (when (and (vector? t) (seq t))\n256:     (nth t 0)))\n257: \n258: \n259: (defn- tuple-kinds\n260:   \"The operand kinds of one tuple's mnemonic, in tuple order.\"\n261:   [t]\n262:   (mapv second (get vector-operand-table (nth t 0))))\n263: \n264: \n265: ;; Each rule below takes the whole vector and returns a defect or nil. Rules\n266: ;; run in §7.5's order and the first defect wins, so a rule may assume every\n267: ;; earlier one held — exactly as `rules` above runs over a batch.\n268: \n269: (defn- nonempty\n270:   \"Rule 1. An empty vector names pc 0, where the first instruction belongs;\n271:    so does anything a pc cannot index: the canonical form is a vector\n272:    (UCF §7.3.2), so a list outer is not the form at any length.\"\n273:   [v]\n274:   (when-not (and (vector? v) (pos? (count v)))\n275:     (tuple-defect :nonempty 0)))\n276: \n277: \n278: (defn- mnemonic\n279:   \"Rule 2.\"\n280:   [v]\n281:   (some (fn [pc]\n282:           (when-not (contains? mnemonics (mnemonic-of (nth v pc)))\n283:             (tuple-defect :mnemonic pc)))\n284:         (range (count v))))\n285: \n286: \n287: (defn- arity\n288:   \"Rule 3. §2.4's operand table as saturated and made positional by UCF\n289:    §7.3.2: the mnemonic plus exactly its operands.\"\n290:   [v]\n291:   (some (fn [pc]\n292:           (let [t (nth v pc)]\n293:             (when-not (= (inc (count (get vector-operand-table (nth t 0))))\n294:                          (count t))\n295:               (tuple-defect :arity pc))))\n296:         (range (count v))))\n297: \n298: \n299: (defn- operand-kind\n300:   \"Rule 4. An operand is judged only by the kind §7.5 gives it.\"\n301:   [v]\n302:   (some (fn [pc]\n303:           (let [t (nth v pc)\n304:                 kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n305:             (some (fn [i]\n306:                     (let [check (get operand-kind-checks (nth kinds (dec i)))]\n307:                       (when (and check (not (check (nth t i))))\n308:                         (tuple-defect :operand-kind pc))))\n309:                   (range 1 (count t)))))\n310:         (range (count v))))\n311: \n312: \n313: (defn- saturation\n314:   \"Rule 5. A saturated operand is materialized in a canonical tuple; a nil\n315:    one names its pc.\"\n316:   [v]\n317:   (some (fn [pc]\n318:           (let [t (nth v pc)]\n319:             (when (some #(and (contains? saturated-operands [(nth t 0) %])\n320:                               (nil? (nth t %)))\n321:                         (range 1 (count t)))\n322:               (tuple-defect :saturation pc))))\n323:         (range (count v))))\n324: \n325: \n326: (defn- target-bounds\n327:   \"Rule 6. A resolved pc must be an integer inside the segment.\"\n328:   [v]\n329:   (let [length (count v)]\n330:     (some (fn [pc]\n331:             (let [t (nth v pc)\n332:                   kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n333:               (some (fn [i]\n334:                       (when (= :pc (nth kinds (dec i)))\n335:                         (let [x (nth t i)]\n336:                           (when-not (and (integer? x) (<= 0 x (dec length)))\n337:                             (tuple-defect :target-bounds pc)))))\n338:                     (range 1 (count t)))))\n339:           (range (count v)))))\n340: \n341: \n342: (defn- terminator\n343:   \"Rule 7. Nothing labelled follows pc length-1, so the one instruction\n344:    that can violate the rule is the last.\"\n345:   [v]\n346:   (let [pc (dec (count v))]\n347:     (when-not (contains? terminators (mnemonic-of (nth v pc)))\n348:       (tuple-defect :terminator pc))))\n349: \n350: \n351: (defn- argc\n352:   \"Rule 8. The :uint operands of `:call` and `:ffi-call`.\"\n353:   [v]\n354:   (some (fn [pc]\n355:           (let [t (nth v pc)\n356:                 kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n357:             (some (fn [i]\n358:                     (when (= :uint (nth kinds (dec i)))\n359:                       (when-not (count? (nth t i))\n360:                         (tuple-defect :argc pc))))\n361:                   (range 1 (count t)))))\n362:         (range (count v))))\n363: \n364: \n365: (def ^:private vector-rules\n366:   [nonempty mnemonic arity operand-kind saturation target-bounds terminator\n367:    argc])\n368: \n369: \n370: (defn well-formed-vector?\n371:   \"Check one canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2) against §7.5\n372:    (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`).\n373: \n374:    Returns nil when the vector is well formed, else the first defect as\n375:    `{:rule r :pc p}` — `:nonempty`, `:mnemonic`, `:arity`,\n376:    `:operand-kind`, `:saturation`, `:target-bounds`, `:terminator`, or\n377:    `:argc` — with p the offending pc (`:nonempty` names pc 0, where the\n378:    missing first instruction belongs). Rules run in §7.5's order, each\n379:    assuming the earlier ones held. §2.6 rules 1–4 are satisfied by the\n380:    vector form by construction (pc is the index); rules 5–6 and the UCF\n381:    §7.3.4 checks are the eight here. The address check — a vector hashing\n382:    to the address it claims — is not a structural rule and is not made\n383:    here: a correct hash is never structural validation.\"\n384:   [v]\n385:   (some #(% v) vector-rules))\n386: \n387: \n388: ;; =============================================================================\n389: ;; Segment rows (§6.2)\n390: ;; =============================================================================\n391: \n392: (defn project-segment\n393:   \"§6.2 (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`): the canonical instruction\n394:    vector with pc prepended — one `[pc tag & ops]` row per instruction, the\n395:    tag being the mnemonic. The relation is per segment: pcs collide across\n396:    segments, and source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a tuple\n397:    slot, so bare-pc rows from different segments are never unioned into one\n398:    relation. A query over several segments takes them as separate sources\n399:    or uses the segment-qualified form (`project-segment-qualified`).\"\n400:   [v]\n401:   (mapv (fn [pc t] (into [pc] t)) (range) v))\n402: \n403: \n404: (defn project-segment-qualified\n405:   \"§6.2's segment-qualified form `[segment-address pc tag & ops]`: one\n406:    address column prepended, so every tag's arity gains one uniformly and\n407:    per-tag arity stays fixed. The address is the vector's own\n408:    `(jing/segment-key v)` — the §7.1/UCF §7.3.2 rule the loaders already\n409:    follow — computed here rather than claimed by a caller, so a segment's\n410:    rows are named by the content they are projected from.\"\n411:   [v]\n412:   (let [addr (jing/segment-key v)]\n413:     (mapv (fn [r] (into [addr] r)) (project-segment v))))"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"e8c5fb64-e1cf-4912-9ee1-de1fae065d26"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next I need to check linearize.cljc — where `:push` gets emitted, and what the lowering outputs (which instructions get emitted for which AST nodes). This is important for the validity analysis of the `:push` no-op fix.\n\nAlso, I should read the interpreter loop in semantic.cljc more carefully to understand when `:push` is emitted relative to value-producing instructions, and whether `:push` appears anywhere not immediately after a value-producing instruction (e.g., after `:call`? after `:store-put`? after `:stream-next`? after `:resume`?).\n\nKey question about the `:push` fix: in the stack VM, every value producer conj's onto the stack. In the named VM, producers set `val`, and `:push` commits `val` to St. If linearize emits `:push` after every producing instruction that needs to be on the stack, then the no-op in the stack VM is fine as long as the value-producing instructions of the stack VM correspond 1-to-1 with the producers of the named VM. However, there's a subtle point: in the named VM, what does `:call` do? Let me check — in the named VM, `:call` probably pops argc arguments from St, applies, and puts the result in `val`. In the stack VM, `:call` pops from the stack and conj's the result. So a `:push` after `:call` is a no-op — consistent.\n\nBut what about instructions like `:stream-next` or `:resume` that produce values in the named VM (setting `val`) but aren't implemented in the stack VM (B3)? B4 will implement them, and at that point `:push` is already a no-op, so the B4 implementation must conj directly onto the stack. That's a forward-looking design constraint, not a bug at this point in time. Is it worth noting as a P3/P2 comment? The `:push` no-op means that future producers must always conj onto `:stack` — the comment documents that.\n\nAnother important check: does the named VM's `:branch-false` use `val` rather than St? Yes: `7 (recur seg (if val (inc pc) (nth inst 1)) ...)` — branch tests `val`, not the top of the stack! Meanwhile, the stack VM's `:branch-false` peeks the stack and pops. Hmm, wait — in the named VM, the test value: `if val then pc+1 else target`. Does it consume val? It keeps `val` in the register. In the stack VM, `:branch-false` pops the value. But how does linearize emit if? Let's see: the named lowering of `if` probably emits: <test code> (ends by putting the test result in val), then `:branch-false alt`. In the named VM the test result is in `val`. In the stack VM, the producer of the test code conj'd onto the stack, and `:branch-false` peeks and pops. OK — but does the named VM emit `:push` before `:branch-false`? If it did, then in the named VM the value would be both in val and on St… I need to read linearize.cljc.\n\nAlso the stack VM's `:return`: peeks the stack (doesn't pop before truncating to stack-base — truncation handles it). No problem.\n\nAlso `:halt`: the named VM halts with `val` as the result. Stack VM's `:halt` sets status; `value` = peek stack. If linearize emits `<producer> :halt`, then the stack VM's peek returns the produced value. OK.\n\nWait — but there's a critical issue: in the named VM, `:call` — how are arguments passed? Does linearize emit `:push` after each operand producer to commit val onto St, and then `:call argc tail?` reads f and arguments from St? Let me read the call case in semantic.cljc and linearize.cljc.\n\nAnother potential issue: in the named VM, does `:call` put the result in `val`, or on St? Then does a subsequent `:push` commit it. In the stack VM, `:call` conj's the result onto stack', and `:push` is a no-op. Consistent.\n\nBut what about a `:push` emitted where the value in `val` was produced by an instruction that in the stack VM doesn't conj onto the stack? For example: `:resume` in the named VM puts the resume value into val; a `:push` commits it. Not implemented in B3. In B4 it must conj. Not a problem.\n\nWhat about `:store-put`? The stack VM conj's the value onto the stack and stores it. In the named VM, `:store-put` probably sets val ← value, and a subsequent `:push` commits it. Consistent.\n\nSo next, let me read the relevant parts of linearize.cljc and semantic.cljc (call, return, branch, store-put, halt).\n\nI've finished reading B1 and the auxiliary files. Next, I need to check how `linearize/lower` emits `:push` (to verify the validity of the no-op fix), as well as the `:call`/`:return`/`:store-put` cases in semantic.cljc.\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_9d25065f56504a6e99c85c62","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":":push|push","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"-C":6}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790067345572,"messageId":"3c257a76-2fe9-4be3-a88d-f408f0e35e53"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_9d25065f56504a6e99c85c62","content":[{"type":"text","text":"src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-87-                       (swap! bodies conj [l e (get-attr e :yin/body)])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-88-                       (emit! e :closure\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-89-                              :yin.code/params (get-attr e :yin/params)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-90-                              :yin.code/body l))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-91-             :application (let [operands (get-attr e :yin/operands)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-92-                            (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/operator))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:93:                            (emit! e :push)\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-94-                            (doseq [o operands]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-95-                              (lower-node o)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:96:                              (emit! e :push))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-97-                            (emit! e :call\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-98-                                   :yin.code/argc (count operands)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-99-                                   :yin.code/tail? (boolean\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-100-                                                     (get-attr e :yin/tail?))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-101-             :if (let [else (fresh!)\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-102-                       end (fresh!)]\n--\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-107-                   (mark! else)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-108-                   (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/alternate))\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-109-                   (mark! end))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-110-             :dao.stream.apply/call (let [operands (get-attr e :yin/operands)]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-111-                                      (doseq [o operands]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-112-                                        (lower-node o)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:113:                                        (emit! e :push))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-114-                                      (emit! e :ffi-call\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-115-                                             :yin.code/ffi-op (get-attr e :yin/op)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-116-                                             :yin.code/argc (count operands)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-117-             :stream/make (emit! e :stream-make\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-118-                                 :yin.code/buffer (get-attr e :yin/buffer))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-119-             :stream/put (do (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/target))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:120:                             (emit! e :push)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-121-                             (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/val-node))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-122-                             (emit! e :stream-put))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-123-             :stream/cursor (do (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/source))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-124-                                (emit! e :stream-cursor))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-125-             :stream/next (do (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/source))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-126-                              (emit! e :stream-next))\n--\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-264-             :variable (emit! path [:var (nth slots 0)])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-265-             :lambda (let [l (fresh!)]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-266-                       (swap! bodies conj [l (nth slots 1) path])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-267-                       (emit! path [:closure (nth slots 0) l]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-268-             :application (let [operands (nth slots 1)]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-269-                            (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:270:                            (emit! path [:push])\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-271-                            (dotimes [j (count operands)]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-272-                              (lower-node (nth operands j) (conj path [3 j]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:273:                              (emit! path [:push]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-274-                            (emit! path [:call (count operands) (nth slots 2)]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-275-             :if (let [else (fresh!)\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-276-                       end (fresh!)]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-277-                   (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-278-                   (emit! path [:branch-false else])\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-279-                   (lower-node (nth slots 1) (conj path 3))\n--\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-282-                   (lower-node (nth slots 2) (conj path 4))\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-283-                   (mark! end))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-284-             :dao.stream.apply/call (let [operands (nth slots 1)]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-285-                                      (dotimes [j (count operands)]\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-286-                                        (lower-node (nth operands j)\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-287-                                                    (conj path [3 j]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:288:                                        (emit! path [:push]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-289-                                      (emit! path [:ffi-call (nth slots 0)\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-290-                                                   (count operands)]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-291-             :stream/make (emit! path [:stream-make (nth slots 0)])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-292-             :stream/put (do (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:293:                             (emit! path [:push])\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-294-                             (lower-node (nth slots 1) (conj path 3))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-295-                             (emit! path [:stream-put]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-296-             :stream/cursor (do (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-297-                                (emit! path [:stream-cursor]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-298-             :stream/next (do (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc-299-                              (emit! path [:stream-next]))"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"1d107faa-20db-4132-a769-817889d168ee"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`:push` is emitted right after lowering each value-producing subnode: after the operator and each operand of `:application`, after each operand of `:dao.stream.apply/call`, and after the target of `:stream/put`. So `:push` always follows immediately after a producing instruction sequence whose last instruction leaves the produced value in `val` (named VM) / on the top of the stack (stack VM).\n\nBut wait: subnodes could be `:if` nodes. `(if t a b)` as an operand of application: lower-node(if) produces code ending in a branch that leaves the result in `val` — and then `:push` commits it. In the stack VM, the if code ends with each branch's producer having conj'd onto the stack; `:push` is a no-op. Consistent.\n\nWhat about subnodes like `:stream/put`? In the named VM, does `:stream-put` set val? Probably yes (val ← stream-put's result). In the stack VM's B3, `:stream-put` is unimplemented (B4). But if `:push` after `:stream-put` is a no-op, then B4's implementation will have to conj onto the stack directly. That's fine, already documented in the comments.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue with the stack VM's `:branch-false`: in the named VM, branch tests `val` and doesn't consume from St (since test's producer leaves the result in val, and lowering of `:if` doesn't emit `:push` after the test, so test never got pushed onto St). In the stack VM, test's producer conj'd onto the stack, and `:branch-false` peeks and pops. Consistent.\n\nNow, the critical verification about the `:push` no-op: is there anywhere the named VM produces a value into `val` without a corresponding stack VM conj? Consider the named VM's `:call` — need to check: does it put the result into val, or somewhere else? Let me read the call case (opcode 5?) and return in semantic.cljc. Also `:store-put` — the named VM sets val ← value, and probably further writes to the store; stack VM's `:store-put` conjs value onto the stack and writes to the store. Consistent.\n\nWhat about `:return` in the named VM? And `:halt` — halts with val as result; in the stack VM: halts, value = peek of the stack. When the program is `<producer> :halt`, named's val = produced value, stack's peek = produced value (because producer conj'd). But wait — in the named VM, `:halt` uses `val`; in the stack VM, the last producer conj'd onto the stack. But consider a program ending in `:return` with an empty continuation: the stack VM sets `:stack [val]` and halts. OK.\n\nHmm — one subtle case: named VM's `:call` — does it leave the result in val, or push it onto St? Let me read semantic.cljc around the call/return/halt cases, and also the places where St is used.\n\nAnother potential issue: `:branch-false` — in the named VM, does the test value get consumed from St? It only reads `val`, and St is unchanged. In the stack VM, it pops the stack. Since the test was never pushed onto St in the named flow (no `:push` after the test), stack discipline matches: the stack VM's producers push, branch pops; the named version's producers set val, branch reads val. The stack VM's St corresponds to the named version's St, plus the extra top element corresponding to the named version's val? Actually no — the invariant is: stack VM's `:stack` = named's `St ++ [val]` when val is \"pending\" (produced but not yet pushed). And `:push` in the named version commits val: St++[val], val unchanged. In the stack VM: the producer has already put it there; `:push` is a no-op. Invariant maintained.\n\nWhere does the named version read St? `:call` pops argc+1 from St. At that point, in the named flow, all operator and operands have been `:push`ed, so St has f and args on top. In the stack VM, since producers conj'd directly and pushes were no-ops, the stack has the same elements.\n\nBut wait: the named version's `:call` — after applying, where does the result go? Into val, probably. In the stack VM, it conjs the result onto stack'. Then a subsequent `:push` (if call is a subnode of another application) is a no-op, but in the named version val gets committed. Consistent.\n\nNow, `:return` in the named version: probably takes val, pops the continuation, restores St truncated to stack-base and… does it push val onto St? Let me check the return case in semantic.cljc. In the stack VM: `:return` peeks the stack (result), truncates to stack-base, and conjs val. When a body ends with a tail call `[:call n true] [:return]`: tail call in the stack VM — continuation isn't extended, pc jumps to the body. The inner body's `:return` pops the continuation frame — which is the caller of the tail-caller (correct TCO). Frames are restored from the continuation record. But note: for tail calls, the current frame's `:frames` are… when the tail-called body returns, `:return` restores `(:frames frame)` from the continuation record — the caller's frames. Good.\n\nBut subtle issue with tail calls in the stack VM: `:stack-base` is recorded on the continuation at non-tail calls. For a tail call, the stack is truncated to stack' (removing f and args) but no new continuation entry is created; the inner body executes with the caller's continuation. When the body returns, the stack is truncated to the caller's continuation frame's stack-base and val is conj'd. Correct.\n\nNow, does the named VM handle `:return` the same way? I should read the call/return in semantic.cljc to verify consistency, especially whether the named version's `:call` leaves the result in `val`, and whether the named version's `:return` uses val. Also opcode 23 `:halt` halts with val.\n\nPotential issue with `:halt` in the stack VM: at the top level, after `<producer> :halt`, value = peek stack = produced value. But consider a program ending with `:call n false :halt` at the top level: call conjs the result, halt, peek = result. Good. And `:halt` in a body…? The root of the main sequence ends with `:halt`. Bodies end with `:return`.\n\nNow — the key question the brief asks: could `:push` ever be needed to move a value from another register? In the named VM, val is the only producer register. `:push` is emitted only right after lowering a producing node. In the stack VM, all producers (const, load-bound, load-free, closure, call, store-get, store-put) conj directly. B4 producers (stream operations, ffi-call, gensym, resume, current-continuation) will also have to conj directly — the comments establish that convention. Since stack.cljc throws `:not-yet-implemented` for those opcodes, there's no path where `:push` becomes a silent no-op after a value was produced into a different register.\n\nOne more check: does the named VM have any `:push` that isn't preceded by a producer — for example, stream/put emits `:push` after lowering the target. What does the named version's `:stream-put` consume… In stack VM B3, stream-put is unimplemented. Fine.\n\nWait, let me actually check linearize lines 119-122: `:stream/put` lowers the target, `:push`, lowers the val-node, `:stream-put`. In the named VM: target produced into val, `:push` commits to St; val-node produced into val; `:stream-put` reads target from St and value from val. In the (future) stack VM: target conj'd, `:push` is no-op, val-node conj'd, `:stream-put` pops both. Consistent as long as B4's `:stream-put` pops value then target. Not a current defect.\n\nOK, let me read the step in semantic.cljc around the call/return/halt cases to confirm the named version's `:call` result placement.\n\nThe output location of `:push` has been confirmed — it's always immediately after the lowered producer node. Next, in semantic.cljc, I'll verify the placement of results for the named VM's `:call`/`:return`/`:halt`, and check the overall context of the interpreter loop.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_f55ef1f123ad46eb8bd40d04","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc","offset":180,"limit":200}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790067413740,"messageId":"378a6d4a-0d20-4a98-8472-e2adfb1496c2"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_f55ef1f123ad46eb8bd40d04","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 200 lines\nNote: showing lines 180-379. More of the file remains. Use offset=380 to continue.\n180: \n181:    No `:restore-fn` crosses this boundary. A parked entry is pure data and\n182:    the scheduler below resumes it explicitly, so nothing live is attached to\n183:    the wait set (§1.1).\"\n184:   [vm seg pc val St E K effect park-entry-fns]\n185:   (let [{:keys [state value blocked?]}\n186:         (engine/handle-effect (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n187:                               effect\n188:                               (when park-entry-fns\n189:                                 {:park-entry-fns park-entry-fns}))]\n190:     (if blocked?\n191:       {:stop (assoc state :control nil :k nil)}\n192:       {:continue [state value]})))\n193: \n194: \n195: (defn- apply-call\n196:   \"The §4.2 call transition, with the arguments already isolated from the\n197:    operand stack. Returns `{:goto [seg pc val St E K vm image]}` to continue,\n198:    `{:stop vm}` when the call blocked, or throws for a non-function.\n199: \n200:    A tail call grows neither K nor St: the frame the caller would have pushed\n201:    is the frame its own caller gave it. A primitive that yields an effect\n202:    parks with the continuation after the call site.\"\n203:   [code seg pc val St E K vm tail? f args]\n204:   (cond\n205:     (= :closure (:type f))\n206:     (let [E' (merge (:env f) (engine/bind-params (:params f) args))\n207:           frame {:type :return, :segment seg, :pc (inc pc), :env E,\n208:                  :stack-base (count St)}\n209:           seg' (:segment f)]\n210:       {:goto [seg' (:entry f) val St E' (if tail? K (conj K frame))\n211:               vm (get code seg')]})\n212:     (fn? f)\n213:     (let [result (apply f args)]\n214:       (if (module/effect? result)\n215:         (let [{:keys [state value blocked?]}\n216:               (engine/handle-effect\n217:                 (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n218:                 result\n219:                 {:park-entry-fns (call-park-entries seg pc E St K)})]\n220:           (if blocked?\n221:             {:stop (assoc state :control nil :k nil)}\n222:             {:goto [seg (inc pc) value St E K state (get code seg)]}))\n223:         {:goto [seg (inc pc) result St E K vm (get code seg)]}))\n224:     :else (throw (ex-info \"Cannot apply non-function\" {:fn f}))))\n225: \n226: \n227: ;; =============================================================================\n228: ;; The hot loop\n229: ;; =============================================================================\n230: \n231: (defn- vm-hot\n232:   \"Run loaded work with the registers in loop locals.\n233: \n234:    `fuel`, when non-nil, is a number of instructions to execute before\n235:    materializing: `step` supplies 1 and `run` supplies nil. The loop exits by\n236:    returning a record when the machine halts, parks, blocks on an effect, or\n237:    runs out of fuel; every non-exit transition is one `case` arm and one\n238:    operation on locals (§4.3). A machine with no control and no continuation\n239:    is between evaluations, so one scheduler round is run instead.\"\n240:   [vm fuel]\n241:   (if (and (nil? (:control vm)) (nil? (:k vm)))\n242:     (scheduler-round vm)\n243:     (let [code (:code vm)\n244:           {:keys [segment pc]} (:control vm)]\n245:       (loop [seg segment\n246:              pc pc\n247:              val (:value vm)\n248:              St (or (:stack vm) [])\n249:              E (:env vm)\n250:              K (or (:k vm) [])\n251:              vm vm\n252:              image (get code segment)\n253:              fuel fuel]\n254:         (if (and fuel (zero? fuel))\n255:           (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n256:           (let [inst (nth (:code image) pc)\n257:                 op (nth inst 0)]\n258:             (case op\n259:               ;; :const — val ← literal (opcode 1, :literal)\n260:               1 (recur seg (inc pc) (nth inst 1) St E K vm image\n261:                        (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n262:               ;; :var — val ← resolve(E, S, prims, modules, name) (2, :load-var)\n263:               2 (recur seg (inc pc)\n264:                        (engine/resolve-var E (:store vm) (:primitives vm)\n265:                                            (:modules vm) (nth inst 1))\n266:                        St E K vm image (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n267:               ;; :closure — val ← clo(params, entry, seg, E) (4, :lambda)\n268:               4 (recur seg (inc pc)\n269:                        {:type :closure,\n270:                         :params (nth inst 1),\n271:                         :entry (nth inst 2),\n272:                         :segment seg,\n273:                         :env E}\n274:                        St E K vm image (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n275:               ;; :push — St ← St ⧺ [val] (22, :push)\n276:               22 (recur seg (inc pc) val (conj St val) E K vm image\n277:                         (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n278:               ;; :jump — pc ← target (8, :jump)\n279:               8 (recur seg (nth inst 1) val St E K vm image\n280:                        (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n281:               ;; :branch-false — pc ← val ? pc+1 : target (7, :branch)\n282:               7 (recur seg (if val (inc pc) (nth inst 1)) val St E K vm image\n283:                        (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n284:               ;; :halt — halt with val as the result (23, :halt)\n285:               23 (put-registers vm nil nil val [] E nil)\n286:               ;; :return — pop a frame, or halt on an empty K (6, :return)\n287:               6 (if-let [frame (peek K)]\n288:                   (recur (:segment frame) (:pc frame) val\n289:                          (subvec St 0 (:stack-base frame))\n290:                          (:env frame) (pop K)\n291:                          vm (get (:code vm) (:segment frame))\n292:                          (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n293:                   (put-registers vm nil nil val [] E nil))\n294:               ;; :gensym — val ← fresh id; counter advances (9, :gensym)\n295:               9 (let [[id vm'] (engine/gensym vm (nth inst 1))]\n296:                   (recur seg (inc pc) id St E K vm' image\n297:                          (and fuel (dec fuel))))\n298:               ;; :store-get — val ← S[key] (10, :store-get)\n299:               10 (recur seg (inc pc) (get (:store vm) (nth inst 1))\n300:                         St E K vm image (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n301:               ;; :store-put — S[key] ← v; val ← v (11, :store-put)\n302:               11 (let [vm' (assoc vm :store (assoc (:store vm)\n303:                                                    (nth inst 1)\n304:                                                    (nth inst 2)))]\n305:                    (recur seg (inc pc) (nth inst 2) St E K vm' image\n306:                           (and fuel (dec fuel))))\n307:               ;; :current-continuation — val ← {seg, pc+1, E, St, K} (19)\n308:               19 (recur seg (inc pc)\n309:                         {:type :reified-continuation,\n310:                          :segment seg,\n311:                          :pc (inc pc),\n312:                          :env E,\n313:                          :stack St,\n314:                          :k K}\n315:                         St E K vm image (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n316:               ;; :park — write {segment pc+1 env stack k} and halt (17)\n317:               17 (-> (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n318:                      (engine/park-continuation {:segment seg,\n319:                                                 :pc (inc pc),\n320:                                                 :env E,\n321:                                                 :stack St,\n322:                                                 :k K})\n323:                      (assoc :control nil :k nil))\n324:               ;; :resume — restore the parked configuration with val (18)\n325:               18 (let [vm' (engine/resume-continuation\n326:                              (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n327:                              (nth inst 1)\n328:                              val\n329:                              (fn [base parked resume-val]\n330:                                (put-registers base\n331:                                               (:segment parked)\n332:                                               (:pc parked)\n333:                                               resume-val\n334:                                               (:stack parked)\n335:                                               (:env parked)\n336:                                               (:k parked))))\n337:                        control (:control vm')]\n338:                    (recur (:segment control) (:pc control) val\n339:                           (or (:stack vm') []) (:env vm') (or (:k vm') [])\n340:                           vm' (get (:code vm') (:segment control))\n341:                           (and fuel (dec fuel))))\n342:               ;; :call — apply f to argc popped arguments (5, :call)\n343:               5 (let [argc (nth inst 1)\n344:                       total (count St)\n345:                       f-pos (- total argc 1)\n346:                       r (apply-call code seg pc val\n347:                                     (subvec St 0 f-pos) E K vm\n348:                                     false (nth St f-pos)\n349:                                     (subvec St (inc f-pos) total))]\n350:                   (if-let [[seg' pc' val' St' E' K' vm' image'] (:goto r)]\n351:                     (recur seg' pc' val' St' E' K' vm' image'\n352:                            (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n353:                     (:stop r)))\n354:               ;; :call with :yin.code/tail? — same, frame-free (20, :tailcall)\n355:               20 (let [argc (nth inst 1)\n356:                        total (count St)\n357:                        f-pos (- total argc 1)\n358:                        r (apply-call code seg pc val\n359:                                      (subvec St 0 f-pos) E K vm\n360:                                      true (nth St f-pos)\n361:                                      (subvec St (inc f-pos) total))]\n362:                    (if-let [[seg' pc' val' St' E' K' vm' image'] (:goto r)]\n363:                      (recur seg' pc' val' St' E' K' vm' image'\n364:                             (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n365:                      (:stop r)))\n366:               ;; :stream-make — effect :stream/make (12)\n367:               12 (let [r (run-effect vm seg pc val St E K\n368:                                      {:effect :stream/make,\n369:                                       :capacity (or (nth inst 1)\n370:                                                     vm/default-stream-capacity)}\n371:                                      nil)]\n372:                    (if-let [[vm' val'] (:continue r)]\n373:                      (recur seg (inc pc) val' St E K vm' image\n374:                             (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n375:                      (:stop r)))\n376:               ;; :stream-put — target popped from St, value in val (13)\n377:               13 (let [St' (pop St)\n378:                        r (run-effect vm seg pc val St' E K\n379:                                      {:effect :stream/put,"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"a3f08825-c1e6-46e9-98fa-34fe639f432e"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed the architecture of the named VM: `val` accumulator; `:push` commits val onto St; producers set val; `:call` leaves the result in val (apply-call passes `val` → result); `:return` uses val; `:halt` uses val.\n\nThe stack VM inverts this: producers conj onto the stack; `:branch-false` peeks/pops; `:return` peeks; `:halt` — value = peek of the stack.\n\nNow, let me carefully verify the correctness of the stack VM's `:halt`. Named `:halt`: halts with val as the result, and does put-registers vm nil nil val [] E nil. Stack `:halt`: sets :status :halted, leaves the stack as-is. `value` = peek (:stack). Consider a lowered root program: `<producer sequence> :halt`. The producer leaves its result on the top of the stack. But wait — what about a root that ends with a `:call` (non-tail) in an empty continuation? `:call` conj's the result onto the stack, then `:halt`, peek = result. Good.\n\nWhat about a root that ends in tail position with `:return` in an empty continuation? Stack `:return` with empty continuation: `(assoc vm :status :halted, :stack [val])` where val = peek stack. Good.\n\nBut here's a real candidate difference: `:halt` in the stack VM does NOT truncate the stack. If `:halt` is reached when the stack is [...garbage... result], peek is still the result. In the named flow, root's lowering: does linearize emit `:halt` after the root node's code? Probably yes. The root node produces val; the stack might have leftovers… Actually, in the stack VM, can leftovers appear beneath the final value at the point of `:halt`? `:push` is a no-op, and `:call` consumes everything it pushed… Root application: lower the operator (conj f), :push (no-op), lower the operands (conj each), :call argc → pop f+args, conj the result. Clean. An `:if` branch leaves one value. Therefore at the point of :halt, the stack has exactly the root's result… But wait, what about a root-level `:stream/put`? It lowers the target, :push (no-op), lowers the value, :stream-put — B4 consumes 2. No problem.\n\nHmm, but actually the no-op `:push` has a subtle issue with `:stream/put`: named: the target's producer sets val, `:push` commits the target onto St, then the value's producer sets val (target is still on St), `:stream-put` pops the target from St and uses val. In the stack VM's B4 (future): target is conj'd, push is no-op, value is conj'd, stream-put pops the value then the target. Equivalent. Good.\n\nNext, checking `:resume` in the named VM: `resume-continuation ... val` — restore the parked continuation with val as the resume value. The resumed program continues at the parked pc+… In the stack VM, B4 must conj the resume value. No problem, future work.\n\nNow — the real question: are there any places where the named lowering emits `:push` and the value in val was NOT produced by the immediately preceding instruction, i.e., where val is stale? Looking at `:stream/put` — the val-node is lowered between the :push and :stream-put, so val is fresh. In application, each :push comes right after the lowering of a node, and each node's lowering ends in a producer (literal→:const, variable→:var, lambda→:closure, application→:call, if→branch merge where both arms end in producers, stream ops end in :stream-make/:stream-put/:stream-cursor/:stream-next, etc.). So when the :push executes, val is always fresh.\n\nIn the stack VM, correspondingly, each node's lowering ends with an instruction that conj'd a value onto the stack. Therefore a no-op :push is correct for every output site. One exception to check: does `:if` lowering end in a producer in both arms? Arms are `lower-node(consequent)` … jump end; mark else; lower-node(alternate); mark end. Each arm ends in the arm node's producer. And linearize's `mark!`/label assignment — the named VM's :branch-false reads val (the test's value, freshly produced by the test node), and the stack VM's :branch-false pops the test from the stack. Consistent.\n\nSo the :push no-op is sound with respect to all current output sites, and the comment's reasoning is also verified: the named VM has a split between val and St (semantic.cljc:275-277), and the stack VM's producers conj directly.\n\nAlso the requirement: \"Verify that this fix doesn't silently mask the case where B2 or future callers emit :push for genuinely different purposes.\" B2 doesn't emit :push — `rewrite` copies tuples other than var/closure unchanged, so :push comes only from linearize/lower. All output sites are \"commit the val that was just produced.\" No other uses. Confirmed.\n\nBut — one thing to double-check: `code/mnemonics` and B1's opcode-table carry over `:push` with zero operands. `debruijn-code`'s `terminators` — code/terminators. What are the terminators? Probably #{:halt :return :jump}. No problem.\n\nNext, checking the correctness of B2 itself. Let me carefully trace `named-canonical-vector`:\n\n```clojure\n(let [[order attrs] (index-batch datoms)\n      seg (first (filter #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) order))\n      structural #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op}\n      instructions (filterv (fn [e]\n                              (and (not= e seg)\n                                   (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %) structural)))\n                            order)\n      ...\n```\n\nHmm — `instructions` excludes `seg` (the segment entity), but includes any entity that has one of the structural attributes. The segment entity itself has :yin.code/type :segment and probably :yin.code/… Wait, do instruction entities have `:yin.code/segment`? Probably yes — each instruction datom has a :yin.code/segment pointing to the segment entity, :yin.code/pc, and :yin.code/op. Does the segment entity itself have :yin.code/pc? Probably not. But the filter is `(not= e seg)` plus having any structural attribute — if the batch has other entities with those attributes (e.g., the root entity?), they'd be included incorrectly. What exactly does lower output? I need to check linearize/lower's output datoms. Let me look at the top of linearize.cljc — what datoms does it output (segment entity + instruction entities with tempid?).\n\nAlso, `index-batch` handles `[e a v]` triples. And by-pc is a pc→entity map; ordered = (mapv by-pc (range n)) — assumes pcs are exactly 0..n-1. Then pc-of = zipmap ordered (range) — a new position equal to the pc. OK.\n\nPotential defect: `tuple` reads operands from `code/vector-operand-table` — for `:closure`, the operands are [:yin.code/params :syms] and [:yin.code/body :pc]. Body's ref: `(get ia a)` — is :yin.code/body stored as an entity id in the datoms? And `(get pc-of (get ia a))` resolves to a position. But what does lower output for :yin.code/body — a tempid/entity ref? In the semantic/load-image precedent, `resolve-ref e (get ia a)` resolves it. OK.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety with the pc-of resolution for `:jump`/`:branch-false` targets: `(get pc-of (get ia a))` where the attr's value is the target instruction's entity id. No problem.\n\nBut what about `:yin.code/target` for if labels: lower emits marks (labels) — those become jump/branch instructions with target entity refs. No problem.\n\nNext, checking `seg`: `(first (filter #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) order))`. Do segment entities have :yin.code/type :segment? Need to check linearize. Let me read the first ~90 lines of linearize.cljc.\n\nAnother concern: `instructions` filter — entities that have :yin.code/pc… But does the SEGMENT entity have :yin.code/pc? No. But the exclusion is only for the first segment entity found. If the batch had multiple segments… lower probably produces one segment per call. OK.\n\nNext, `rewrite`: for `:var`, stack = the parameter vector of the chain of closures enclosing pc, innermost first. `chain-of owner pc`: walks body-owner from pc: owner maps pc→the innermost closure c whose body range covers pc. Then recur c — looks up owner for the closure instruction's own pc c (the position of the `:closure` instruction itself, which is inside the parent body). This is correct per the design: \"its parent is the body containing the `:closure`.\"\n\n`resolve-name` expects a stack with depth 0 = innermost frame. chain-of builds innermost first: chain = [c_inner ... c_outer]? Let me trace: loop p=pc, chain=[]. c1 = owner(pc) (innermost closure covering pc). recur c1, chain=[c1]. c2 = owner(c1) — the closure whose body covers the pc of the `:closure` instruction c1 — the next-outer one. chain=[c1 c2]. So innermost first. stack = mapv (nth (nth v c) 1) over chain = parameter vectors innermost first. resolve-name searches (range (count stack)) from depth 0 = innermost. Correct.\n\nBut a subtlety: owner for the pc of a `:closure` instruction inside a body: body-owner's ranges are [body, r] where r = the first :return at or after body. Closures in the main sequence (outside any body) have no owner → chain terminates. Correct.\n\nEdge case: what about a closure instruction emitted out-of-line? In linearize, the `:closure` instruction is emitted inline in the parent's sequence (emit! then body is appended later). The body is out-of-line. So the parent chain works.\n\nWait — actually there's a subtlety: in linearize, `(emit! e :closure :yin.code/params ... :yin.code/body l)` where l is a fresh label — the body is emitted later via the `bodies` queue. So all bodies are after the main code? The body queue is processed later, and each body itself may contain lambdas which are appended to the queue. So bodies are appended after the main sequence, each ending with… hmm, does each body end with `:return`? Let me check the body-emitting code in linearize. The tail flag: `(emit! e :call argc tail?)` — for a tail call in a body, does the body end with `[:call n true]`, followed by… `:return`? closure-body-ranges assumes that each body's range ends at the first :return at or after the body. If a tail call's body ends without an explicit :return… let me read linearize fully.\n\nAlso `layout-conforms?` requires \"no body covering pc 0\" (b > 0) and disjointness of bodies. If linearize emits bodies interleaved… need to verify.\n\nAlso `closure-body-ranges` computes r = the first :return at or after the body — but what if there's an :if inside a body with a :return in one of its arms? Does the named lowering emit :return explicitly at the end of a body, or do tail positions emit :return? Let me read linearize.\n\nI've confirmed that the named VM has a val/St split (semantic.cljc:275). Next, I need to read all of `linearize.cljc` to verify B2's layout assumptions (bodies are out-of-line, each body closes with `:return`, no early `:return` within a body).\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_de047d5d1d3f48e9a1fa2677","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790067506407,"messageId":"afe3e4ca-1954-4650-b732-8f522aca5ab9"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_de047d5d1d3f48e9a1fa2677","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 447 lines\n1: (ns yin.vm.linearize\n2:   \"Lowering: Universal AST datoms (`:yin/*`) → linear executable datoms\n3:    (`:yin.code/*`), per `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` §5.\n4: \n5:    Lowering is VM lineage and knows no surface syntax. It is a recursive\n6:    descent in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to\n7:    right), emitting the main sequence first and lambda bodies out of line\n8:    after it. Labels are symbolic while emitting and are resolved to\n9:    instruction refs in a second pass: the output carries refs, never pc\n10:    integers.\n11: \n12:    Lowering reads the AST datom index directly rather than reconstructing\n13:    maps, because every instruction names the AST entity it came from in\n14:    `:yin.code/source` and reconstructed maps have no entities.\n15: \n16:    The row lane (`lower-rows`, `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`\n17:    §5.1/§5.3) lowers the projected row set to the canonical positional\n18:    instruction vector of UCF §7.3.2 instead, threading §2.5 structural\n19:    paths for the provenance side table. It shares this flattening order;\n20:    the datom lane stays beside it for media that carry datoms (§7.1's\n21:    projection path), and `rows-loader` composes the row lane for the row\n22:    medium an encoder observer forwards to (`yin.vm.encoder`).\"\n23:   (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n24:             [yin.vm :as vm]))\n25: \n26: \n27: (def unsupported\n28:   \"Node types outside both evaluators' supported corpus (§2.4), rejected at\n29:    lowering with an error naming the node.\"\n30:   #{:yin/macro-expand :vm/store-update})\n31: \n32: \n33: (def ^:private label-attrs\n34:   \"Operand attributes whose emitted value is a label, resolved to the\n35:    instruction entity at that label's pc.\"\n36:   #{:yin.code/target :yin.code/body})\n37: \n38: \n39: (defn- reject-unsupported!\n40:   [type node]\n41:   (when (contains? unsupported type)\n42:     (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot lower unsupported node \" type)\n43:                     {:type type, :node node}))))\n44: \n45: \n46: (def ^:private min-safe-id\n47:   \"Lowest integer exactly representable by every supported host (a JS\n48:    number's safe range). Code tempids stay at or above it so the ids of one\n49:    segment are distinct on CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD alike.\"\n50:   -9007199254740991)\n51: \n52: \n53: (defn- reject-host-value!\n54:   [source attr v]\n55:   (when-not (vm/plain-data? v)\n56:     (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot lower a host value into \" attr)\n57:                     {:node source, :attr attr, :value v}))))\n58: \n59: \n60: (defn- flatten-program\n61:   \"The §5.3 flattening from `root`. Returns `[code labels]`: `code` is a\n62:    vector of `{:op :source :operands [[attr v] ..]}` in pc order, and `labels`\n63:    maps each label to the pc it marks.\"\n64:   [get-attr root]\n65:   (let [code (atom [])\n66:         labels (atom {})\n67:         bodies (atom [])\n68:         label-count (atom 0)\n69:         fresh! #(swap! label-count inc)\n70:         mark! (fn [l] (swap! labels assoc l (count @code)))\n71:         emit! (fn [source op & operands]\n72:                 (let [operands (partition 2 operands)]\n73:                   (doseq [[a v] operands\n74:                           :when (not (contains? label-attrs a))]\n75:                     (reject-host-value! source a v))\n76:                   (swap! code conj\n77:                          {:op op, :source source, :operands operands})))]\n78:     (letfn\n79:       [(lower-node\n80:          [e]\n81:          (let [type (get-attr e :yin/type)]\n82:            (reject-unsupported! type e)\n83:            (case type\n84:              :literal (emit! e :const :yin.code/value (get-attr e :yin/value))\n85:              :variable (emit! e :var :yin.code/name (get-attr e :yin/name))\n86:              :lambda (let [l (fresh!)]\n87:                        (swap! bodies conj [l e (get-attr e :yin/body)])\n88:                        (emit! e :closure\n89:                               :yin.code/params (get-attr e :yin/params)\n90:                               :yin.code/body l))\n91:              :application (let [operands (get-attr e :yin/operands)]\n92:                             (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/operator))\n93:                             (emit! e :push)\n94:                             (doseq [o operands]\n95:                               (lower-node o)\n96:                               (emit! e :push))\n97:                             (emit! e :call\n98:                                    :yin.code/argc (count operands)\n99:                                    :yin.code/tail? (boolean\n100:                                                      (get-attr e :yin/tail?))))\n101:              :if (let [else (fresh!)\n102:                        end (fresh!)]\n103:                    (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/test))\n104:                    (emit! e :branch-false :yin.code/target else)\n105:                    (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/consequent))\n106:                    (emit! e :jump :yin.code/target end)\n107:                    (mark! else)\n108:                    (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/alternate))\n109:                    (mark! end))\n110:              :dao.stream.apply/call (let [operands (get-attr e :yin/operands)]\n111:                                       (doseq [o operands]\n112:                                         (lower-node o)\n113:                                         (emit! e :push))\n114:                                       (emit! e :ffi-call\n115:                                              :yin.code/ffi-op (get-attr e :yin/op)\n116:                                              :yin.code/argc (count operands)))\n117:              :stream/make (emit! e :stream-make\n118:                                  :yin.code/buffer (get-attr e :yin/buffer))\n119:              :stream/put (do (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/target))\n120:                              (emit! e :push)\n121:                              (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/val-node))\n122:                              (emit! e :stream-put))\n123:              :stream/cursor (do (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/source))\n124:                                 (emit! e :stream-cursor))\n125:              :stream/next (do (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/source))\n126:                               (emit! e :stream-next))\n127:              :stream/close (do (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/source))\n128:                                (emit! e :stream-close))\n129:              :vm/gensym (emit! e :gensym :yin.code/prefix (get-attr e :yin/prefix))\n130:              :vm/store-get (emit! e :store-get :yin.code/key (get-attr e :yin/key))\n131:              :vm/store-put (emit! e :store-put\n132:                                   :yin.code/key (get-attr e :yin/key)\n133:                                   :yin.code/value (get-attr e :yin/value))\n134:              :vm/park (emit! e :park)\n135:              :vm/current-continuation (emit! e :current-continuation)\n136:              :vm/resume (do (lower-node (get-attr e :yin/val-node))\n137:                             (emit! e :resume\n138:                                    :yin.code/parked-id (get-attr e :yin/parked-id)))\n139:              (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot lower node of type \" type)\n140:                              {:type type, :node e})))))]\n141:       (lower-node root)\n142:       (emit! root :halt)\n143:       ;; A body may hold lambdas of its own, which append to `bodies` while\n144:       ;; this loop runs.\n145:       (loop [i 0]\n146:         (when-let [[l lambda body] (get @bodies i)]\n147:           (mark! l)\n148:           (lower-node body)\n149:           (emit! lambda :return)\n150:           (recur (inc i))))\n151:       [@code @labels])))\n152: \n153: \n154: (defn lower\n155:   \"Lower one AST program, as `[e a v t m]` datoms, to one code segment.\n156: \n157:    Options:\n158:      :t        transaction id (default 0)\n159:      :id-start the segment tempid; instruction pc n is `id-start - 1 - n`.\n160:                Defaults to below every entity id in the input, so the code\n161:                and its AST can travel in one batch without colliding.\n162: \n163:    Throws naming the node on an unsupported, unknown, or missing node type,\n164:    or on a host value (a function or object) in a data operand or its\n165:    metadata. Throws on a supplied :t that is not an integer, and on an\n166:    :id-start that is not a negative integer, whose range leaves the\n167:    host-safe integers, or whose range collides with an input entity.\"\n168:   ([datoms] (lower datoms {}))\n169:   ([datoms opts]\n170:    (let [datoms (vec datoms)\n171:          {:keys [get-attr root-id error]} (vm/index-datoms datoms)\n172:          _ (when error\n173:              (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower a program with a dangling root\" error)))\n174:          _ (when (nil? root-id)\n175:              (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower a program with no root\" {})))\n176:          [code labels] (flatten-program get-attr root-id)\n177:          input-ids (set (map first datoms))\n178:          ;; A supplied option is validated as given, even when falsey.\n179:          seg (if (contains? opts :id-start)\n180:                (:id-start opts)\n181:                (dec (reduce min (- datom/first-user-id) input-ids)))\n182:          _ (when-not (and (integer? seg) (neg? seg))\n183:              (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower with an :id-start that is not a negative integer\"\n184:                              {:id-start seg})))\n185:          ;; pcs allocate seg-1 .. seg-length, below seg and so all negative.\n186:          ;; They are distinct on every host only while the whole range stays\n187:          ;; within min-safe-id; the sum is exact because both terms are safe.\n188:          _ (when (< seg (+ min-safe-id (count code)))\n189:              (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower with code tempids below the host-safe integer range\"\n190:                              {:id-start seg, :length (count code),\n191:                               :min-safe-id min-safe-id})))\n192:          last-id (- seg (count code))\n193:          collisions (sort (filter #(and (number? %) (<= last-id % seg)) input-ids))\n194:          _ (when (seq collisions)\n195:              (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower with code tempids colliding with input entities\"\n196:                              {:id-start seg, :last-id last-id, :collisions (vec collisions)})))\n197:          pc->eid #(- seg 1 %)\n198:          t (get opts :t 0)\n199:          _ (when-not (integer? t)\n200:              (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower with a :t that is not an integer\" {:t t})))\n201:          m datom/default-op]\n202:      (into [[seg :yin.code/type :segment t m]\n203:             [seg :yin.code/length (count code) t m]\n204:             [seg :yin.code/derived-from root-id t m]]\n205:            (mapcat (fn [pc {:keys [op source operands]}]\n206:                      (let [e (pc->eid pc)]\n207:                        (concat [[e :yin.code/segment seg t m]\n208:                                 [e :yin.code/pc pc t m]\n209:                                 [e :yin.code/op op t m]]\n210:                                (map (fn [[a v]]\n211:                                       [e a\n212:                                        (if (contains? label-attrs a)\n213:                                          (pc->eid (get labels v))\n214:                                          v)\n215:                                        t m])\n216:                                     operands)\n217:                                [[e :yin.code/source source t m]])))\n218:                    (range)\n219:                    code)))))\n220: \n221: \n222: (defn- row-slots\n223:   \"The slot values of row `id` in §2.3 table order: its body minus the tag.\"\n224:   [rows id]\n225:   (subvec (get rows id) 2))\n226: \n227: \n228: (defn- flatten-rows\n229:   \"The §5.3 flattening from `root` over rows, the row-lane twin of\n230:    `flatten-program`: the same recursion in the walker's evaluation order,\n231:    with `lower-node` reading slots by the grammar table's positions instead\n232:    of `get-attr` over an entity index. Returns `[code labels paths]`:\n233:    `code` is a vector of `[mnemonic & operands]` in pc order holding label\n234:    ids where a resolved pc belongs, `labels` maps each label to its pc,\n235:    and `paths` carries the emitting node's §2.5 structural path per pc.\n236: \n237:    The rows must already have passed `validate-rows`; nothing here\n238:    re-checks shape, arity, or slot kinds.\n239: \n240:    Path steps are §2.5 row positions (id 0, tag 1, first slot 2 — the\n241:    base of §2.5's own `[2 [3 0]]` example, which `occurrences`' relation\n242:    also uses and which provenance joins on `[root path]`): a `node` slot\n243:    is its row position, a `nodes` item the pair `[position i]`.\n244:    `validate-rows`' defect paths number the first slot 1; the example is\n245:    normative for occurrence keys.\"\n246:   [rows root]\n247:   (let [code (atom [])\n248:         paths (atom [])\n249:         labels (atom {})\n250:         bodies (atom [])\n251:         label-count (atom 0)\n252:         fresh! #(swap! label-count inc)\n253:         mark! (fn [l] (swap! labels assoc l (count @code)))\n254:         emit! (fn [path tuple]\n255:                 (swap! paths conj path)\n256:                 (swap! code conj tuple))]\n257:     (letfn\n258:       [(lower-node\n259:          [id path]\n260:          (let [tag (nth (get rows id) 1)\n261:                slots (row-slots rows id)]\n262:            (case tag\n263:              :literal (emit! path [:const (nth slots 0)])\n264:              :variable (emit! path [:var (nth slots 0)])\n265:              :lambda (let [l (fresh!)]\n266:                        (swap! bodies conj [l (nth slots 1) path])\n267:                        (emit! path [:closure (nth slots 0) l]))\n268:              :application (let [operands (nth slots 1)]\n269:                             (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\n270:                             (emit! path [:push])\n271:                             (dotimes [j (count operands)]\n272:                               (lower-node (nth operands j) (conj path [3 j]))\n273:                               (emit! path [:push]))\n274:                             (emit! path [:call (count operands) (nth slots 2)]))\n275:              :if (let [else (fresh!)\n276:                        end (fresh!)]\n277:                    (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\n278:                    (emit! path [:branch-false else])\n279:                    (lower-node (nth slots 1) (conj path 3))\n280:                    (emit! path [:jump end])\n281:                    (mark! else)\n282:                    (lower-node (nth slots 2) (conj path 4))\n283:                    (mark! end))\n284:              :dao.stream.apply/call (let [operands (nth slots 1)]\n285:                                       (dotimes [j (count operands)]\n286:                                         (lower-node (nth operands j)\n287:                                                     (conj path [3 j]))\n288:                                         (emit! path [:push]))\n289:                                       (emit! path [:ffi-call (nth slots 0)\n290:                                                    (count operands)]))\n291:              :stream/make (emit! path [:stream-make (nth slots 0)])\n292:              :stream/put (do (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\n293:                              (emit! path [:push])\n294:                              (lower-node (nth slots 1) (conj path 3))\n295:                              (emit! path [:stream-put]))\n296:              :stream/cursor (do (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\n297:                                 (emit! path [:stream-cursor]))\n298:              :stream/next (do (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\n299:                               (emit! path [:stream-next]))\n300:              :stream/close (do (lower-node (nth slots 0) (conj path 2))\n301:                                (emit! path [:stream-close]))\n302:              :vm/gensym (emit! path [:gensym (nth slots 0)])\n303:              :vm/store-get (emit! path [:store-get (nth slots 0)])\n304:              :vm/store-put (emit! path [:store-put (nth slots 0) (nth slots 1)])\n305:              :vm/park (emit! path [:park])\n306:              :vm/current-continuation (emit! path [:current-continuation])\n307:              :vm/resume (do (lower-node (nth slots 1) (conj path 3))\n308:                             (emit! path [:resume (nth slots 0)]))\n309:              (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot lower row of type \" tag)\n310:                              {:type tag, :id id})))))]\n311:       (lower-node root [])\n312:       (emit! [] [:halt])\n313:       ;; A body may hold lambdas of its own, which append to `bodies`\n314:       ;; while this loop runs; a body is lowered at its lambda's path\n315:       ;; continued through the lambda's body slot, and its :return names\n316:       ;; the lambda, as the datom lane's `(emit! lambda :return)` does.\n317:       (loop [i 0]\n318:         (when-let [[l body path] (get @bodies i)]\n319:           (mark! l)\n320:           (lower-node body (conj path 3))\n321:           (emit! path [:return])\n322:           (recur (inc i))))\n323:       [@code @labels @paths])))\n324: \n325: \n326: (defn lower-rows\n327:   \"Lower one projected row set to the canonical instruction vector of UCF\n328:    §7.3.2 (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §5.1): one positional\n329:    tuple per instruction, pc is the index, defaults saturated, refs\n330:    resolved to pcs, no header. `[:call argc tail?]` is kept unfolded for\n331:    the decoder to fold into `:tailcall`, exactly as the datom lane's\n332:    `load-image` does; a lowering that emitted the folded form would hash a\n333:    different vector and break the projection-path equality of U5.\n334: \n335:    `bc` is `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`'s `{:root id, :rows {id row}}`;\n336:    it is validated (§7.4) first and a defect throws naming it. `origin` is\n337:    nil for the legacy bare-row lane or one of §2.5's ruled portable shapes;\n338:    arbitrary opaque values are rejected. Returns `{:vector v, :provenance [[pc origin root\n339:    path] …]}` with one provenance row per pc in pc order, `path` being the\n340:    emitting node's §2.5 structural path (row-position steps — id 0, tag 1,\n341:    first slot 2; a `nodes` item a `[position i]` pair) rooted at the\n342:    tree's root row id, so every provenance path joins `occurrences`'s\n343:    relation on `[root path]` (§5.3).\"\n344:   ([bc] (lower-rows bc nil))\n345:   ([{:keys [root rows] :as bc} origin]\n346:    (when-not (or (nil? origin) (vm/occurrence-origin? origin))\n347:      (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower rows with a malformed occurrence origin\"\n348:                      {:rule :origin, :origin origin})))\n349:    (when-let [{:keys [rule path id]} (vm/validate-rows bc)]\n350:      (throw (ex-info \"Cannot lower rows that fail validation\"\n351:                      (cond-> {:rule rule}\n352:                        path (assoc :path path)\n353:                        id (assoc :id id)))))\n354:    (let [[code labels paths] (flatten-rows rows root)\n355:          resolve (fn [tuple]\n356:                    (case (nth tuple 0)\n357:                      (:jump :branch-false) (assoc tuple 1 (get labels (nth tuple 1)))\n358:                      :closure (assoc tuple 2 (get labels (nth tuple 2)))\n359:                      tuple))]\n360:      {:vector (mapv resolve code),\n361:       :provenance (mapv (fn [pc path] [pc origin root path]) (range) paths)})))\n362: \n363: \n364: (defn lower-envelope\n365:   \"Lower the evaluator root of a provenance-bearing §8.5 batch envelope.\n366:    The envelope's composition-minted token and logical medium identity form\n367:    `[:source medium batch-token j]`; the member index is the selected\n368:    `:yin/root`. Side tables remain beside the lowering result for consumers\n369:    that retain or index them.\"\n370:   [{:yin/keys [source-medium batch-token batch root\n371:                source-positions frontend-metadata entity-occurrences]\n372:     :as envelope}]\n373:   (when-not (and (vector? batch)\n374:                  (integer? root)\n375:                  (<= 0 root)\n376:                  (< root (count batch)))\n377:     (throw (ex-info \"Malformed provenance-bearing row batch\"\n378:                     {:rule :batch-envelope, :envelope envelope})))\n379:   (let [origin (vm/source-origin source-medium batch-token root)]\n380:     (assoc (lower-rows (nth batch root) origin)\n381:            :origin origin\n382:            :source-positions (or source-positions [])\n383:            :frontend-metadata (or frontend-metadata [])\n384:            :entity-occurrences (or entity-occurrences []))))\n385: \n386: \n387: (defn- ast-children\n388:   \"Structural child nodes of an AST map. Literal values and other data\n389:    operands are opaque: a literal map shaped like a node is not a node.\"\n390:   [node]\n391:   (case (:type node)\n392:     :lambda [(:body node)]\n393:     :application (cons (:operator node) (:operands node))\n394:     :dao.stream.apply/call (:operands node)\n395:     :if [(:test node) (:consequent node) (:alternate node)]\n396:     :stream/put [(:target node) (:val node)]\n397:     (:stream/cursor :stream/next :stream/close) [(:source node)]\n398:     :vm/resume [(:val node)]\n399:     nil))\n400: \n401: \n402: (defn lower-ast\n403:   \"`lower` ∘ `yin.vm/ast->datoms`. Unsupported nodes are rejected here\n404:    first, because `ast->datoms` cannot represent them.\"\n405:   ([ast] (lower-ast ast {}))\n406:   ([ast opts]\n407:    (doseq [node (tree-seq map? ast-children ast)]\n408:      (reject-unsupported! (:type node) node))\n409:    (lower (vm/ast->datoms ast (select-keys opts [:t])) opts)))\n410: \n411: \n412: (defn ast-loader\n413:   \"Adapt a code loader `(fn [vm code-datoms])` into one for a program stream\n414:    carrying AST datoms: §3.1's `(comp vm-load-program lower)` for a binary\n415:    loader. The composition makes this choice; no evaluator learns which form\n416:    travels.\n417: \n418:    Successive batches on one medium are independent AST programs whose\n419:    tempids restart, so the default `:id-start` would hand two programs of\n420:    the same size one segment id, which the loader rejects as a conflict. Each\n421:    batch is therefore lowered below the input and below every segment the VM\n422:    already holds under `:code`; closures and continuations naming earlier\n423:    segments keep resolving.\"\n424:   [load-program]\n425:   (fn [vm datoms]\n426:     (let [floor (reduce min\n427:                         (min (- datom/first-user-id) (vm/loaded-code-floor (:code vm)))\n428:                         (map first datoms))]\n429:       (load-program vm (lower datoms {:id-start (dec floor)})))))\n430: \n431: \n432: (defn rows-loader\n433:   \"Adapt a vector loader (`semantic/load-vector`) into the row lane's loader\n434:    for a row medium (§7.1): each observed batch is one tree's canonical row\n435:    set `{:root id, :rows {id row}}`, already projected by whatever producer\n436:    owns the medium — the encoder observer, for the composition that\n437:    attaches the semantic VM behind one (`yin.vm.encoder`). The set is\n438:    validated (§7.4), lowered to the canonical instruction vector (§5.1),\n439:    and loaded; a batch of any other shape fails validation and throws.\n440: \n441:    The composition makes this choice; no evaluator learns which form\n442:    travels.\"\n443:   [load-vector]\n444:   (fn [vm bc]\n445:     (load-vector vm (:vector (if (and (map? bc) (contains? bc :yin/batch))\n446:                                (lower-envelope bc)\n447:                                (lower-rows bc))))))"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"7f2499e3-549a-4d21-b7e0-69faf830a6c3"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key facts from linearize:\n\n1. Bodies are emitted out-of-line after the main sequence (loop after `:halt`). Each body is `mark! l` (label at body start), `lower-node body`, then `(emit! lambda :return)`. So each body ends with an explicit `:return`. Good — the first `:return` at-or-after body in `closure-body-ranges` is the body's own terminating `:return`, but only if there's no earlier `:return` within the body's pc range. Can `:return` appear inside a body before its end? `lower-node` never emits `:return` — only the body-emitting loop does. So within a body, the first `:return` at-or-after body is exactly its terminator… wait, bodies are emitted sequentially: body1 [.. :return] body2 [.. :return]. The first `:return` at-or-after body1's start is body1's own terminator. Because lower-node never emits :return. Correct.\n\nBut wait — what if one closure's body contains a nested lambda? The nested lambda's `:closure` instruction is emitted inline within the outer body; the nested body is appended later at the tail of the queue. So bodies never interleave: body i's range is [start_i, return_i], and body i+1 begins after return_i. Non-overlapping.\n\n2. Main sequence: root code + `:halt`. Bodies come after. So no body covers pc 0. Good.\n\n3. Now — `named-canonical-vector` filter: `instructions` excludes `seg`, includes entities that have one of #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op}. lower's output also includes the AST datoms themselves (input `:yin/*` datoms are part of the batch — wait, `lower` takes named-datoms and emits code datoms; the output is `(into [[seg ...] ...] (mapcat ...))` — does the output include the input AST datoms? Looking at `lower`: the return value is just segment entity + instruction entities. Doesn't re-emit input AST datoms. But B2's `adapt` calls `(linearize/lower named-datoms opts)` and passes only that output into `named-canonical-vector`. So batch = segment entity (attrs: :yin.code/type, :yin.code/length, :yin.code/derived-from) + instruction entities (:yin.code/segment, :yin.code/pc, :yin.code/op, operands, :yin.code/source).\n\nFilter: segment entity is excluded both by `(not= e seg)` and by not having structural attrs — wait, does it? Segment has :yin.code/type, :yin.code/length, :yin.code/derived-from. None in the structural set #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op}. So double-excluded. Instruction entities have all three structural attrs. OK.\n\nHmm — but what about AST entity references: `:yin.code/source` value is the AST entity id (from input datoms). That's an attribute of instruction entities, not its own entity in this batch. Fine — `:source` vector carries those ids.\n\nAlso `:yin.code/derived-from root-id` on segment. Fine.\n\nNow `index-batch`: datoms are `[e a v t m]` — 5 elements. Destructuring with `[e a v]` — takes first 3. Fine.\n\n`seg` detection: `(first (filter #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) order))`. OK.\n\n4. `named-canonical-vector` resolves `:pc`-kind operands through `pc-of` — for `:closure`, operands from vector-operand-table are [[:yin.code/params :syms] [:yin.code/body :pc]]. So tuple = [:closure params body-position]. body's label value in datoms is `(pc->eid (get labels v))` — an entity id; `pc-of` maps entity → position. Position equals original pc since ordered = by-pc over range n. Good.\n\n5. Now `adapt`: calls `code/well-formed-vector?` on named vector, then `rewrite`. rewrite checks `layout-conforms?`.\n\nChecking `layout-conforms?`:\n- ranges: for each :closure c in [c body r].\n- spans = distinct [b r].\n- every span has r non-nil and b > 0.\n- `(= (count spans) (count (distinct (map second spans))))` — r values are distinct. Hmm, this checks each body has a distinct end :return. Two closures sharing the same body would collapse in spans-distinct but then have equal r… if two :closure instructions point at the same body pc, spans distinct collapses to one, and r-distinct count is 1 = spans count 1. So shared bodies pass. Does lower ever emit two closures sharing a body? Each lambda gets a fresh label. But occurrence expansion — \"The image inherits occurrence expansion\" — each occurrence is expanded separately so no sharing. Fine either way.\n- Non-overlap: for every pair x<y (compare on [b r] vectors), `(< r1 b2)` or `(< r2 b1)`.\n\nWait — there's a subtle issue with non-overlap check when a body ends with r and the next body starts at b2 = r+1. Then (< r1 b2) is true. Fine.\n\nCan spans ever nest? Body A [b1 r1] contains a nested closure whose body B [b2 r2] satisfies b1 < b2 <= r2 <= r1? In lower's layout, nested bodies are appended after all outer bodies… actually the bodies queue processes FIFO: a body's nested lambdas get appended to the tail of the queue, so nested bodies come after the outer body's :return. So non-overlap holds.\n\nBut wait — could the outer body's [body, first-:return-at-or-after-body] range extend past the actual :return if there's no :return inside the body? No: each body ends with its own :return (emit! lambda :return). The first :return at-or-after body start is inside the body… unless the body itself contains an earlier :return — impossible since lower-node never emits :return. Correct.\n\nBut — what about `:halt`? Main sequence ends with :halt at some pc, then bodies begin. body-owner maps only body ranges. Closures in main sequence have no owner. Good.\n\n6. Now the important correctness question for `rewrite` on `:var`: `chain-of owner pc` — walks owner from the var's pc. owner maps each pc in [body, r] to the innermost closure c whose range covers it. But what about the `:return` instruction itself at pc r — it's included in range [body, (inc r)) — `(range body (inc r))`. Fine.\n\nBut subtlety: consider a `:closure` instruction located at pc c inside an outer body. owner(c) = outer closure. chain-of for a var at pc p inside an inner body: owner(p) = inner closure c_in; then owner(c_in) — c_in is the pc of the `:closure` instruction inside the outer body → outer closure c_out; then owner(c_out)… etc. Matches design: \"its parent is the body containing the `:closure`\".\n\nEdge case: closure instruction in main sequence: owner(c) absent → chain terminates. Good.\n\n7. `:var` at a pc not covered by any body (main sequence): stack = [] → resolve-name returns {:free name} → [:load-free name]. Correct — a var in main sequence can only be free (no enclosing binder). Matches named semantics (resolved against E).\n\n8. Now `image-scope-defect`:\n- Guard `(when (vector? v) ...)`.\n- For each pc, if tuple is :load-bound with count >= 3: depth/position check:\n  - count? both (integer, non-negative)\n  - chain = chain-of owner pc; if depth >= (count chain) → defect\n  - c = (nth chain depth); ct = (nth v c nil); if ct nil or count < 2 or arity (nth ct 1) not count? or position >= arity → defect.\n\nNote: for :load-bound tuples with count < 3, `image-scope-defect` silently skips (delegated to well-formed-image?'s arity rule). The docstring states \"assumes v is otherwise shape-valid\". OK.\n\nBut — chain-of depends on body-owner which depends on layout-conforms? being true. image-scope-defect doesn't check layout-conforms? first. If handed a non-conforming layout (overlapping bodies), body-owner is ill-defined (into {} keeps last). Docstring doesn't mention this. Hand-built test fixtures: `[[:closure 1 3] [:push] [:return] [:load-bound 5 0] [:return]]` — closure body at 3; ranges: [3, first :return at-or-after 3] = [3,4]. spans: b=3>0, r=4. Non-overlap trivial. Conforms. pc 3 body-owner → {3 c0, 4 c0}. chain-of(owner 3) = [0] (owner(3)=0; owner(0)? pc 0 is not in [3,4] → chain [0]). depth 5 >= 1 → defect {:rule :scope :pc 3}. Matches test expectation.\n\nSecond fixture: `[[:closure 1 2] [:return] [:load-bound 0 3] [:return]]` — closure body 2, range [2,3]. load-bound at pc 2: chain [0], depth 0 < 1 OK; ct = [:closure 1 2], arity 1; position 3 >= 1 → defect pc 2. Matches.\n\nHmm wait — in first fixture, `layout-conforms?`: body 3, r=4. Also pc 1 is `:push`, pc 2 is `:return`. Only closure at pc 0. spans [[3 4]]. pos? 3 OK. Fine.\n\nBut subtlety: the `:return` at pc 2 — irrelevant.\n\nNow — does `image-scope-defect` agree with B1's `scope-defect` on all inputs? Not required to be identical, but tests assert both reject hand-built fixtures with same {:rule :scope :pc p}. B1's scope-defect walks reachability. For fixture 1: B1: all-body-chains: walk from 0: pc0 :closure → chain '() owner{0 '()}, nested [[3 '(1)]]. Successors of pc0 = [1]. pc1 :push owner{1 '()}. pc2 :return terminator. Then queue [3 '(1)]: walk-body from 3: pc3 :load-bound owner{3 '(1)}, pc4 :return. Then scope-defect: :load-bound at pc3: chain '(1), depth 5 >= 1 → {:rule :scope :pc 3}. Matches.\n\nFixture 2: `[[:closure 1 2] [:return] [:load-bound 0 3] [:return]]`: B1 walk from 0: closure body 2 chain (1); pc1 :return terminator; body 2: pc2 :load-bound, pc3 :return. Load-bound at pc 2: depth 0 < 1, position 3 >= arity 1 → scope defect pc 2. Matches.\n\n9. Now deeper question about `image-scope-defect`: it uses chain-of/body-owner over the whole vector, but for a `:load-bound` in a body whose closure chain differs from the reachability-based chain (B1) — e.g., :jump across bodies. B1 handles conflicts; B2's is \"B2's own copy\" — the design says \"B2 validates non-negative depth and position against the frame arity vector\". Both validators are required to accept, so B2's simpler approach is acceptable. Note though: B2's image-scope-defect is NOT called from `adapt`! Look: adapt does well-formed-vector? check, then rewrite, then side-table merge. Never calls image-scope-defect on its own output, and never calls dc/image-defect either. Tests do: `every-corpus-program-adapts-to-a-valid-image` asserts both validators on the output. The design says \"scope validation is required at both entry points where an image can be accepted: B2 validates…\" and \"every image B2 emits must be accepted by B1's validator\". Should `adapt` itself run image-scope-defect? Hmm — rewrite uses resolve-name which constructs depth/position from the actual chain, so the output is scope-valid by construction. The validation requirement is about entry points where an image is accepted (i.e., where an external image is validated). B2 emitting is by construction. Design doc: \"B2 validates non-negative depth and position against the frame arity vector\" — this is the image-scope-defect function which B2 provides. Whether adapt calls it internally is arguable; the function exists and is tested. Acceptable, probably a P3 note.\n\nActually wait — does rewrite's output always satisfy scope-validity? `:load-bound`'s depth/position comes from resolve-name over the parameter vector chain. Position < (count params). But the de Bruijn image's `:closure` carries arity = (count params). chain-of at the var's pc gives the closure; its arity = count of its params. So position < arity and depth < chain length. Yes, valid by construction.\n\n10. `lift`:\n- `lift-once` for :load-bound: chain = chain-of owner pc; c = (nth chain depth). If depth is out of range (invalid image), nth throws IndexOutOfBounds — lift assumes valid image. Docstring doesn't state this. Minor.\n- `lift-params`: uses recorded params if given and count matches arity; else synthesizes. Synthesized names use `used` = free names ∪ all params assigned so far. `synthesize-name` loops until candidate not in used. Names `g__c_i`. Note: `(into free (mapcat identity) (vals acc))` — accumulates params assigned so far. reduce over closure-pcs (keep-indexed order = pc order), so deterministic. Good.\n\nBut subtle bug candidate: `lift-params` checks `(= (count given) arity)` but not that `given` is a vector of symbols. A foreign side table with right count but garbage names (e.g., numbers) would be used and then `lift` round-trip check catches if rewrite produces a different image — `resolve-name` compares `(= name (nth frame p))` — a numeric \"name\" would resolve identically (var refers to same position), so rewrite would produce same image and lift would return garbage \"names\" in named vector. But that's a foreign table supplying weird data; round-trip check is the design's own acceptance rule (\"the side table supplied is accepted only if its adapted lift returns the original image\"). By-design behavior. OK.\n\nAnother subtlety: round-trip check in `lift` uses `rewrite` which requires layout-conforms? and throws otherwise. If v's layout doesn't conform, lift throws a layout error rather than a lift error. Acceptable.\n\nNow exact-match law: `lift(adapt(lower x), side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`. lift-once reconstructs :var from params recorded in side-table. But wait — for rightmost-wins duplicate parameters: (fn [x x] x) resolves to [0 1]. lift-once reads (nth params 1) = 'x — original spelling. Exact match. Good.\n\nBut what about `:var` slots in the side table? rewrite records `{:kind :var}` for var slots (no name) — and adapt merges `:source`. lift doesn't need them. And named vector reconstruction: lift-once copies every other tuple unchanged. But named vector for :var is [:var name] — one operand. lift-once emits [:var name]. Exact-match law requires equality against named-canonical-vector's output where :var tuple is [:var name]. Yes.\n\nBut — structural detail: :var's side-table entry has :kind :var, and lift ignores. Fine.\n\n11. Now potential issue: `rewrite`'s output is built with `(conj debruijn ...)` starting from `[]` — conj on vector appends; final :debruijn is a vector. lift compares `(= v (:debruijn (rewrite attempt)))` — if v is a vector, vector equality with a vector is fine. adapt returns :image debruijn (vector). Tests use (= image (:debruijn (dl/rewrite lifted))). rewrite returns a vector; lifted is a vector from mapv. OK.\n\n12. `named-canonical-vector` — another check: `instructions` filter uses `(some #(contains? (get attrs e) %) structural)`. What about entities in the batch that have :yin.code/segment but aren't instructions? None. What about AST input entities? Not in lower's output. OK. But wait — in the test's `adapted` and `named-vector`, `dl/adapt (ast-datoms ast)` — ast-datoms = (second (vm/ast->datoms-with-root ast)). lower emits only code datoms. Good.\n\n13. Now `:closure` inside `named-canonical-vector`: vector-operand-table gives :closure [params :syms] [body :pc]. Datoms carry :yin.code/params (vector of syms) and :yin.code/body (resolved entity id → pc-of → position). Good.\n\nWait — potential defect in `tuple` fn: `(get code/vector-operand-table mnem)` — for mnemonic `:push` the entry is [] → tuple is [:push]. For `:resume` it's [[:yin.code/parked-id :kw]] → [:resume parked-id]. OK.\n\n14. `source` vector: `(mapv #(get-in attrs [% :yin.code/source]) ordered)`. adapt merges :source into each side-table entry — but only entries that exist (var and closure slots). `(reduce-kv ... side-table)` on existing entries. Design says side-table indexed by pc with closure entries carrying params and \"every entry carrying :source\". Yes — only :var/:closure entries exist. Matches B2 docstring (\"side table records binder names indexed by instruction slots… :var slots don't carry their own name\").\n\nHmm, design §2 says \"binder names and provenance are carried in a diagnostic side table indexed by instruction slots\". Fine.\n\n15. Now B2 test corpus: claims \"every named mnemonic yin.vm.code/mnemonics declares is reached at least once: :const :var :closure :push :call :return :jump :branch-false :halt :gensym :store-get :store-put :stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close :park :resume :current-continuation :ffi-call\". Let me verify coverage against corpus:\n- :const — literals ✓\n- :var — worked-example ✓\n- :closure ✓\n- :push — application ✓\n- :call ✓\n- :return — body ✓\n- :jump — if-in-tail ✓\n- :branch-false ✓\n- :halt ✓\n- :gensym — :default-gensym {:type :vm/gensym} ✓ and store-and-control with :prefix ✓\n- :store-get/:store-put — store-and-control ✓\n- :stream-make — :streams {:type :stream/make :buffer 4} and :default-buffer ✓\n- :stream-put — :streams ✓\n- :stream-cursor ✓, :stream-next ✓, :stream-close ✓ (nested in :streams)\n- :park — :park-resume ✓ ({:type :vm/park})\n- :resume ✓\n- :current-continuation — store-and-control ✓\n- :ffi-call — :ffi and :ffi-no-args ✓\n\nBut wait — is the test actually asserting mnemonic coverage? `every-corpus-program-adapts-to-a-valid-image` only asserts image-defect nil and side-table map. Nowhere does it assert that the corpus actually reaches every mnemonic! The docstring claims coverage but there's no test that computes the set of mnemonics reached across corpus and compares to code/mnemonics. Hmm — is that a defect? Design's B2 completion criterion: \"all nodes and opcodes\". A test that just claims coverage in docstring without asserting it is weaker — if a corpus program silently failed to emit a mnemonic (e.g., :park — does lowering of {:type :vm/park} require anything?), nothing catches it. This is a legitimate P2: mnemonic coverage is asserted only in prose, not in code. Actually wait — more importantly, would `adapt` succeed on all corpus programs? For instance, :streams AST `{:type :stream/put ...}` — vm/ast->datoms-with-root must support those node shapes. Test presumably passes (orchestrator has run it). Also `:park-resume`: if node with test being false literal, consequent park, alternate resume. OK.\n\nBut further, one actual check: does ast->datoms-with-root accept `{:type :stream/make}` without :buffer (default-buffer)? Presumably yes, since tests pass.\n\nHmm, but I can't run tests. My job: find defects by reading.\n\nCoverage-assertion gap: P3/P2.\n\n16. `corpus-images-load-and-run-on-b3` runs only 5 programs (:literal