:val :node]] ✓ the test uses :parked-id/:val ✓.\n\nLet me briefly read the docstring of ast->datoms-with-root to verify.\n\nActually — also check the `:ffi` node in the test: {:type :dao.stream.apply/call, :op :op/echo, :operands [...]} ✓ matches the grammar [[:op :kw] [:operands :nodes]].\n\nAlso `if-in-tail`: `(app (lam '[n] (if-node ...)) (lit 3))` — application lowering: operator :closure, :push, operand :const 3, :push, :call 1 false. Body: test `(app (v '<) (v 'n) (lit 1))` → load-var <... no wait, named :var '<, :push, :var 'n, :push, :const 1, :push, :call 2 false; :branch-false else; :const :done; :jump end; else: :var '-, :push, :var 'n, :push, :const 1, :push, :call 2 true (tail); end: :return.\n\nbody-owner: body range [body, first :return after body]. The body here contains a branch — the first :return after body is the terminator ✓ (lower-node doesn't emit :return).\n\nHmm wait — important check about `closure-body-ranges` against a body whose tail-call branch is followed by `end` mark, then `:return`. ✓ no problem.\n\nNext — subtle layout check: in `if-in-tail`, the if's :jump targets `end` which is the pc right before :return. All within the body range ✓.\n\nOne more: `chain-of` for a var inside a body: owner(pc)=c; owner(c): c is the pc of the :closure instruction — in the main sequence → no owner → chain=[c] ✓. For nested-closure: outer lam [a a] body contains inner :closure instruction at pc c2; inner body contains var a (depth: inner chain = [c2 c1]? let me trace: var 'a at pc p in inner body. owner(p) = c2 (inner closure). owner(c2) = c1 (outer closure, since c2 is inside outer body range). owner(c1) = nil (main). chain = [c2 c1]. stack = [params(c2) params(c1)] = [['b] ['a 'a]]. resolve 'a → depth 1, position 1 (rightmost-wins) → [:load-bound 1 1] ✓. resolve 'b → [0 0] ✓. At runtime: when the inner body runs, frames = [outer-locals inner-locals]; depth 1 → idx = 2-1-1 = 0 → outer frame, position 1 → second 'a ✓. Matches the named bind-params rightmost-wins ({'a second}) ✓.\n\nAt runtime of the nested-closure program: outer is called with (lit 1): main: :closure 2 b1; :const 1; :push? wait — app without explicit tail: (app (lam ...) (lit 1)) → operator closure, :push, operand const 1, :push, :call 1 false, :halt. Call: f = closure map {:arity 2 :body-pc b1 :frames []}; args [1]; locals = (bind-positional 2 [1]) = [1 nil]; body-frames [[1 nil]]. Inner body: :closure 1 b2 captures frames [[1 nil]]; :return → continuation empty? outer call is non-tail: continuation gets frame {return-pc (pc after call), frames [] (caller's), stack-base}. Inner :return: pop continuation → restore frames [], truncate stack, conj closure value. Then :halt. Doesn't execute in tests (only 5 programs run; nested-closure is not in the execution list — only in adapt/lift tests). No problem.\n\nNext, `duplicate-param` program: (lam '[x x] (tail (v 'x))) — the root is a LAMBDA, not an application! lower: main = [:closure ['x 'x] body] [:halt]; body = [:var x → load-bound 0 1] [:return]. Runs in B3 (included in execution list): :closure conj, :halt → value = peek = closure map, status halted ✓. The test asserts (= :halted (:status result)) ✓.\n\n`worked-example`: app... :call 1 false? Tail flag: the body of `(lam '[x] (tail (app ...)))` is a tail application → [:call 2 true]. Main: closure, :push... wait: (app (lam ...) (lit 10)): operator → :closure; :push; operand :const 10; :push; :call 1 false; :halt. Body: :var + → load-free +; :push; :var x → load-bound 0 0; :push; :const 1; :push; :call 2 true; :return.\n\nTrace the stack VM: pc0 :closure conj clo{arity 1 body 8? frames []}. pc1 :push noop. pc2 :const 10 conj. pc3 :push noop. pc4 :call 1 false: stack = [clo 10]; f-pos = 2-1-1=0; f=clo; args=[10]; stack'=[]; non-tail → continuation [{return-pc 5, frames [], stack-base 0}]; frames = [[10]]; pc = body.\n\nBody: :load-free + → resolve-var free-env {} store {} primitives {...} modules {} → + fn conj. :push noop. :load-bound 0 0 → frame-value [[10]] 0 0 → idx=0 → 10 conj. :push noop. :const 1 conj. :push noop. :call 2 true: stack=[+ 10 1]; argc 2; f-pos=0; f=+; args=[10 1]; stack'=[]; tail? true → continuation unchanged; fn? path: pc+1, conj (apply + [10 1]) = 11. Stack [11]. :return: val=11; frame={return-pc 5 frames [] stack-base 0}; pc 5; frames []; stack = (conj (subvec [11] 0 0) 11) = [11]; continuation []. pc5 = :halt → halted. value = 11 ✓.\n\nWait — pc of :call 2 true inside body: after the call, pc = body-pc of the call instruction +1 = :return ✓.\n\nThe named VM gives (+ 10 1) = 11 ✓.\n\n`zero-arity-call`: (app (lam [] (lit 1))): main: :closure [] body; :push; :call 0 false; :halt. body: :const 1; :return. Stack VM: closure conj; push noop; call 0: f-pos = 1-0-1 = 0; f=clo; args=[]; locals=[]; body-frames=[[]]; continuation frame. Body: const 1 conj; return → restore; halt. value 1 ✓.\n\n`if-in-tail`: runs the branch machinery — :branch-false pops the test ✓. Trace quickly: main: closure(1) push const 3 push call 1 false halt. Body: load-free < ; push; load-bound 0 0; push; const 1; push; call 2 false → (< 3 1) = false conj'd; branch-false else → pop false, jump to else; else: load-free -; push; load-bound 0 0 (3); push; const 1; push; call 2 true → (- 3 1) = 2; return → 2; halt. ✓ named: (< 3 1) false → alternate (- 3 1) = 2 ✓.\n\n`:literal`: main = [:const 42] [:halt] ✓.\n\nGreat — the 5 executable programs are consistent.\n\nNext verify the `:push` fix doesn't break any existing stack_test fixtures: stack_test has no :push in any hand-built segment (grep — I don't see any in what I read; the segments use const/closure/call/load/branch/jump/halt/store/return). ✓ no behavior change against the merged tests.\n\nOne more check about the case placement of `:push`: inside the `case` — `:push (assoc vm :pc (inc pc))`. ✓.\n\nCLJS `case` with keyword test constants — no problem.\n\nNow — potential issue with `step1`'s `(nth segment pc)` when pc is out of range (e.g., fall-through past :halt? no — halted stops). If :jump target is the last+1? target-bounds validation prevents. Kernel assumes a validated image ✓ documented as sole admitter.\n\n`:call` when f is a closure with :arity but stack has fewer entries than argc — nth with negative index throws ✓ loud.\n\nNext let me also check `frame-value`'s bounds check: `(when (or (neg? idx) (>= idx n))` — when idx = n-1-d, (>= idx n) can't happen; harmless.\n\nOK. Next let me also verify the B2 file docstring claims: \"Reproduces the algorithmic shape of the private closure-ranges and layout-conforms? of yin.vm.completion\". Let me spot-check the yin.vm.completion private functions to confirm B2's copies match — the brief doesn't explicitly demand it, but B2's docstring asserts it; the design says \"reproduces the rules of the private closure-ranges and layout-conforms? rather than modifying them\". Let me quickly look at completion.cljc.\n\nThe runtime traces of all five executable corpus programs are consistent. One spot check remains — B2 claims that it is a duplicate of the private `closure-ranges`/`layout-conforms?` in `yin.vm.completion`.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_13e26cffc7a644a981faab38","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"closure-ranges|layout-conforms\\?","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"-C":14}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790068078918,"messageId":"707930af-6059-4947-81e4-158b7762f08e"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_13e26cffc7a644a981faab38","content":[{"type":"text","text":"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc-118-(defn context-of\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc-119-  \"§4 `context(E)`: the set of abstract values of E's bindings. A plain\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc-120-   persistent set — structural equality is context identity. Well-founded:\n/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b2/src/cljc/yin/vm/com